      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Tun Indian smokes the pipe of peace, the Irish. man the piece of pipe. Fish are not weighed in their own scales, simply because fish-scales are not built weigh. TEACHER: "Can any of you tell nee what the Great Plague Was ?" Tommy : " Lessons." CONGENTAT. COMPANIONS.—Wife: "Sir, you're a brute Husband (plouralully) : " Why can't we be congenial then "I scerosa Mrs. Howe's children call her the "Mater" because she has suceeeded in marrying off her six daughters in two years." "James, you have been lighting. I can tell it by the look in your eye." " Yes, but, mother, you should see the look in the other boy's eye." VISITOR (to butler who is showing him through the picture gallery of the old mansion) : "That's a flee portrait. Is it an old master?" Butler : "No; that's the old miasma" Humor]) "Dress, dress, always drees ! I don't believe you have an idea above a five guinea dress." Wife : "Oh, yes, I have. I have ideas of fifty guinea dresses." MAMMA: "Did you have a nice time at the party, Harry?" Harry: "Oh, yes splendid I had five kinds of cake. First I had some sponge cake and chocolate cake, and then I had jelly cake and cream cake, and then I had the stomach-cake." The sun never sets except in the west, but a hen will set any Where. This shows how far more liberal-minded than the sun a hen is. AN INFANT HEsCULES.—" Is the baby strong?" "Well, rather. You know what a tremendous voice he has?" " Yes." "Well, he lifts that five or six times an hour." Docroa ; Well, my dear sir, what seems to be the seat of your disease ?" Patient; "It doesn't seem to have any seat, doctor. It's jumping up and down all the while." MAGISTRATE—" You are accused of not support- ing your wife." Prlsoner—"But, your honour, you don't know my wife. She is insupportable." YOUNG woman (to American naval officer)— "What was the last engagement that you were in, lieutenant ?" Lieutenant ((rankly)—" Oh, With a Chicago girl." Mns. TIMID: "Did you ever find a man under the bed?" Mrs. Bluff': " Yea ; the night we thought there were burglars in the house. I found my husband there." "CAN YOU. let me have £lO for a week or two." "I've only got £9." "All right that'll do, and you can owe me a pound." "You said this coal was economical, why, it won't burn at all." "Well, ma'am, well, what could you have more economical than that?" A MAN will spend enough money warming him- self by grog shop fires to buy out a coal and wood business. ,A VOMAN delights in a speaking acquaintance, especially if she is permitted to do most of the speaking. "Is that cement any good?" asked a prospective purchaser of a peddler, "Any good ;' was the reply, " Why you could mend the break of day with that cement." "PA, you say that heat expands and cold con- tracts; Is that why the days are so long in the summer and so short in the winter?" No answer, but spank, spank, spank. JOESON —" What was the first thing that struck you on entering our model schools this morning ?" Hobson (rnefully—" A pea from a pea-shooter.' "LOOK how fast that man is painting that house" "Yes, he's trying,to get done before his paint gives out." A Scot, being shown Niagara, was asked if he had ever seen aught EO beautiful and strange. He replied, "Wed, for bonny I'll no say ; but, eh, moo, far queer, I ance saw a peacock wi' a wood-n leg at Peebles." A CHEAT MPROVEMENT.—Bobby (at the table): "Ma, chuck me a piece of bread." Mother (shocked) : "Bobby, is that the way to ask for bread?" Bobby (guiltily) : "Chuck me a piece of bread, please." IRATE FATTIER (to young Bloke) : "See, here, young man, didn't I tell you never to enter my gate again?" Young Binks : "Yes, sir, and I didn't; I clum over the fence." "CAN I see Mrs. B.?" asked the reporter. "She is out, sir," said the servant. "One of the family, then?" "All out, sir." " Well, wasn't there a tire here last night? " " Yes ; but that's out, too." IRATE parent in the door, to his clerk, who is caressing his daughter. "Young man, you are not hired for that kind 'of work. "That's so. I'm doing it for nothing." JUDGMENT SUSPENDED. —Laura "What did your husband say about your new hat ?" Mrs. Golightly " Nothing. He hasn't seen the bill yet." Ms. EEEDICK (praising young Mr. Adlet to her daughter) : " lie doesn't smoke, drink, or swear. He's a good boy and would be true to you." Miss Keedick (shaking her head); "116's too good to be true, mamma." As A POI.Or;Y.—" I am extremely sorry I offended you yesterday; you must not be angry with me; you see I always get excited when I hear people talk such rubbish as you did yesterday." EVENING CALLERS.—Miss Smith: "Did any- body call this evening, Bridget?" " Yis, miss : Moike O'Shamus and Tim Blarney." Miss S. ; " What ? I don't know them !" Biddy: "They called on me, mum." "PLEASE, ma'am it was two minutes after nine when you got here. When we're late you always keep us after school," said the smart boy. "Very well," said the teacher, "you can all stay and keep me after scho.ol if you wish." The smart boy subsided. Ax Kurry 130asx.—Boastffal Swell: "I am glad you admire my scarf pin. The stone has a history. It was intimately associated with an ancient Hindoo family." Cynical friend (examin- ing the stone critically) : "One of the windows of his palace, I sappose ?' MUST ,HAVE BEEN AN ACCIDENT. Digsby : "Did you hear about Chappie?" Lightweight: "No ! What's happened to the deah boy?" Digsby : "I understand that he has sustained a compound fracture of the skull." Lightweight: "Gwaelousl How did such a frightful accident happen?" Digsby : "Must have been an accident. I understand that an idea struck him." A LADY whose husband was the champion. snorer of the community in which they resided, confided to a feniale friend the following painful intelli- gence: "My life has not been one of unalloyed delight. I have had the measles, the.chiaken-pox, the cholera, the typhoid fever and inflammatory rheumatisma hut I never knew what real misfor- tune war until I married a burglar alarm." Jos and Brown were talking of a young clergyman, whose preaching they had been to hear. "What do you think of him?" asked Brown. "I think," said Jones, "he did much better two years ago." " Why, he did not preach then," said Brown. "True," said Jones; "that is what I mean.''</p>
        <p>that</p>
        <p>boys.</p>
        <p>A CHOLERIC; old gentleman on arriving late at ehtirch found a stranger occupying his pew. Hastily.snatchbag up a hymn-book he wrote on the margin "This is my pew," and handed it to the stranger. After the next hymn he received it back with the additional words written "Very nice pew how much do you pay for it?" A GOOD story wam told not long ago of a lad lost overboard while mailing in a certain harbour, who was saved by his own presence of mind by floating nntil rescue sonld reach him. But he was a very little boy, and when actually safe began to cry: "You were fright nd, weren't you, dear "No, Indeed," returod he scornfully between his sobs, "but I was afraid the fishes would bite me." -</p>
        <p>".WHAT NEXT? .—Mistress (to new housemaid fresh from the countri): Now see, Mary, this is the way to light the gas. You turn t,bis little tap, so, and then apply the match so. You under. stand! "Yes,.ms!em ; quite, ma'am." Mistress (next morning); Why, what a horrible smell of gas! Where can it come from? We shall all be suffocated." New housemsid (with much pride): Please ma'am, what shall Ido next, ma'am? I've made all the beds, and dusted the room, and turned on all the gases ready for the night, and— AN ADVANTAGE.—EtnpIoyer : You are having a decided Ilirtationwith the girl who has charge of our teleptone wire ! Truthful clerk (with cold chills running up and down his spine, and with visions of instant discharge) Y-e•e-s, air; but, please, sir—Employer—Well, keep it up. She will give more attention to our calls, if you do.</p>
