        <p>DENEEST NELLIE, One of the latest skirts consists of seven or nine straight breadths of soft summer silk, not gored in the least, without trimming, widely hemmed, and hang from thsbelt-on a flimidation atitt-ottaffeta. To dispose of the iciness at the top, three or four pleats are laid deeply in each simian, layer upon_ layer, the outer pleat concealing entirely tuose beneath it, and all the pleats turned towards the back. This arrangement narrows the top of the breadths to the width of goretl breadths, and yet preserves the amplitude below. When made of soft silks, barege or grenadine, the clfeet.ia charm- ing. Thin silks in twilled elf eta are most esel for these full skirts, some having dashes of blue and heliotrope on a white ground, others of pink and moss green on cream colour, the da.snes long enough to cross, giv.ing sabarred or plaid design. A capital black serge worn with a white silk blouse draped with lace, looks particularly pretty, whilst a good effeo i cbtained by a black and white striped linen coat and skirt, and attractive, too, are a brown serge with a heliotrope shirt, a complete costume of white 1)41116, a dark red serge with a white vest spotted ‘vit'. red, and a cream flannel with an accordion pleated vest of yellow stxiped with white insertion</p>
        <p>A pretty over-dress tor a child of one or two years is crocheted with cream linen or coloured crochet thread. It should be worn over a coloured skirt of silk or sateen, and the short sleeves of lace are tied up in double bows, the colour of the skirt. A ribbon belt, or cord and tassels, are tied around the waist. Take a fine steel hook and make a chain the length required for the bodice, or the waist of the dress, and work backward and for- ward in d c (double crochet) in each stitch, taking up the back horizontal loop until you reach the armhole, and for this a few.rows must be worked shorter ; then do not break off the thread, but make a chain for the shoulder strap, and work as before backward and forward for the width, and the end of the strap is then sewn to the back of the bodice. After the shoulder strap is finished the front of the bodice is continued again straight up, and the second half of the bodice is hetod in an opposite direction. In the last row but one at the right edge behind four button-holes are to be made, for which 5 ch is worked instead of 5 d ca.</p>
        <p>crnol</p>
        <p>One of the most inexpensive shirts of the season is made in ftrired entnliria of various culours, and is sold at Is. II!,I. It is made with a yoke and a convenient draw string at the hick, while It is fur- nished in front with very full double frills of the cambric.</p>
        <p>A pretty and convenient little bag for use as a handkerchief case requires five-eighths of A yard of silk and one yard of baby ribbon. Cut a circular piece of cardboard four inches in diameter. This is for the bottom of the bap:. Cover one side of It with cambric. Now cut off a strip 16i inches wide from your silk and sew the two on to the circular piece of cardboard, Wing careful to have the gathers evenly distributed around the card- board. Then cover the seam, thus made, neatly on the inside with a piece of cambric, padding the cardboard with one layer of wadding and putting in sachet powder first. Now with the remainder of your silk a contrasting colour is more desirable; face the upper edge of your bag; and instead of hemming it down, make a place four inches from the top for a draw-string. The yard of ribbon is then run in, completing the bag.</p>
        <p>• Few people know bow to cover their beads at the seaside in a manner at once becoming and sen- sible. Feathers, cowers, And &amp;lb:late millinery materials are speedily reduced to "rags.' Broad. brimmed hate render their weavers' lives a burden, cloth caps and Tam o'Sbenters generally disfigure the wearer; and so, when all have been tried and found wanting, the sailor hat, either plainly bound with a broad coloured ribbon, or smartly trimmed with strung silken bows and hardy poppies, triumphantly holds Its own as the most successful seaside head-y,ear as yet devised.</p>
        <p>Holland gowns for the country and seaside are generally quite plain as to the skirt, and made with the inevitable open coat ; it is therefore quite a treat to find holland made in a novel style, such as a plain skirt with Russian blouse, the blouse trimmed up the side with red and white Russian embroidery, and the band made of the same ma- terial. Another gown of thin yellow drill had a banded bodice set in pleats, and not very full. This a deep collar of string-coloured lace attached to green ribbon, and a high flat waistband of the lace over green ribbon. Yet another pretty gown is made of pretty fancy hopsack with a deep flounce put on with piping and headed and edged with braiding.</p>
        <p>Yours affectionately, JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Wash grained woodwork with cold tea. Wash rusty gilt frames with spirits sf wine. The moment you are instinctively prompted to rub your eyes, that moment stop using them. Bits of camphor laid shout closets where mice are, wont to frequent will draw their visits to a close.. .</p>
        <p>Three things ruin a inan : To kri-ow."--.lit-tle and talk much, to have little and spend much, and to be worth little and .presume much. A lump of fresh charcoal placed in a clothespress will often prevent the unpleasant smell which clothes have when they have long been put away. A piece of chamois skin bound on the edges, shaped to fit the heel and kept in place by a piece of elastic rubber, worn over the stockings, will save much mend- ire.</p>
