      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LADIES' LETT</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETT</p>
        <p>Tal clotn3 fri mg and sl will pernu</p>
        <p>-lAA (ways ta totlis frornthetne while ,- Iting the and snappitig process Li._ and strength , I permit. If allowed to b ne en- tirely dry on the line, there will be wrinkles in the table linen that will be difficult if not impossible to iron .ut. Care must be taken as to how they are hung up in the first place. Do not let them be dragged all out of shape. by hanging from a single clnthespin or being thrown or the clothes post, making a projecting corner that it yak be next to impossible to get out without wetting the.- cloth all over. When they have been thoroughly shaken, join them evenly on a straight,firm line. Take care that the pins are clean,and the line as well. They should never be allowed to whip or flap in a very high wind., Fine linen is often seriously injured; by this. A quiet day with a bright sun is the best time for‘dolng up goods of this sort. Never dry them indoors or by fire if it can be avoided. ; ,</p>
        <p>Al ?peal me</p>
        <p>Cleaning Decanters. The use of shot in cleaning decanters is not adjisable, a deposit ot oxide of lead is lett within, wtEch is . . . . .</p>
        <p>, rioisonou-s, especially if the shot has been used repeat- ehly. If shot is used, the vessel should be washed with abetic acid to remove the lead. Crushed egg shells with water is the quickest and most effective method, and 14ry simple and convenient. Coarse quartz, sand, or saiall pebbles are also safe and cleansing. , Gooseberry Jam. 4To 11b.1 gooseberries ilb sugar. Choose zed hairy ,0 seberries, gather them when quite ripe, and in fine eather. 'Top and tail them, put them hi a preserving On, with the sugar, and let them simmer till the jana becomes firm; stir and skim frequently. It will take about an hour to boil thoroughly; your into pots, and When cold cover.</p>
        <p>Apricot Cream. ICook twelve Apricots with half a pound of sugar. pass the product through a sieve, and let it cool, mixing it with half a glass of white wine and the yokes of eight eggs. Pass the mixture again through the sieve, add- ing sugar according to taste; pour it into a mould and heat it by placing it in a pan filled with boiling water. Serve in small custard cups.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>Custard Sauce.</head>
        <p>Custard Sauce.</p>
        <p>Take half a pint of milk, one tetespoonful of corn flour, one teaspoonful of caster sugar, two eggs and a pinch of nutmeg. Mix the corn flour with a tablespoon- ful of milk, add the sugar, and mix very well. Boil the rest of the milk, pour it on to the cornflour, stirring all the time. Return it to the saucepan, and boil for five minui.es, still stirring. Beat up your eggs pour them in also, stir for two minutes, and serve up in a boat. This is' an excellent sauce for fig pudding. The cost is three- pence.</p>
        <p>The Princess of Wales's Pets. j An attractive article by Mr. Ernest M. Jessop, on the Princess °Males's pets at Sandringham is the main feature of the '• Idler ' for* the current month. Her Royal Highness is a great lover of animals, and her affections have a wide range, ' embracing birds of all descriptions, dogs, horses, and cats., Perhaps the most privileged pet on the estate is " Cockle " : A jolly-looking white cockatoo witha salmon-coloured crest surmounting his quaint and somewhat venerable countenance. liith head sloped to one side, he balances himself his metal perch with the double object of having his portrait taken and listening comfortably to the recital of his history. " Cookie," as is explained to me (the bird emphasizing each sentence by alternately raising and lowering his crest, but maintaining strict silence), has an objection to wearing feathers during the present fine warm weather. In fact, he carefully removes from his person each one as it grows, with the sole exception of those on his head, neck, and tail, which he probably thinks adds dignity to his appearance and enable him better to support his character as the chief Royal pet. He has been in the possession of the Princess for the last fifteen or twenty years, and is exceedingly fond of her, as well he may be, considering his exceptional privileges. He was an inmate of the Princess own dressing-room until the last twelve months and as only been removed on account of the increased power of his lungs. A terrific screech from " Cockie" here convinced me of the wisdom of the change of quar- ters. As soon as the Princess is near enough to his perch when making her morning visit, " Cockie " holds her hand with his big black claw and rubs his head against her with the greatest affection, evidently trying . his utmost to show his gratitude for his many kindnesses. He has now a small sunny room in Brunsdon's house allotted to him for a sleeping c...mpartment, and looks in the best of health and spirits, though his appearance, minus his natural clothing, irresistibly reminds me of the far-famed jackdaw of Rheims, when under the ban of the Church. The Princess of Wales has a special fondness for dogs : —" When in residence it is her Royal Highness's usual practice to visit the kennels every morning, accompanied by her daughters and guests. Her dress covered with a large white apron, she is followed by Brunsdon carrying two baskets tilled with small square pieces of bread ; the dogs, to the number of sixty or seventy, are then let loose in one of the small grass-covered enclosures, and the Princess feeds every dog in its turn. Needless to say, she is occasionally almost overwhelmed with their caresses. The Princess names all her dogs herself, and knows each one by its proper name. Her particular pets are the quaint but handsome smooth bassets presented to her by Mrs. C. C. Ellis, and the Chinese dogs bred here from her old favourite Plumpie„ who always travels with her, and Beatie, the Prince's pet white Pomeranian, who died in January last. Plumpie is known to fanciers as a red Chinese Chow-Chow, and is a grand-looking dog, with a beautiful coat. She has been the Princess's tra- velling companion for many years." In the stables are to be found quite a number of Royal favourites. Special notice must be taken of 'Viva,' the particular pet of the Princess. 'Viva,' a very hand- some bay mare, 15.2 in height, is no less than twenty- one years of age, although time has seemingly passed very lightly over her pretty head. This is in all proba- bility due to the thoughtful care of the Royal mistress, who has ridden her for the last sixteen years. ' Viva' has been a great traveller, having made many Continen- tal journeys; among other notable excursions may be mentioned that to France on the occasion of the wedding of 1111.11. the Dec d' Aumale. She invariably accom- panies the Princess to Ascot, Goodwood, and the other rice-meetings which her Royal Highness graces with her presence." Her Royal Highness takes a very deep in- terest in the stables and their inmates :—" The Princess also takes as much in the stables arid their inmates as her Rosa] Consort. Every horse seems to know and love her. It is her kindly habit to constantly visit each stall, and feed its occupant with her own hand from a basket of carrots or similar dainties carried by an attendant. And a pretty sight it is to see the . long rows of horses turning their heads at the sound of her voice in antici- pation of their accustomed tit-bit. Much do I hear also of the Queen's interest in these model stables during her visits here. One circumstance, for instance, seems al- ways fresh in the attendants' memories. This is that when the Prince lay for so long a time at death's door, a stable-lad, 6trieken by the same dread complaint at the stables, vas visited every day by her Majesty, until, un- fortunately, death put an ond to his sufferings. 11l the midst of her own sad trouble she forgot not the. poor and lowly. Such things dwell long in men's minds."</p>
      </div>
