        <p>„ AO</p>
        <p>PIETRO'S PROCEDURE. The experiences of the night which had witnessed he reunion of the lovers were not allover for Gwen 'hen she, returned to her own rooms. That night was big with events, whose fatal porn was destined to darken other lives besides ier own. But no premonition of fate came to warn ler of approaching doom. No presentiment of iorror stirred her young soul. Danger and terror vere advancing swiftly upon her, and she did not inspect it. She entered her sitting•room, all joy and glad- less, her pulses throbbing, her bright young face lushed with tenderness, a brooding happiness in</p>
        <p>her eyes. Her candles were burning on her writing-table, as she had left them. Her easy-chair was drawn up near the hearth. Her book lay open, just as, she had put it down. An hour ago she had sat in that chair ; but all the world had changed for her in that brief space of time. Her lover tvas not false to her, as she had believed; he loved her more than ever—he had found her. She was to become his wife. very soon! • "Is there in all. the world any one so happy as I?" she thought. "Heaven has been very good to me !"</p>
        <p>She went into her bedroom and knelt for a few</p>
        <p>moments by her bedside. When she returned to the outer room there was a reverent look in the lovely eyes, a new and strange meekness, a deeper tenderness than had possessed them before.</p>
        <p>She sat doWn. at her window, and drawing back her curtains looked across the quadrangle at the ruins and the ball-room with eyes full of yearning. The moon was going beyond a bank of heavy clouds; a great shadow began to enfold the ancient building. Suddenly, as If by magic, the lights streamed from the many windows of the ruins, and the soft strains of music floated out on the night air, and the dance went on merrily in the light of forests of wax candles which had been prepared with a view to their possible requirement in just such an emergency. The time was nearly midnight. Soon after the candles were lighted, supper was served .to the dancers. Gwen watched for sour glimpse of her lover, and was rewarded by -seeing -him once or twice-as he passed the window. The lights were not again extinguished. The moon thenceforward acted the coquette, now ap- pearing with pale and mellow lustre, then again coyly hiding herself behind roving clouds, from the edge of which she peeped to watch the effect of her obscurement.</p>
        <p>Gwen was not in a mood to go to bed. She could not sleep while Lord Chilton was in the house. She resolved to await the departure of the guests and seek the Lady Georgina in her own rooms afterwards.</p>
        <p>"I do not feel as if I could ever sleep again," she thought in her happiness. " Georgina will speak to Ronald, I know. He is the handsomest man present to-night. She will talk of Lim—and I will tell her what he is to me, and that I am to leave her very soon, perhaps to-morrow. Occupied with her pleasant dreams, she did not notice the dark figure of the Maltese valet skulk- ing in the shrubbery near the ruins ; but he was there, watching her keenly, and . revolving in his mind certain plans for his own self-aggrandisement —which plans comprehended her misery. "To-morrow," said Pietro to himself, "my Lord Chilton will come with Miss Norreys and take the girl away. To-morrow the signor will learn that Miss Myner is in truth Miss Gwendoline Winter— the child of Miss Markham ! Cospetto ! To-morrow will be to too late for me. I must work to-night. I must work now. The moon goes often into the shadow. An hour from now she will go behind that great black bank of clouds, and hidden for hours, perhaps—long enough, at auy rate, to answer my purpose l" A little longer for deliberation—a little longer for the perfection in his own mind of his devilish purpose—and then he sneaked across the quad- rangle, taking care not to show himself to the ser- vants, who were still watching the guests still in the ruins, entered the door at which Gwen had come in, and ascended by the private staircase to the great corridor, off which the rooms of the Lady Georgina and her governess opened. He knocked at Gwen's sitting-room door, and her sweet voice bade him enter.</p>
        <p>- He opened the door and went iii.• Gwen arose amazed at his unseasonable visit, and demanded what was wanted. Her first thought was that Lord Dtvrkwood or the Lady Georgina might have sent for her. The fellow remained near the door, cringing, sleek, and stealthy, taking good care to keep out of the range of the window. - "If you please, miss," he said, humbly, "Lord Darkwood sent me to you. I want to get a letter written, and I can't write in English, and his lord- ship tOld.mo this evening to come to you and you would write it for me. .</p>
        <p>"But this is no hour for letter writing," said Gwen, utterly fearless, and not at all distrustful. "Come in the morning, Pietro', and I will write your letter." "I came twice before this evening, miss," lied the Maltese, " butyou were absent." Gwen blushed vividly. "it iS important to me to have the letter written to-night. My lord said that you had writ- ten letters for the maids and would for me. The hour is later than I thought. I will go--but the chance for the letter will not come to me so well</p>
        <p>- . again." Gwen hesitated. She had written letters for the maids. Lord Darkwood might think her disoblig- ing if she refused to write for Pietro. But then the hour ! _</p>
        <p>"I hear a maid in the hall," said Pietro, com- prehending the reasons of her reluctance. "Let me leave the door open—so, miss! Now it is as if we were in the hall. It is but a few lines I want —they will take you but a minute !" The door was open: the Maltese stood just within the threshold, humble and supplicating. "What do you want written!" asked Gwen, wavering, "It is a note to one of the maids," answered Pietro. "You see, miss. I have been .spooning after one of the housemaids, but I meant no harm: I never meant it to come to anything. And this very evening she says to :no that we'll be married before Midsummer, and I was frightened—for I've a wife and seven children in Valetta, miss. I went to my lord, and he told me that I must get a letter to the girl to-night, or he'd discharge me ! He said that I need not tell her all the truth about the wife and children, miss, but I must let her know that no marriage could be thought of. And so if you rause to write the letter to•night," he added, I shall lose my place, and my innocent family will lose their support. I cannot go to Mrs. Dover. She is in the ball-room." _ . _</p>
        <p>He looked espairingly. Gwen wa.s touched by his demeanour. . -</p>
        <p>"Stay where you are," she said, write your letter." • She opened her little portable desk, and took out a sheet of paper. It was white, plain, and thick —the only kind she possessed. She sat down, dip. Ded her pen in ink, and said kindl,y— -" Now, Pietro, you may dictate your letter, and I will write it."</p>
        <p>"and I will</p>
        <p>Pietro was quite ready for this demand. " Write this," he said, dictating sentence by sentence the following epistle, which we will set _down as it appeared when fully committed to paper in Gwen's peculiar and characteristic handwrit- ing : "My DARLING,—When we met and when we parted this evening, I wished that,l might die in my great joy. For, I love you above all others— you are my life, my soul If it might be that I could belong to you and you to me—if our lives could only be united for ever and for ever, I should be supremely blessed. • But in my happi- ness to-night forgot that an obstacle lay between u4—an obstacle that cannot be surindunte4l.—an obstacle that must part us for ever. "I can never he yours, darling, nor can I see your face again. lam not worthy of you. In time you will find some more fitting mate than I. I pray that you will soon forget me. I have said that I cannot sce you again. By the time you receive this letter I shall be lost to you'for ever. It will break my heatt to see you—to listen to your en- treaties—und know that I must for ever be a stranger to you. God bless and keep you. Forget me—that is all I ask or pray for." Pietro dictated this precious letter with his handkerchief at one eye, the other being employed iii a vigilant watch against possible intruders. "What shall I sign it?" asked Gwhn. , "Do not sign it," he exclaimed. "I would not have my name to it, for she will show it. Thanks itiss. I shall never forget your kindness,"</p>
        <p>"URDA Y, At*ST 12, 189,</p>
        <p>CHAPTER LVI. GWEN'S DOOM.</p>
        <p>).11111: r Gwen folded the unsigned letter into an env4 d brought it to him. "You are going away, Pietro ?" she asked. At daybreak, till this thing blows ( ..she will have got cool again," said Pietro. caine over me as you were writing, miss, that I couldn't stay in the castle along of her, if she she should take on bad about her disappointment. I'll gO and give her this letter, and I'll tell the master after the guests are gone, and get a month's leave of absence. Thank you kindly, miss. Good- night." He placed the letter in an inner pocket of his coat, and bowing humbly took his departure. The girl breathed more freely when he had gone. She closed her door, and resumed her place by the window. The dance was over. The guests were crossing the quadrangle on their way to the drawing-room. A little later their carriages rolled away, the Lady Georgina came upstairs, and went into her own apartments, slamming her doors. Gwen's mbod had changed. She decided to defer her interview with Georgina until morning. The lights disappeared from the ruins, the servants re- i tired, silence fell like a mantle upon the castle and its occupants, and still Gwen sat at her window and dreamed her sweet girlish dreams. The moon shone fitfully—now light, now dark- ness covering the scene without. Gwen's candles burned low. One expired in a guttering sound; the other began to flicker. Her fire was gone out. The shadows began to thicken in le corners and draw nearer the slight figure at the darkened win- dow, and her Waking dreams became sleeping ones. The lids drooped over the sweet, happy eyes ; the protul little head drooped slowly to her breast. And now her door opened very softly an inch— another—another. A swarthy, sleek visage ap- peared at the aperture, and a pair of piercing eyes peered in.</p>
        <p>"She le asleep !" said Pietro to himself; in sinis- ter exultation. "The way is elear. The hour has come. Now for it !"</p>
        <p>Ana like a snake he crept into her unconscious presence !</p>
        <p>Gwen (lid not awaken at the entrance of that sinister intruder.' His movements were noiseless, a pair of thick stockings encasing his feet. ,He halted just within the door and contemplated her, his' burning eyes piercing through the shadows beginning to enfold her. Her head did not lift from its drooping. position upon her breast. He could not hear her breathing, but he knew that she was sleeping. - The light which -remained was expiring in a little sputtering pool of grease. Only a faint red ember glowed sullenly amid the coals in the grate. Pietro waited until the sputtering of the light had ceased —until darkness and silence filled the chamber— awl then lie crept forward, a deeper shadow among shadows, an incarnation of evil, bent upon the per- petration of some wicked and awful design. The letter Gwen had written at dictation was still in his peel:et. He drew it forth, stole to her desk, and laid the letter npon it. It was unsigned; but it was unmistakably in her handwriting. No one could controvert that fact. . It was not addressed. Pietro's ingenuity made that of small account, Scarcely daring to breathe, glaring over his shoulder at the sleeper with a cat- like vigilance, he dipped her pen in the ink, and scrawled an address upon it. Then quickly, silently, and dexterously, he upset the ink-bottle upon the envelope, so that a thick pool of black ink lay upon its face, and dripped from it, drop by drop, to the floor. And now he crept towards the young sleeper. It was nearly two o'clock. Gwen, worn with emotions, tired in body and in mind, was sleeping profoundly. His approach was silent as that of death. Hushing his breath, he paused behind her. And now his sinister preparation became more evilly significant. He withdrew a vial and a large handkerchief from his pocket. Unstopping the former, he emptied nearly all its contents on to the latter, and with a swift pouncing movement he stooped forward and pressed the handkerchief to the girl's face. . She started, awakening. Her frightened eyes saw through the gloom the dark face above her. She made an attempt to struggle, but he held her as in a grip of iron, and the drug upon her face was a very giant in its strength and power. Her breath came in wavering gasps, she lost strength and sense and comprehension together, and fell back limp and motionless against his arm. The taint light that streamed in at the window fell upon his swarthy visage, all aflame with exul- tation.</p>
        <p>lle laid her back in her chair, pressed the hand- kerchief to her nostrils, felt her pulse, and watched her breathing for one long, silent, intense minute. " do l" he then said to himself. -"The first step is taken." He glided towards her dressing-room, opened the door and passed within. The curtains were lowered and a candle, very near its end, was burning on the dressing table. A single, long. comprehensive glance put the Maltese in possession of a knciwledge of its occupant's effects and thtir position. " Her box was in a corner—open. Her trave/Png bag was upon a small table. Her wardrobe had its key in the lock.</p>
        <p>Pietro moved silently and took up the hand bag. He opened the box and took out Gwen's few trinket's her pocket-book, and some changes of linen, and deposited them in the bag. Then, with this in possession, he stole to the wardrobe, and thence took her hat, jacket, and a thick, warm shawl.</p>
        <p>He returned with these things to the sittingroom. Gwen still lay as he had left her, limp and motion- less, and a ray of moonlight streaming in at the window revealed that portion of her face which was uncovered as marble white and rigid.. A momentary alarm seized upon him, but he ascertained that her pulse was heating feetily, and he breathed more freely. He removed the drugged handkerchief to his pocket, placed her hat upon her head, and her shawl around her.</p>
        <p>"Now come the worst,he-easid to himself. "I must see if, the coast is clear." Ile crossed the floor and peered oat into the boll. All was silent without, and intense darkness filled the lonely passage. The Maltese left th,. door ajar and returned table victim. She was a slight creature; a little below the medium stature, and pf light weight. He was unusually tall, not stout; but very wiry and strong, and he could carfy her in his arms without diffioulty. He wrapped her jacket over her head and face, attached the hand-hag to his waistband, and gathered her up in his arms as if she bad been a child.</p>
        <p>He carried her into the hall and closed the door of her chamber softly. He crept along the gloomy passage and silently descended the stairs. . The door at which lie had entered had been secured by some careful servant. Be laid the girl down upon a wooden settle and undid the bars and bolts •, but in spite of all his prec,utions the chains would rattle; and the bolts made a harsh grating; noise. He paused often to listen, but no one heard. him. The hour, and the fact that that wing of the building was lonely and isolated, were in his favour. The door opened with a loud creaking sound. Pietro picked up his charge again, and approached the threshold. The moon was disappearing again behind heavy black clouds.. The Maltese waited until the luminary was completely obscured, and a thick darkness covered the scene. Then he passed out at the door, closed it behind him, and stode swiftly across the quadrangle in the direction of the ruins. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ ...... He abated nothing of his caution, although he had little fear of being seen. No private rocms other than those Gwen had occupied overlooked the quadrangle. Ths ruins were, upon one side. Upon the ther was the great banqueting hall, over which Gwen's rooms were situated, and between the two wing?, ancient and modern, were state apartments, library, study, and a large room used as a cabinet for curiosities. There was no danger that he would be seen, except by some watchmen, and from that risk the opportune darkness screened</p>
        <p>He gained the ruins unseen and unheard. He had left the door unlocked in view of this very need, and he pushed it open and entered the grim old precincts with his priy. He fastened the door, and still carrying her, although now her.weight began to embarrass him, he strode along the. stone eorriders through the darkness, knowing every foot of the way, making for the interior of the ruins.</p>
        <p>Ile had the keys in his possession, and did not pause in his march until he made the descent of the old stone staircase, an.-vas safe in the vaults below.</p>
        <p>Then at last he halted. Ve laid his captive down upon the damp stone floor, wiped Lis face with his sleeve, and groped about for a laakrn he had placed there shortly before. Ho found it, and struck a diatch, lighting his laptern. _ _</p>
        <p>Then, removing the wrapping from Gwen's head and face, he turned the light of the lantern upon WC.</p>
        <p>fihe was still marble pale, her eyes were closed ; but already signs of returning consciousness were appa,rent in the quivering muscles. • Gathering her up again in his arms, and taking the lantern also, Pietro hurried through the long, dark passages, his footfalls echoing, his light streaming out in advance of him, his face in shadow.</p>
        <p>The way seemed interminable. 1 The dungeons had formerly been left open to the ' inspection of curious visitors, and all of Lord Dark- wood's precautions had not been able to keep out visitors still, as had been proven by the experiences of the previous day. The lady of Beechmont and• her vests might desire to renew their explorations of the Castle dungeons, or others might seek to visit them. It was Pietro's design, therefore, to take Gwen to one of the farthermost dungeons, quite out of the view of possible visitors. He threaded the labyrinth of passages and cells like one well used to them. Ile passed the spot at which Miss Norreys had halted when hearing the ghostly cry, and tramped on through a corridor which opened beyond, and halted before the door of a dungeon beneath the far tower. Taking from his pocket a small chain, to which was attached two massive keys, he unlocked the door and passed in. - The light of his lantern illuminated dimly a grim and terrible dungeon. The robky walls were I dripping with damp. The rocky floor was wet also. Far beneath the surface of the ground, no , 1 light penetrated this cell and the air within it was damp, and unwholesome. , A slight effort had been made by Pietro to render the place habitable. A heap of straw had been tossed in a corner, and upon it was thrown a ' pile of blankets. A bench had upon it a loaf of ' bread and a jug of water. There was a chair also, and a few toilet appurtenances. But these few preparations—and these were all—only served to render the place more desolate in its appearance than before. Pietro put down his lantern upon the bench, and deposited the girl in the chair. He poured water in his hand from the jug and sprinkled her face. She was beginning to revive, and his proceeding hastened her recovery. Pietro seized his lantern and retreated to the door, where he paused. Gwen's features quivered, she stirred slightly, and then she came to herself with a long, sobbing sigh, and opened her eyes. For a moment she started in a weak surprise, as if she thought herself still dreaming. She turned her liead feebly, and her ,aze took in the rim surroundings, and, last of all, the evil counten- ance of Pietro, as it was revealed in the glare of his lantern.</p>
        <p>And now her look flamed into a wild incre- dulity. After one moment's horror-stricken si- lence, she leaped to her feet, and passed her hands over her eyes twice—thrice—and uttered a strange cry that echoed through the vault and the corridor beyond. _ _</p>
        <p>" What does this mean ?" she whispered, seem- ing to fear that she had gone mad. "Am awake ? Oh, heaven, have I lost my senses ?" Pietro smiled darkly. The girl's gaze became fixed upon him. "Pietro ! ' she whispered. "Yes, miss," he answered, his sleek voice only too horribly real. " Pietro !" she repeated, her tones growing clear and shrill. "Where am I?"</p>
        <p>"In one of the dungeons of the ruins, miss," answered the Maltese, respectfully. "No harm is meant you I shall treat you with the greatest respect. I brought you here from your own rooms in the new castle. You were asleep by your window." "I remember I"</p>
        <p>"And I crept into your room, and-put a little chloroform to your nose, and brought you here without awakening you. Here are some clothes for you," and he unfastened her hand-bag from his waist and deposited it upon the floor. "Your money and your trinkets are there too." The girl struggled with her terror and bewilder. men t.</p>
        <p>"Why have you brought me here?" she de- manded. " Why?" echoed the Maltese. "Because-I love you "Gwen moved backward in horror of. him. Could he be mad?</p>
        <p>"It is true that I have seen little of you, mss," said Pietro; "but I was attracted by your beauty the first time I saw you. I love you, and I would make you happy, if once you made up your mind to be contented with me." "Let me go !" commanded Gwen terrified .be- yond expression "Stand aside, and let me go r "Never !" he answered. "You shall never go free again, except you give me first your oath to keep this night's affair secret, and to be my wife !"</p>
        <p>"Your wife ! Yours I Why, you told me that you are already married !"</p>
        <p>"That was a little fiction to persuade you to write that letter," said Pietro, with the exultant smile of a demon. " That letter, which will be found in the morning, and which, although unsigned, is in your handwriting, will be supposed to be your own letter !"</p>
        <p>Gwen was appalled. She could not remember ease word of that letter she had written, but she knew it might well pass as her own,, if it should fall into Lord Chilton's hands.</p>
        <p>" I am not friendless," she cried. shall be sought to-morrow and I shall be found ! Let me go, Pietro, and you shall not be punished for what you have done! But detain me here and yea will suffer !" •</p>
        <p>"I do not fear. Let your friends search they cannot find you. I know that you loved Lord Chilton. I witnessed you meeting with him to- night. But Lord Chilton, powerful as he is, can never find you. Your letter will be given him to- morrow. lie will think that you have left Dun- holm Castle, and vv ill never dream of searching for you here. Who could dream of the actual circum- stances under which the letter was written ? No, miss, you are lost I Lost to the world, lost to Lord Chilton, lost to yourself, unless you allow me to BiI.VC you!" cried Pietro, impressively. "But I will pay you. You. shall have all my mong."</p>
        <p>"1 is you I want—you! And you I mean to possess ! Miss, you might as well appeal to stone as to me. It is best for you to know the truth at *nee. I know your real name and identity. Yott are not Miss Myner, not the relative of the respec- table curate. You are really and truly Miss Gwen- doline Winter, the daughter of a pauper outcast, and by no means my social equal !" Gwen uttured a passionate exclamation ; he paid no heed to it, continuing steadily— "l have been searching for months for you, when all the while you were under the same roof with me, and under my eyes daily. I know all about you, Miss Winter—the secret of your paren- tage—eyerytking." "You know ?"</p>
        <p>" who that woman was who lies buried in Penistone Churchyard. I know who your father was! These secrets I shall reveal to you -when you become my wife—not before. I love you, miss. I will be a good husband fb you. You may as well make a virtue a necessity, If I will take you back to your own rooms vill you first swear to , marry me .' Gwen looked her scorn and contempt of him—she did not speak. He repeated the question. "I will not IP she answered. "Not to save yourself a lingering imprisonment here—perhaps death t" "No !—a thousand times death rather than a marriage with you she cried scornfully. "Not to learn the secret of your parentage r "No,, not for any inducement you can offer I I can scarcely answer you seriously. Marry you ! Yon must be mad !" and .wen regarded him with a haughty scorn that stung him to the goal. • It is said that solitude brings the hardest man to his marrowb4es," observed' Pietro grimly. "I will visit you to-morrow night awl hear what you have to say then. On the one hand I oiler you freedom, a knowledge of your parentage, wealth, and my hand in marriage. On the other hand is this damp dungeon for ever and ever till you die'! Your proud spirit will break down, my lady. Better give in before you become an idiot, or insane, or a helpless invalid for life. 1:14 choice is in your power now ; the sooner you decide to accept, the better, for you will so decide in the end. If you hold out until you lose your reason, I'll ma ry you then in- spite of yourself."</p>
        <p>" Wilruo entreaty, no bribe move you?" "Nothing but your oath to marry tne In ancient days strong men have perished in this dungeon. How long will a delicate girl like you hold out against a will like mine? You must die or marry me ! This is fixed as fate. I will come to-morrow night for your decision. Let us hope that it will be favourable to me, and so favourable to yourself." • Owen made a last wild appeal to him; but she . might as well have appealed to the hard rock 'en- ' closing her. •H l...o,vcci with a_ trium:hant lecr,iand slwl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>, askt.... 1 over and "It all 1</head>
        <p>, askt.... 1 over and "It all 1</p>
        <p>backed out of the cell door:-</p>
        <p>As Gwen flew towards it, wild with horror and terror, it slammed in her face, the key grated the lock, and she was alone. Alone in the awful solitude, in the black darkness Alone in her desolation and her anguish—alone in her helpless- ness and misery! She fell on her kfiees, crying aloud to Heaven to have pity on her.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>ROBBERY WITH VIOLENCE AT ARPLEY</head>
        <p>ROBBERY WITH VIOLENCE AT ARPLEY</p>
        <p>FIELDS. - A DANGEROUS ROAD FOR PEDESTRIANS. At the Warrington Borough Court, on Monday before Messrs. F. Monks (chairman), T. Birchall, W. Hayward, and S. Wright, James Taylor was . chafged that on the 3rd inst., in Arpley fields, he .1 assaulted and robbed Alfred Wilson, stealing from " him two pairs of boots, one silk handkerchief, a pen- knife,and £2 in gold.—From the Chief Constable's state inent, it appeared that Wilson, who is a navvy, living at , Acton Grange, was going home from Warrington on S iturday night, going past Arpley railway bridge. When he had got to the second stile of the footpath hi I the cricket field, he met four men, who separated, two at each tide of the path to let him go through. Just as he was passing the men, one of them struck him down. Wilson cried out for help, but one of the, men put his hand upon his mouth and held him doOn, whila theY rifled his pockets, and took everything they could find, including the silk handkerchief, £2 in gold, and some loose silver, anew pair of boots,whicti he had purchased in Warrington that night, and a penknifa. They also took the shoes he was wearing off his feet, and then bolted. Wilson was obliged to walk home In his stocking 'r feet. He came to Warrington the next morning, and made known his loss to the police. Inspector Bowen, in going round to make inquiries found the silk handker- , chiet at Mrs. Leslie's second-hand shop. Mrs. Leslie' stated that sh4ll had bought it that (Sunday) morning , from John Jones, the deputy of Mr. Bruichemite'slodg- ing-house. The matter was now placed in Detective Monaghan's hands,and he went to Bruichemito's lodging - house to see Jones. Jones told him that he had sold it on the request of the prisoner, who was the bed-maker in the lodging-house. Monaghan upon this information brought Jones and the prisoner to the Police Office, and , and from what passed there, prisoner was detained. Monaghan then went in company with Jones to pri- soner's lodging room, and in it they found the pair of boots which had been pulled off Wilson's feet, and Wil- son's penknife. Prisoner, when charged with commit- ring robbery with violence. pleaded not guilty.—Alfred ' Wilson corroborated Mr.. Hunt's statement as to the robbery, but said he could not identify any of the men as it was dark at the time( and they kept their faces away from Min.—The Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. H. a. White) upon this said that as there were no witnesses tn ' identity the prisoner as one who was present at the time</p>
        <p>ot the robbery, the charge of robbery with violence could not he sustained. The charge was accordinglY reduced to that of having in his possescion,and knowing them to be stolen,a pair of boots,a silk handkerchief,and a penknife, valued at 55., the property of Alfred Wilson. —John Jones,deputy ot Bruichemite's lodging-house,and a boy named Edward Dooney, and Detective Monaghan ' having given evidence, the prisoner was sent to gaol for three months' hard labour—Mr. F. Monks said that he thought the road to Acton Grange was often waylaid. Mr. Hunt said that was true, and the same night as Wilson was robbed, another man along the river was set upon by some men, hut he managed to get clear away.</p>
        <p>MRS. CALAP GOES TO SEE MRS. MILLS.. And it was not for an hour's chat over a cup of tea that she went to see her, but on a much more serious matter. For Mrs. Mills had been quoted in the newspapersas having said something which might be of importance to Mrs. Calap, and also to others. Now the newspapers print so many things that nobody can make ' head or tail of, that Mrs. Calap thought the only sure way was to go and see Mrs. Mills and ask her if it wao true what was said. What Mrs. Mills told her is con- tained in the annexed statement made about a year . afterwards :</p>
        <p>"I„ JaneCalap, of 3, Vincent-st., York-road, Leeds do solemnly and sincerely declato as follows :</p>
        <p>" In the early part of November, 1887, I fell into a low, weak state. I was tired, languid, and weary, and felt as if something had come over me. All my bones ached and I had so much pain that I did not know where to put myself. I was constantly vomiting, some times a green, bitter fluid came away, at other times frothy water. I had a dull heavy pain at the right side the whites of my eyes were a yellow colour, and my skin wassallow as if I had the jaundice. I had an awful taste in the mouth, my tongue and teeth being covered with slime-so thick that I had to scrape it away. My appetite fell away, and after eating the simplest and lightest food I had so much pain that it nearly killed me. I had always great pain and weight at my chest and through to my back;also a gnawing, sinking sensation at the pit of my stomach. I was greatly troubled with wind which rolled all over me, and give me so much pain it was like spasms, for I could not straighten myself. 1. gradually got weaker and weaker, and felt so weak and exhausted that I could scarcely drag myself along. AS time went on I wasted away until I got as thin as a match,and could barely walk across the floor. I felt se downhearted that I used to say I shall never get better any more in this world. I took all kinds of medicines, but finding myself getting worse I got a recommendation to the Leeds Infirmary, where I was attended to by' several doctors, who gave me medicines, which I took month after month, but I got no better. The doctor, sounded my chest and lungs, and seemed puzzled with my sufferings, for they frequently changed nil medicine. Getting no better I next went to the Dispensary in North-street, and persevered taking their medicines, but it was all to no purpose, I now gage up taking physic, for I had lost all faith in it, and mi sufferings continued until January, 1891, when I heard a neighbour of mine, Mrs. Ann Mills, 40, Bread-street. , having been cured (after the doctors had given her up) by a medicine called Mother Seigel's curative Sp up. 1 went with my daughter to see Mrs. Mills, who told roe that Seigel's Syrup had saved her life, and would do .ine good. I got a bottle of the medicine, and after taking a few doses I felt relief. I continued with the Syrup,. and after taking three bottles all the pain left me, my food agreed with me, and! gradually gained strength. I can now take any kind of food and never feel any distress and am as strong as ever I was. After my recovery a lady customer of mine said to me ' Mrs. Calap, what- ever have you been taking, for you do look so well.' 1 told her, as I tell everyone, that Seigel's Syrup had made me a new woman, and but for it I should not be alive. I wish others to know of the benefit I have derived frorn the medicine, and I give full ;.permission of the Proprie- tors to use this statement as they may think fit, and f make this solemn declaration conscientiouslP na lieving the same to be true. By virtue of the Pre' visions pf the statutory Declaration Act, 1835 IV. c. 62." " Declared before me at Leeds this "25r1, day of January, 1892. (Signed) "(Signed) ALF. COOKE. J.l'. JANE GALA?. "Ex. Mayor pf Leeds." The pubic may remember the account of Mrs. Mills' illness and recovery, published some time ago. We are glad that Mrs. Calap heard of it and went straight to ' that lady herself for the information she wanted. Tte.. visit resulted just as might have been expected. not" our good friends had suffered from the same disease, in- digestion and dyspepsia, and the remedy which cured )0 the first case was equally successful in that of her neigh' bour. No wonder Mrs. Calap had lost all faith in physic' and if Mother Seigel's Syrup were "physic," we shnni.a not look for people to have fai,th in it either. But it IS a remedy, not "physic." It doesn't upset and disgust' it soothes and heals. Men fall ill, to be sure, hut lecoe/1 bear most of the pain in this sad world, and when once acquainted they and "Mother Seigel are 'ever ' the be° of friends," like Joe and Pits in Dickens' story.</p>
