        <p>hop, ',South Australians must smile at the fuss we Rst;:aen making over the little breeze in the a recent Thursday night. Their new ttertihacle Mster, the Hon. C. C. Kingston, Q.C., is bro:VIY under a bond to keep the peace towards a e4,l"ler legislator, the Hon. R. C. Baker, until the then ef this year. Seven months ago Mr. Kingston, gro,,b,ja.Opposition, conceived himself to have been sati Insulted by Mr. Baker, and be demanded (14aetIon in French fashion. The challenger told aPPeared at the appointed spot with pistols be other requisites, but instead of the challenged in Countered the police, who promptly took him ba custody, brought him before the Adelaide en ,aßistrates, and had him bound over for twelve "-Ltils.</p>
        <p>No,l.'llat Charmed circle of which Mr. Balfour is one, calls itself "The Souls," has published a .5 volume of verse called " Iroden," and much y has been rife as to who is the author. One pfh; L°llB been found in the poem "Tan-yr-alt," %Cte C° is the name of Mr. Howard Sturger 8 Welsh litlPersonally, however, I should have very ae hesitation in saying the book is the work of W ittlonY and, and certainly one is led to believe that an„ g them is that of the young lady who was 4,3onced by some papers as engaged to Lord aimsseb,etY, and whom Mr. Mallock in his " Fort- Review " verses, indicated by rhyming her kustian name to Argot. I refer, of course, tst Rot Tennant, daughter of Sir Charles Tennant, has just been made a trustee of the :National a lery,</p>
        <p>61LeallY Tr. yon has notified to the Admiralty that fin nes the pension to which she is entitled as 4drniral's widow. The amount is .£6OO a year. It L ady Tryon's original intention to place the to—rla Fihnt at the disposal of the trustees of the Vic- in Fund, but the sum collected now being already eiii,",cess of that really required she has simply dv e Pns • • tb P ion At one time Lady Tryon wab tleXt • t heiress to the ancient peerage of Willoughby hr tbre6bY, as she was born some years prior to her er, now Lord Ancae,er. At the time of her iCt'lsrlage to Admiral Tryon he was only a lieutenant. tio-"e Navy. It was alove match, and their affec- /late terms lasted all through their lives.</p>
        <p>eheP. .Marquis of Bute's munificence in purchasing pjrB nor, of St. Andrews with a view to its con- Into a Roman Catholic college in connec- a paiLit.h the University of St. Andrews, is only on No With many other acts of thoughtful benefac- renn for which his lordship's co-religionists have oerl to be grateful. The Marquis is, however, not Y a deeactOr, but a profound scholar and a hili).thinker. As Mayor of Cardiff be performed ivllle,rvie duties with a conscientious devotion that long be remembered.</p>
        <p>I:01111 ,I,le keeps somewhat out of society it is quite as theetu,from personal disinclination to take part in sota'll,volities of those about him as because, as has Ithieunies been asserted, of his kinship with the ortunate Lady Flora Hastings, against whom, "nen a Maid of honour at Court half a century ago, P 1 'Y unfounded a charge was brought. Lady ,ittll.B early death,largely accelerated by the scan- fairl the untruthful accusations levelled at her ope,;.'inie, was one of the first shadows across the "Illg years of the Queen's reign.</p>
        <p>Ivalit lea little difficult to see how th 4 Prince of fitil'eB can exactly escape the responsibility which Curt uPon him in respect to the existence of the On his South London property. As those th°,llugh, tin contact with H.R.H. know, the Prince. it 'if gn in the eyes of the outside world given up to oft? of distraction, tempered by a fair share of .tioc duties, takes great pride in a most conscien- te `Oil attention every morning to the purely basi- -444,88:10nian of his existence. He never fails to re " the periodical meetings of the Council which Iloßrlilates the affairs of the Duchy of Cornwall on a shithti°4 of the propertg belonging to which the —4 in question exist.</p>
        <p>_- Th 'D ' e tinCe, too, as those about him know very tra:neElossesses an ability which hf: has cleverly ti th c,....1a mastering matters of detail, and he can is vo i-m off a long file of accounts with a smartness ill i'l many a City Dian might envy. His Royal esta.;"ess looks after the affairs of his Sandringham vhi-e, With a minute devotion to minor matters, the en, it is to be regretted, he has not applied to Ith, istnanagement, of the London portion of his "Y of Cornwall's sources of income.•</p>
        <p>I:l2any of our young women who complain of Warit of work and the lack of variety in em- ens'eYrnr eat for their. sex displayed the tact and plei,,,R,Y of Miss Eleanor Archer, their collie of con:I- N."' WOUId speedily disappear. bliss Archer is the liolcce°llector for Barford, the only woman who kids Boob a post in Britain. The work is not well whoBortiething like -020 per annum, but those • eer; t"rook fresh ground frequently find that pion- nave more honour than pay.</p>
        <p>vilLtV,t. month AfisrArcher went to Durham to stay ti • 4iBs Eliza Maidment, the Royal Agricultural °metv, .ater - 8 demonstrator in dairy work. Becoming tu..• ested in the work, she profited by her oppor- tixty to study the subject thoroughly, and re- hiao"ed to her home with a diploma for practical Se:ledge of dairy work granted by the College of eliow"°_? at Newcastle-on-Tyne. The certificate raark'd that out of a possible 100 she gained 9:8 l'eaultse or was her success without satisfactory 44 tt , • She was at once engaged by a newspaper 'dairy special" for Royal Show week.</p>
        <p>de.atimerican papers to hand this mail tell of the bh;it_,ll On Monday week of Mr. John Stephenson, ftt-c.;_uter of the first New York street car. His large prot,",,rY now employs 500 men, and has supplied Air",!auses and tramcars for most European cities. iititntrephenson was a native of Ireland, born the kit„ Year as Mr. Gladstone, and landed on Unite(i the territory at the early age of two. During hiiliaric.Of 1837 his business failed, but by 1850 Jord Paid all his creditors in full. • One of them, tw, an L. Mott, refused to take thecioney, declar- e " bust" to be quite honest. When Mott</p>
        <p>afterwards ordered a truck, Stephenson built it and endorsed the invoice—" Received payment by the bankruptcy debt. John Stephenson." Mott, not to be outdone in generosity, forthwith covered the truck with bunting and the following words and drove it through the streets :—" This is the way a bankrupt pays his debts. His name is Honest John Stephenson."</p>
        <p>The Queen of the Hellenes, whose daughter, the Duchess of Sparta, has just added a fresh olive- branch to the Greek Royal family, is certainly one of the youngest grandmanimas to be found not only in Royal circles, but perhaps in society generally. The Duchess of Sparta's eldest son Prince George, horn just three years ago, made the Queen of the Hellenes, wko is sister-in-law to that other youth- ful grand-parent, the Princess of Wales, a grand- mother when she was barely 39. Her Majesty was indeed a child of 16 when she married the King of Greece, and she was still a girl of 17 when she bore to her husband the Duke of Sparta, who was him- self only 19 when he married the sister of the Ger- man Kaiser.</p>
        <p>In point of fact, however, the Empress of Austria may be said to have beaten the record even of the Queen of Greece in regard to being a juvenile grand- mother, for she was 36 when her first grandchild was presented to her. But the still surviving Duchess of Montpensier, the mother of the Infanta Eulalie, and the sister of Ex-Queen Isabella of Spain —who was married when 16—enjoys the unique position of having been a great-grandmother at 55, having been married when scarcely 13.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone celebrated now four years ago his golden wedding, but it is just half a century since the occurrence of the little mishap by which the Premier—a fact not perhaps generally known to the outside public—was deprived of the forefinger of his left hand. It was a little before the birth of Mr. Gladstone's eldest daughter,Mrs. Wickham,when out shootnlg with his brother-in-law, the then rec- tor of Hawarden, the Rev. Henry Glynne. that the accident happened. It was, let it be remembered, in the days of the old-fashioned ram-rod. Mr. Gladstone bad fired one of his barrels, and had by some oversight left the hammer of the other barrel at full-cock. When proceeding to re'oad, the butt of his gun being on the ground, a stiff piece of the stubble, it was afterwards surmised, must have pressed the trigger.</p>
        <p>It was only by a miracle, or rather, perhaps, from early training against the danger of standing over a fowling-piece when re-loading, that the charge did not enter the future statesman's head. As it was, he found that the forefinger of his left hand had been very nearly blown away. The operation which was necessitated was far from painless. but conducted by the sufferer's old friend, the late Bir Robert Phillimore, and borne with that calm which the G.O.M.has since proved in many a scene scarcely less trying, the amputation was successfully per- formed.</p>
        <p>In leaving Bridgewater House for Worsley Hall, Manchester, the Earl of Ellesmere finds himself able to personally overlook his magnificent stock- breeding farms, which are his hobby. Of shire horses and shorthorns Lord Ellesmere is a noted owner and judge, but his principle success as a breeder has been with pigs. Pigs black, pink, pie- bald, and brown, long legged, short-backed, straight bristled, curly-haired. Every variety of porker the Earl has brought to a state of porcine perfection. The animals from Worsley are deservedly celebrated throughout England, and are eagerly brought up whenever a chance occurs.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Ellesmere, who is among the richest peers of our time, derives his great wealth from an ancestor, the third Duke of Bridgewater, who, fore- seeing the traffic on inland waterways, devoted his entire fortune, save £4OO a year, which just kept him in the necessaries of life, to perfecting the canals of England.</p>
        <p>His forethought has founded a very wealthy family. Bridgewater House, overlooking St. James's Park. contains a fine picture gallery, and many priceless art treasures. Lord Ellesmere's places, in addition to his London House and Worsley Hall, comprise Manor House, Brackley, Stetch worth Park, Newmarket, and a big new place near the same town, called Egerton House.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>MURDER OF A POLICE•SERGEANT</head>
        <p>THE MURDER OF A POLICE•SERGEANT</p>
        <p>IN ESSEX.</p>
        <p>DETAILED CONFESSION BY JOHN DAVIES:</p>
        <p>Both John and Richard Davies, who are now awaiting their execution in Chelmsford prison for the murder of Police-sergeant Eves, at liazeleigh, Essex, have made a full confession of their crime. This confession will be reduced to writing, and will be properly attested by the prisoners. The statement made by John Davies is that on the night of the 15th April last, a man, whose name he gives, who had been threshing corn at Hazeleigh came to him and promised him half-a-sovereign if he would assist him to steal some of the corn. He con- sented, and his brother, Richard Davies, also promised to make one of the party on the same consideration. The three armed themselves with sacks, sticks, and a spade, and proceeded to the barn. While they were removing three sacks of corn. John Davies and the third man being a long way in front, Police Sergeant Eves suddenly appeared on the scene. He recognised John Davies, and said," Hallo, Jack, is that you? hat's the game ? " Davies, with the view of escaping arrest more than of murdering the policeman, at once struck him a heavy blow with a bludgeon. tollowing this up with several others. The sergeant was knocked down, and while Davies held him to the ground the third man cut his throat. Richard Davies, then came up, and saw what had been done. The three at once proceeded to remove all traces of the crime, and went home to bed. Richard Davies, upon being asked why he did not tell the truth before, says he kept a silent tongue on acccunt his brother John, who begged and prayed him not to , say one word. All the details of the confession have been sent to the Home Office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>BRUNNER, MOM), AND COMPANY,</head>
        <p>BRUNNER, MOM), AND COMPANY,</p>
        <p>LIMITED.</p>
        <p>100 PER CENT. DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening, Messrs. Brunner, Mend, and Co., Limited, chemical manufacturers, Winnington, Northwich. issued to the shareholders the half-yearly report, which shows a balance to the credit of profit and loss account on the working of the half-year ended on the 30th June, 1893, of £251,219 7s. 10d., which, with the amount of £172,754 139. 10d. brought forward from the previous half-rear, makes a total of £423,974 15..8d. The Directors propose to deal with the above balance as follows :—Dividend on the preference capital at 7 per cent, per annum, £15,323 Bs. 9d. ; dividend on the ordinary capital at 100 per cent, per annum, £316,250 ; amount to be written off patents account, £2,500 ; balance to be carried forward, £89,900 12s. 11d. Act- ing upon the special resolution passed in February last, the Directors propose to offer to atlot at par to both ordinary and preference shareholders a number of ordin- ary shares of the value of £lO, of which £2lO per share is to be paid to rank for dividend from Ist July, 1893. There will be issued to the shareholders dividend war- rants for one half of the dividend to be declared at the half-yearly meeting, and, with the consent of the share- holders, the other half will be applied in payment of £2 10s. per share on the new issue.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COUNTY MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>SIR GILBERT GREENALL'S COMMISSION.</p>
        <p>With the county treasurer's annual statement of receipts and expenditure during the past financial year is published the usual roll of county magistrates. From this it appears that in the Hundred of West Derby the present number of county magistrates is 178, a decrease of five as thirteen justices died, and only eight new ones have been appointed. The total number ot Lancashire county magistrates is now 748, against 742 on the roll of 1892. The new appointments rarnber altogether 43,and the deaths were 37. The " father " of the magistracy of the county is Mr. W. Bolden Bolden, of Hyning, Cam. forth, in the Lonsdale Hundred, his commission being dated April 4,1836. Sir Gilbert Greenall, of Walton Hall, Warrington, is at the head af the list for the Hun- dred of West Derby, his Commission dating from the 12th January, 1843.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>HOAXING 30,000 PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>HOAXING 30,000 PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary hoax was perpetrated at Newcastle on Monday night. Bills had been posted throughout the city announcing that as a .special attraction for Bank Holiday an aeronaut would make an ascent from the Town Moor in a hot air balloon and descend from the greatest height on record with a parachute, and that a collection wpuld be made on the ground for the " pro- fessor." About 30,000 people assembled on the Moor to witness the feat, and waited patiently for the "artiste " until darkness set in. There were no signs of the aeronaut, and the hoaxed thousands left the Moor dis- gusted.</p>
        <p>The Medical Annual advises Practitioners to remember that when recommending COCOAS as food and beverage for Invalids, the name CADBURY on any packet of Cocoa is a g urrantee of purity.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
