        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills may be had of all chemists, or direct by post from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company from the above address. The price at which these pills are sold makes a course of treatment comparatively in- expensive as compared with other remedies or medical treatment.—Huddersfield Weekly News, July 15.</p>
        <p>BANK QUAY SOAP WORKS. BOWLING i)LAIN. The Bank quay Soap Works employes played their sixth annual bowling main on Saturday at the "King and Queen," Padgate. The weather was all that could be desired,and the green was in splen- did condition. Some of the games were very ex- citing. The following were the successful com- petitors :—First prize, copper kettle and 145., J. Handles; second do., copper kettle and 125., J. Wilcock; third do., copper kettle and 9a, H. Dagaall ; fourth do., set of castors and Bs., Samuel Welsby ; fifth do. jam dish and 45., T. Dykes; sixth do., cloak and 45., do., Jackson; seventh do.,half-a-dozen knives and forks, and 35., J. Willett; eighth do.,half-a-dozen knives and forks, and 35., James Millington; ninth do., half-a-dozen knives ' and forks,and 2s.,W.Williamson ; tenth do. ,steel and 45., Robert Warburton; eleventh do., cruet and vases and ls.6d.,Josepli Oultram •, twelfth do.,bottle of whisky and is. 6d., T. Welsby ; and twelve prizes of 2s. each to S. Allcock, S. Jones, H. Chalmers, W.Brown, J. Taylor, W. Bandies, W. E. Longmore, Peter Hill, L. Dobson, G. Wilcock, J. Barton and Jervis Woods.—The prizes were presented by Miss Clare of the "King and Queen," who said that it gave her great pleasure in presenting the prizes to the successful competitors. They must all be greatly indebted to Mr. Willett the secretary for getting , up the main and the beautiful prizes.—Refreshments, in the form of tea and ham and beef sandwiches were pro- vided and most heartily enjoyed. After this had been partaken of, the men adjourned to the club room and spent the rest of the evening in i convivial manner. Mr. Peter Hill was elected to the chair and Mr. Willett was made vice chairman.—Mr. Hill expressed his pleasure at being present at the sixth annual bowling competition, and hoped that they would spend a pleasant evening.— Mr. Willett was pleased to see the gentlemanly manner the men had conducted themselves as everything had passed off without a hitch. He hoped they would keep united.—The rest of the evening was spent in songs in which the following took part :—Messrs. S. Clare, Tay- lor, Willett, T. Welsby, and G. Wilcock.—A vote of I thanks to the host and hostess was carried with musical honours. A hearty vote of thanks was also passed to the ladies and gentlemen who had so kindly given the prizes. The meeting broke up at 10.30 p.m. everyone being well satisfied with the afternoon's proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>FOOTBALL CLUB SPORTS.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL CLUB SPORTS.</p>
        <p>Despite the rain which fell in sharp showers dur- ing the morning, there was a large attendance at , the Football Club Sports,on Monday afternoon. The total number of persons who passed on to the Causeway ground is computed at 6.500, a number not much greater than the "gate" last year, de- spite the fact that a splendid programme had been arranged, in which 200 competitors were to take part. There is no doubt that had it not been for, the inclemency of the weather, and the several counter attractions in the way of excursions, a much larger "gate " would have been induced. A noticeable feature in the spectators was the great numerical strength in which the female t ommunity were represented, which may be taken as a testimony of the great interest which ladies are now taking in athletics. Besides the officials of the club there were present many well-known local people, including Mr. Percy Davies (president), Mr. W. Pierpoint, Mr. T. Evans, Dr. White, Dr. Kisby, Mr. and Mrs. Kiernan, and several others. .</p>
        <p>Naturally enough, the track was not in particularly good condition, it was heavy for running, and slippery for the cycling. This did not interfere much with the quality ef the work done, however. Although there were more entries than last year there was rather an absence of pvular local amateurs. H. Webster and J. Massey were both away. The most exciting events were the cycling handicaps, and in these there appeared a rather remarkable local rider, A. T. Daintith. Practi- cally speaking, it was his debut before a real Warrington " gate ' as a racer ; but his remarkable performances Out of town during the season were sufficiently well known to make him " the favourite," even in the first race. This was the three miles handicap for the cham- pionship of the Warrington Cycling Club. Amongst the entries were P. W. Fair and J. Buxton, backed by seve- ral other smart riders. F. Silverton got the lead for some distance' and seemed determined to make a hard fight for it,when he caught the ropes at the railway end and fell. The race now lay between Fair, Buxton, and Daintith. Fair led now until the last lap, but was kept well in hand by Daintith. The latter was riding as fresh as possible. As soon as the last lap was entered upon he made a spurt,and amid thundering cheers from the thousands of spectators, he shot ahead of the pack, and absolutely ran away from Fair and Buxton,and won by a considerable distance. Buxton came in second, and Fair third. Following this came a 100 yards foot race, for playing members of the club. There were 19 entries and five heats. In two of the heats only one man turned out. Fair. Turner, Jolley, Lally, Donoghue, and Teare were left in the final. Turner came away with the first prize easily. There was next an open 100 yards handi- cap, with 36 competitors from all parts of the county. T. H. Dobson, Bowling Football Club; J. Mawdsley, Weaste Harriers ; J. P. Rabbit, Warrington ; E.Flowers, Broughton ; T. R. Allen, Warsington ; and R. Howarth, Darwen,were left for the final. The running was rather erratic and inconsistent with the form shown in the heats. Howarth came in for the first prize, and the local favourite Allen came in third. The open mile bicycle handicap was entered by 28 competitors, and I was run in three heats. The race was exciting from start to finish. El. Brown of Leigh Athletic Club who secured his heat was the favourite for the race, kit there was admitted to be but a slight difference between ' his prospects and those of Daintith and S. Heywood of Weaste, and Woodward of Wolverhampton. Indeed in the second lap these riders were almost abreast and Daintith again displayed such splendid staying powers that many placed their faith in him to do "the trick" , when the spurt came on. Something unfortunate hap- pened for him before this, however. He was riding in the inside and at a corner Heywood who was running him close, swerved slightly and collided with the young favourite, breaking one of the spokes of his pneumatic. After this Wrodward put on the spurt and Brown evi- dently placed too much reliance upon himself, kept quiet till the last lap, when he discovered that Woodward had been allowed to get too far ahead. The latter came in by a good length, Brown following Heywood taking the third place. In the 200 yards handicap, won by A. Sigley, Salford (1) ; J. W. Roberts, Salford, (2) ; 0. T. Horan, Warrington, (3) ; there were several Warrington amateurs who did not show particularly good form, and there was again a noticeable inconsistency in the form displayed in the heats and the final. Nothing was more exciting, or appealed more to the risible faculties than the obstacle race. There were amongst other obstacles, suspended barrels to be leaped through, and tarpauline lashed to the ground to be crawled under. Frequently whilst undergoing the latter test competitors would crawl out at the side, thinking they had reached the ex- treme end. This of course made people smile ; but when the competitors reached the barrels the spectators laughed. Here one man would be found stuck in the cask, and " clawing " desperately for a grip of the grass below to extricate himself; and in another place a competitor could be seen stuck equally fast in the barrel and revolving in the air with it. A certain competitor in a heat revolved so much with the cask that when he succeeded in getting out, at the same end that he got in, he c mmenced to run round the course the way that he had come. But the spectators soon drew his attention to the mistake. He did not get in the final. Great things had been expected of Roelink, the South of Eng- land Champion obstacle racer, but D. Gibson, of War- rington, completely ran away from him, and made a splendid win. There was subsequently a fine two mile bicycle handicap with 23 representative competitors. Daintith won his heat against Buxton and Heinen, but the final was taken by Woodward, Wolverhampton ; Newton, Patricroft ; and H. Brown, of Leigh. S. Hey- wood of Weaste, was doing well in the final when he collided with Carr, of Skelrnersdale, and threw him. It was a pure accident, and Heywood dismounted to accept the fate inflicted upon the unfortunate Carr, who by the way was rather severely shaken. There was an interest- ing high jump competition, with some notable jumpers as entries. D. Walsh, of local high leaping renown took off the first prize easily. The following are the results and times:— THREE MILES BICYCLE HANDICAP (championship of the Warrington Cycling Club).-1, A. T. Daintith ; 2, J. Buxton ;3,P. W. Fair. Time llmins. 25secs. 100 YARDS FLAYING MEMBERS' RACE.—i, Fair Turner, 7 yards ; 2, T. Lally, 7i ; 3, 11. Teare, 10; 4, T. Donoghue. 4 100 YARDS HANDICAP.-1, R. Howarth, Harwell, 91 yards ; 2, T. H. Dobson, Bowling Football Club, 7; 3, T. R. Allen, Warrington, 91. Time. lOsecs. ONE MILE BICYCLE HANDICAP.-1, B. Woodward, Wolverhampton, 150 yards; 2, H. Brown, Leigh Athletic Club, 125 ; 3, James Heywood, Weaste. Time, 2mins. 50secs. 880 YARDS HANDICAP.-I,J. Fair, Warrington. 57yds.; 2, P. O'Connor, Widnes Harriers, 57 ;3, J. Marney, 55. Time, 2mins. 50secs. HIGH JUMP.-1, D. Walsh, Warrington ; 2, J. Upton, Hartford, and H. Guest, Leigh, equal. ONE MILE HANDICAP.-1, H. Barton, Northwich Har- riers, 125 ; 2, R. Thomas, Salford Harriers, 140 ; 3, W. Baylis, Halifax, 130. Time, smins. 20secs. 220 YARDS HANDICAP.-1, A. Sigley, Salford Harriers, 15 yards ; 2, J. W. Roberts, Salford Football Club, 12; 3, Thomas Horan, Warrington, 13. Time, 30secs. OBSTACLE RACE.-1, D. Gibson, Warrington ' • 2, W. C. Roelink, Waltbamstow ; 3, R. Horrocks, Leigh Athletic Club.</p>
        <p>Two MILES Bictcris RACE.-1, B. Woodward, Wol- verhampton, 280 yares • 2, J. Newton, Patricroft. 340 ; 3, H. Brown, Leigh Athletic Club, 230. Tine, 6mins.</p>
        <p>440 Yenns.-1, C. Chadwick, Cheadle, 31 yards ; 2, TL. D. Fairbourne, Halifax Harriers, 27; 3, E. Greenall, Wigan, 33. Time, 49ee. The prim s which were supplied by Mr. R. Bennett, of Legh-st., were distributed by Mr. P. Davies (president of the football club), who considered the festival had been a great success. During the afternoon, selections of music were performed by the Warrington Borough Band. The following are the officials. Clerk of the course, Mr. G. Smith ; starter, Mr. E. Baldwin ; judges, Messrs. Percy Davies, W. Pierpoint, J. Evans, and T. Bowers ; committee, Messrs. W. Hewitt, J. W. Evans, W. Speakman, T. Bowers, W. Antrobus, T. Barnes, jun. George Smith, John Boyle, J. T. Bate, J. Jolley, H. , Teare, D. Gibson, H. Ashton, F. Turner, T. Lally ; handicappers, Messrs. T. R. Sutton, and J. H. Hardwick hon. treasurer, Mr. T. Pemberton ; hon. secretary, Mr. J E. Warren ; referee, Mr. T. R. Sutton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>BANK HOLIDAY. ACCIDENTS.</head>
        <p>BANK HOLIDAY. ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>The boating accidents incidental to the August Bank Holiday appear to have been rather more serious than usual on Monday. A strong band of excursionists from the Rhondda Valley visited Aberavon, and some thirty women and children went out for a sail. There was a heavy swell on at the time, and a large wave struck the side of the boat and caused a panic amongst its occu- pants. They rushed to one side of the boat, and the result was that all were thrown into the sea. A pilot boat which went to the rescue succeeded in picking up about six, but at least twenty were drowned. A boat with three occupants capsized on the Severn. One man was drowned, and another, whose body was found beneath the boat, was only brought to life by the strenuous efforts of his rescuers. An unknown youth was drowned in the Ribble near Preston by the capsizing of his boat. Two volunteers were drowned.</p>
