      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>110LMFI1tTH GIRL'S NARROW ESCAPE. EVIDENCE OF A NURSE.</head>
        <p>110LMFI1tTH GIRL'S NARROW ESCAPE. EVIDENCE OF A NURSE.</p>
        <p>Considerable stir has been ciused in Rolmfirth and neighbourhood of late by the report of a wondertul escape by a girl of seventeen, named Martha Ann Boothroyd. Reports, as we know, have a tendency to exaggerae. We look on " wonders " with suspicion. It is a standing admission that the Age of Miracles is passed. Is it surprising, therefore, that we received the Holmfirth story with incredulity P That was at first our attitude towards it ; but before pronouncing judg- ment we decided to investigate the case, as it seemed to us worth inquiring into ; and we are glad now that we did so, as some curious and important results have I been obtained.</p>
        <p>Bent on sifting the matter to the bottom, a "Weakly News" representative journeyed over specially to Holm- firth. Be found the parents of Martha Boothroyd at home. They are well-known and highly respected people, and live at Pogg log, on the outskirts of Holm-' firth. Gamaliel Boothroyd, the father, is a farmer and yeast dealer. He is a hearty and very intelligent man, blessed with a worthy helpmeet and several fair-haired young children. His home is locally a historical dwel- ling. It was the birthplace of Mr. Joseph Woodhead, a Huddersfield celebrity who, from being an Alderman and Mayor of that thriving town, became a member ef Parliament for the Spen Valley Dkvision of Yorkshire. It is the proud boast of Gamaliel Boothroyd that Mr. Woodhead, like himself, began life as a spinner in a woollen mill. The ex-M.P. is landlord of the Pogg Ing property, and often visits Mr. Boothroyd at the home where be was born and reared,enjoining hirn frequently to "look well after the old place."</p>
        <p>We have said that Mr. BoOthroyd is an intelligent in dividual. He is also a shrewd Yorkshireman, not easily deceived ; and he behaved very courteously to the re- porter, on learning whose business he at once entered upon his daughter's case, Mrs. Boothroyd also being pre- sent. Twenty weeks ago, the reporter was told—in February last—Martha was taken ill with pleurisy and slow fever, and she was at home seventeen weeks. Good medical advice was procured for her,and for three months the doctors attended her regularly. At the end of that time he decided to give her no more medicine. He said she had taken it till it had no effect upon her,and recom- mended quinine and cod liver oil—all he could do for her. The oil made the girl vomit, and she could not take it. Her case was a desperate one. The doctor had given her up. She had won down to a shadow, and was haggard and pale. She was helpless and prostrate. They all thought she would die. But she was induced to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People by the reports of what this remedy had done in other cases, and they saved her life.- Her recovery from that time was un- doubtedly steady and sure, and she gained so much in health and strength that early in June she returned to her work as a dressmaker, not a little to the surprise of all who had known of her deplorable condition. " She brought home her third week's wages last Saturday," said her father.</p>
        <p>"flow came you to hear of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills ?" asked the reporter. " Well, the girl sat where you do now," replied Mr. Boothroyd, pointing to the easy chair in which his visi bit- was comfortably ensconced, " When she accidentally saw the pills advertised in the newspaper. She asked me if I would let her try them, and I consented." Then, he explained, they purchased a box. Finding she was mending, she ceased taking the cod liver oil. She dropped that and took to the pills. She then improved steadily. The vomiting ceased, strength returned, and at last she began to get aboth as usual, until finally she returned to work, and now she feels as well and hearty as ever she did in her life.</p>
        <p>" There's no doubt whatever the pills set her up," declared Mr. Boothroyd emphatically. "She took five boxes of them altogether,each box lasting about a week." " Does she take anything still 2" " Nothing whatever in the way of medicine." "Not even the pills?" " No, she gave them up when she returned to her work." " And you attribute her cure entirely to the pills " " Yes, oh! yes." He added that Martha had enjoyed good health as a role until the severe illness from which she had just so providentiaill, recovered.</p>
        <p>"We feel sure the doctor did his best," said Mrs. Boothroyd, " but we quite expected Martha going into a decline. Her mother and two sisters died all within eighteen months. One of the girls was eighteen years old and the other twelve. The elder girl died in a de- cline, and no doubt the doctor expected this one would do the same.</p>
        <p>The reporter afterwards had a conversation with Mrs. Pogson, who lives near the Boothroyds. Mrs. Pogson had nursed Martha Boothroyd during her illness, and knew all about the case. She is the wife of David Pogson, a journeyman gardener in the em- ploy of Mr. Broadhead, of Wooldale. " How long have you known Martha Boothreyd ? " inquired the reporter. " Well, I knew her before we came here three years ago." " And did you attend her in her recent illness ?" "Oh ! yes, a very great deal, I did all for her in the bedroom." 'And was she very ill ?" " She was very bad at the far end. She was greatly plagued in her bowels, and very weak indeed. She could eat middling, but it never seemed 0 do her any good. She lay in bed a month or more. But she took Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. She said they did her good, and I believe they did, especially after the first box." " Did she then improve ?" " Oh ! yes. We felt rather surprised at it after the failure of the medicine. She kept mending till she was able to get out, and now she seems quite well and cheerful." The reporter began to understand Mr. Boothroyd's enthusiastic faith in Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. On his rejoining the cheery farmer in his poul- try yard, the conversation naturally reverted to the subject. The farmer declared that he should never cease to recommehd the pills, which he said were a topic of wide discussion in the neighbourhood at present. He had induced several people to try them, and so far the results had always been satisfactory. Relief had been afforded in many cases, although the pills had only been taken a few days. Among other instances he cited the case of an old man named Batty, living at Springwood, New Mill, who had heard of and procured the pills independently of him. Batty some time ago had a stroke, which laid him up, but since taking Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People lie had been able to get about again. _</p>
        <p>Down in Holmfirth, the reporter called and saw Mr. Edward Hinchliff, of flinchliff &amp; Co.'s drapers, South- lane, where Martha Ann Boothroyd is employed. His errand, he told the employer, was concerning the " wonderful cure." "Oh ! yes," at once exclaimed Mr. Hinchliff, "Martha has just passed through this room." And so she had. But the reporter would never have guessed it, for the girl he had seen looked as fresh and well as any he had seen that day. Martha was sum- moned forthwith. Meanwhile the reporter talked over the curious circumstances of the case with Mr. and Mrs. Hinchliff. The best evidence of 'Mr. Hinchliff's faith in the cure is that he is now taking Dr. Williams' Pink Pills himself, owing to indisposition from which he has been suffering, and has declared that he feels better already. Martha Boothroyd's illness and marvellous recovery were discussed, confirmation tieing given of the girl's long absence from work, and of her condition when the Hinchliff's had seen her durini her illness. She was, Mrs. Ilinchliff stated, as strong and well as Ever now— although at one time they thought they were going to lose her altogether—and was quite Equal to the work she formerly did. The girl herself, in appearance at least, fully corroborated this immediately she entered the room. She is decidedly good-looking, and there was nothing about her to indicate that she Wad recently so narrowly escaped a premature death. On the contrary, she smiled cheerfully and was agile in all her move- ments, and, attired in a neat white blouse, and with her scissors dangling at her side, looked the picture of health and business activity. S!.10 told the reporter she felt as well and strong as ever she did, and that she quite be- lieved Dr. Williams' Pink Pills had cured her. She did the same work as before her illness, and felt no fatigue either in working or walking. Many people, she said, who knew her, had expressed their surprise at her sud- den and complete recovery.</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People contain in a condensed form all the elements necessary to give new</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MR. J. M. WALKER.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MR. J. M. WALKER.</p>
        <p>At St. Andrews Church, Southport, on Wednesday afternoon, the nuptials were celebrated of Mr. J. Monro Walker, youngest son of the late Sir Andrew Barclay Walker, Bart., of the Grange, Gateacre, and Osmaston Manor, Derbyshire, and Miss E. Eileen Bardswell, only daughter of Mr. H. H. Bardswell, of " Larkfield," Albert-road, Southport. The church was filled with a fashionable congregation, and the chancel was decorated by Mr. Edward Carr with palms and flowering plants, the altar rails being fringed with white flowers. At the western porch, the bride, who entered on the arm of her father, who subsequently gave her away, was met by the choir, who sang, "The voice that breathed o'er Eden." The Rev. J. F. floater (rector of Maghull and locum tenens of St. Andrew's) performed the ceremony. The second hymn was " Thine for ever, God of love." Mr. J. C. Clarke, wh3 presided at the organ, played the " Wedding March " (Creser) composed for the Duke of York's marriage, the introduc- tion to the third act of " Lohengrin," the " Bridal March" from" Lohenerin," the "Festal March" (J. C. Clarke), " Berceuse " (Gounod), and the " Wedding March" (Mendelssohn). The bride was attired in a perfectly plain skirt of ivory satin duchesse, the folded bodice being trimmed with point de renise lace, the gift of her mother, with full Sir Joshua Reynolds sleeves,and rnade.with a court train suspended from the shoulder. Her veil was of tulle, fastened with real orange blossoms. The bride's ornaments were a tiara of diamonds, diamond crescent, diamond and pearl brooch, and diamond bracelet, which, together with a bouquet of choice exotics, were all the gifts of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were Miss Dorothy Wrigley, Miss Lisetta Walker (sister of the bridegroom), Miss Evelyn Bards- well,(cousin of the bride),Miss Eleanor Wrigley,and Miss Mable Longton. They were dressed in white satin gowns, the full bodices of which were arranged with Marie Antoinette fichus of white accordeon-pleated chiffon elbow sleeves. Tbe bridesmaids' large white hats were of drawn chiffon and had white feathers and pink roses. They carried :Flower bouquets of La France roses ; these, with diamond and pearl monogram brooches, being the gifts of the bridegroom. Two juvenile bridesmaids, Miss V. Walker, and Miss Cicely Selby, were attired in long, quaint white satin dresses and old-fashioned guipure bonnets. They carried baskets of pink roses. The best man was Mr. A. T. Neilson, of Halewood. The weather was brilliantly fine and the procession of carriages to and from the church was witnessed by large crowds ; while the bells of Christ Church gave forth merry peals. After the ceremony, a receptiOn was held at the Queen's Hotel, Southport, and later in the afternoon the happy pair left for Scotland, where the honeymoon will be spent. In October, Mr. and Mrs. J. Monro Walker will take up their residence at Hartford Grange, Hart- ford, Cheshire. The bride's travelling costume consisted of white serge, yellow silk bodice, covered with guipure lace and trimmed with pale pink velvet, and having large bell sleeves of white serge, finished off at the wrists with guipure. The hat was a sunburnt straw with pale pink chiffon and white Mercury wings and tiny roses under the brim. . .</p>
        <p>- The wedding presents, which were very numerous, were on view at the hotel. They included—Bridegroom to bride, diamond tiara, diamond bracelet, diamond and turquoise ring, diamond crescent, diamond and pearl brooch, diamond and emerald pendant; bride to bride- groom, diamond pin, gold ring, and miniature ; the Hon. Lady Walker, Brussels lace fan with diamond monogram and silver menu stand; Sir P. Walker, largo pink pearl and diamond bangle, silver tray and candlesticks ; Mr. and Mrs. J. It. Walker, silver eat re dishes, sauce boats, and salver; Major Walker, diamond star; Mr. A. B. Walker, sable rug; Mr. and Mrs. Court, silver tea kettle and stand ; Miss Walker, gold salts-bottle, with diamond monogram and silver cafe au last set; father of bride, silver dessert dishes and silver-mounted spirit decanters; Mr. and Mrs. W. Tod, travelling bag; Mr. and Mrs. Cunningham, silver breakfast dish ; the Rec,arder of Kingston, ostrich fan ; the Misses Bardswell, silver gilt ' coffee spoons; Mr. and Mrs. J. Ainsworth, silver bowl ; Mr. H. F. and Mr. G. R. Bardswell, silver tobacco jar; Mr. IL F., Mr. G. It., and Mr. 11. A. Bardswell, standard lamp; the employ&amp; at Warrington Brewery, clock, toilet set, and candlesticks; the employs at Burton Brewery, barometer ; Mr. Fulton Smith, silver dessert dish ; servants at Gateacre Grange, large silver bowl ; servants at Osmaston Manor, album with views of the manor; servants at " Larkfield," silver toast rack; Harriet (old nurse of bride), silver salts; Mrs. Clelland, silver afternoon tea set.</p>
        <p>In honour of the marriage of Mr. J. Monro Walker, the employes at the Warrington breweries of Messrs. Peter Walker and Son were on Wednesday treated to an excursion to Belle Vue Zoological Gardens. Over 400 were conveyed in a special train from the Central Station, Warrington. Free admission to the gardens was provided, and a substantial tea served in the large tea room. 70-day (Saturday) the workmen will further celebrate the event, as arrangements are being made for a dinner for thein at the Volunteer Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>LARGEST FAMILY IN THE WORLD.</head>
        <p>THE LARGEST FAMILY IN THE WORLD.</p>
        <p>According to the" Figaro," the largest family in the world is that of the King of Siam. His Majesty has two official wives, eighty-eight wives of minor order,and seventy-two children. Even if he had not been blest with any direct heirs the throne would not have gone out of the family, for the King has fifty brothers And sisters and 226 uncles and nephews. The King boards his relatives, and this explains why there should he as many as 200 cooks in the Royal kitchen.</p>
        <p>• CADBURY'S COCOA has, in a ien.arkable degree, those natural elements of sustenance which give the system endur- ance and hardihood, with a steady action that renders it a most acceptable and reliable beverage."—ffeaith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMEN 11.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMEN 11.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday, on the con- sideration of the Home Rule Bill as amended in Commit- tee, tile SPEAKER ruled that as Mr. Balfour was not in his place the clause standing in his name could not be moved. It was an amendment proposing to excrude the Irish members from the Imperial Parliament. The SPEAKER also ruled out of order clauses by Mr. Biddulph, Mr. Heneage, and Sir J. Goldsmid, the latter referring to Irish representation. General GOLDSWORTHY sought to move a new clause, but it was ruled out ef order. Another clause, standing in the name of Viscount Wolmer, fell through because he was not in his place. Four clauses by Mr. P. SMITH were ruled out of order, but he succeeded in moving a new clause to provide tor the appointment of Boundary Commissioners todraw up anew electoral scheme. Mr. T. W. RUSSELL was afraid that under the plan of the Government the Unionist element would be crushed. Mr. GLADSTONE said the schedule rested on the principle of population, and he could not consider that a corrupt plan. The discussion was continued by Sir Henry James, Mr. Roby, Mr. Balfour, and Mr. Goschen. Mr. JOHN MORLEY said the Government would consider the question of single-mem. ber constituences when the schedule came on if the House desired it. The clause was rejected by 196 votes to 152. Another clause, moved by Mr. P. Siam, that in the election of Irish members no special facilities should be given to illiterates, was also rejected. A further clause, moved by Mr. Smira, and having minority representation for its object, was also defeated, even Mc. Chamberlain declining to support it. The House adjourned soon after midnight.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords on Monday the Earl of NORTH - BROOK called attention to the exiting obstacles to the importation of manufactured silver from India. His Lordship said there was silver estimated at the value of 130 to 200 millions in the handA of the people of India. Until about a month ago the people could take the silver (principally in the shape of ornaments) to the mints and receive its value in rupees. In consequence of the clos- ing of the mints,the Hindoos could not find a market for their silver at its former price. The result was an enor- mous loss. The Earl of KIMBERLEY admitted that it was most desirable that some change should be made in the law, now that silver in India was only worth the gold value of the metal. The LORD CHANCELLOR, as chairman of the Currency Committee, said he was advised that there were no large hoards of silver in India. Papers relating to the subject were promised, and the subject dropped. TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday Mr. GLAD- STONE, replying to Mr. Jacas, said the Government did not intend to advise any adjournment of the House till they bad disposed of the remaining stages of the Home Rule Bill and achieved the business of voting Supply. The Government would then be disposed to advise an adjournment with a view to an autumn session. Mr. CHAPLIN moved the adjournment of the House to "call attention to the recent changes made in the cur- rency of India by the closing of the mints to the free coinage of silver with the sanction of Her Majesty s Government." He said the main advantage anticipated from this policy, the fixity of the rupee and the stability of the exchange between India and England had not been secured. The Government were escaping a deficit by tampering with the currency and artificially raising the value of the rupee. By their arbitary action they had depreciated an enormous amount of property in the Shape of uncoined silver, and not only inflicted a great wrong on the Indian people, but also caused a contrac- tion of silver securities throughout the world. He prophesied much disaster as a result of closing the mints, and charged the Government with being guilty of plun- der. Sir William Harcourt, in reply, said if the end of the world were to come, Mr. Chaplin would be found de- liverinr, an oration on bitnetalism. He asserted that the Government had acted carefully. The course they had taken was that indicated in the report of the Royal Commission in 1838, and they had adopted with a little modification the plan sent for their consideration by the Indian Governmtnt. Th2y believed that plan was the best for the Indian Government and the people of India, and though Mr. Chaplin was furious, it was a pity that he tried to incense the Indian people against the decision of the Government. In fact, his arguments were futile and mischievous, and he was not entitled to , make the currency question an instrument with which Ito strike at the interests of the Empire. Mr. Balfour contended that the Indian Government had been obliged to take the unhappy step with regard to the closing of the mints, and said the result must injure every human being in India who possessed a store of uncoined silver. The debate was continued by other members, among them being Mr. Courtney, who did not think the Government deserved Mr. Chaplin's condem- nation. The motion for the adjournment was negatived without a division. _ .</p>
        <p>The Home Rule Bill was then further considered. The Unionist amendment relating to the retention of the Irish members was discussed till the House adjourned at SIX minutes past twelve o'clock.</p>
        <p>In the House ef Lords several bills were advanced. Replying to the Bishop of Bangor, the Earl of Kimberley said the charter of the Welsh University had been printed, and woald be circulated. Lord Lovat took the oath and his seat on succeeding to his title. The House adjourned until the 22nd inst. WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday the Home Rule Bill, as amended, was further considered. Discus- sion on the clause moved by Mr. Macartney for the ex- elusion of the Irish members was resumed.. Mr. CRAM- BERLAIN said the Prime Minister had stated in his mani- festo that he desired that nothing should be done in the dark, but when the guilotine fell on the ninth clause not a soul in the country, and few in the House, had the slightest idea of the intentions of the Government on this question. In 1871 Mr. Gladstone denounced the idea of Home Rule. The Premier, interposing, said he did not denounce Home Rule. Mr. Chamberlain reminded him that in 1871, speaking at Aberdeen, lie said, " To accept Home Rule would be ridiculous." Mr. Gladstone re- torted that he did not make that statement in 1871. Mr. Chamberlain asked was it in 1870. An hon. member re- plied in the affirmative, and the Prime Minister said the distinction was important, for in 1871 Home Rule, in- stead of being Mr. Butt's opinion, had been adopted by the Irish Nationalist party. Mr. Chamberlain held that under the present arrangement the relations of the Irish Legislature to the Imperial Parliament would become in- tolerable, and that if they were to have an independent Parliament in Dublin the only logical course was to ex- clude the Irish members from Westminster. Sir Williatn Harcourt gave quotations from Mr. Chamberlain's speeches, showing how he had changed his coat. It was, he contended, only by keeping the Irish members at Westminster that they could make Ireland really part and parcel or the British Empire. Mr. Balfour followed and said the Home Rule Bill, intended to be a settlement of the Irish question, would be the beginning of bitter endless controversies. The clause was rejected by 221v0te5 against 181, a Government majority a 40. The House adjourned at twenty minutes to six o'clock. THURSDAY. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The House of Commons -t;it- Thursday, after a debate] lasting nearly an hour and a half, disagreed with thel Lords' amendment on the London Improvement Bill, and by 221 to 28 votes reinserted the " betterment " clause. Questions to Ministers elicited the information that since the Ist of January, 1890, there had been thirty accidents to her Majesty's ships; that the minutes of the Victoria court-martial, now being printed, would be laid on the table of the House; that the name of J. W. Hobbs would be removed from the commission of the peace for the borough of Croydon, and that of Jabez Spencer Bal- four from that for the county of Surrey ; that there was la decrease in army recruiting, and that the infantry of the line was 1,417 below the authorised number; that the slave trade in Egypt was illegal, and vigorously punished, but that the possession of slaves in that coun- try was not illegal; that Great Britian never had, or ever pretended to have, any protectorate over Swazi- land; that, notwithstanding Lord Salisbury's assertion In the House of Lords, the Redemption of Rent Act (1891) was really a Government Bill; that the report of the departmental committee on the system of classifica- tion of pay adopted in her Majesty's dockyards was of a confidential nature, and that it was not intended to make it,public ; and that Mr. Fdwards's report on the Welsh colleges and the proposed incorporation of a I Welsh University would not be laid on the table, though it had been alluded to on Tuesday in answer to a question. . Dr. Ambrose took..the oath and his seat for West Mayo, in room of Mr. Deasy, resigned. The House then resumed the consideration of the re- port on the Government of Ireland Bill. Sir Hama' JAMES moved a new clause proposing to withdraw from the Lord Lieutenant the power, conferred by an Act passed by the Irish Parliament in 1781, to suspend by proclamation the Habeas Corpus .Act in Ireland in cases where there was actual invasion or rebellion. He said that if it was necessary to suspend the Habeas Corpus Act the Irish Parliament could, after the adoption of his clause,have power to effect the suspension by legislation. After some discussion. Mr.GLAnsroxv intimated that the Cioverntnent would not resist the clause,which was then added to the Bill. Sir H. JAMES then proooied a clause providing for the establishment in every constituency of a separate register of the electors of the members of the Legislative Assembly and of the electors of the members of Parliament, and enacting that no elector should be entitled to be placed on more than one such register in respect of one and the same qualification, provided that every elector might select the register upon which his name should be placed. After remarks fm the Chan- cellor of the Exchequer, Mr. Chamberlain, and Mr. Sex- ton the clause was withdrawn. •</p>
