      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SAD DEATH OF A WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>SAD DEATH OF A WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>Thomas Littlewood, aged 52, of the Unicorn Inn, Winwick-road, died on Sunday morning at 10-30, under distressing circumstances. Deceased was standing on the cellar lid in his premises on Sunday, the 16th July, when it suddenly, gave way, and he was precipitated into the cellar, falling on kis head on the flag floor. He was brought up unconscious and attended to by Dr. Joseph. On Sunday morning last, he had three fits in succession, and succumbed to the third attack. An inquest was held at the Golden Rail Inn,on•Tuesday afternoon, by Mr. S. Brighouse. Mr. Hatton (Messrs. R. Davies and Co.) watched the case on behalf of the Railway Passengers' Assurance Company.—Maria Little. wood said about half-past eleven on the morning of the 16th July, her husband brought in a bucket which he had bees using in washing the window sills, and a few minutes afterwards, her son, who was in the cellar, screamed out, and on going into the cellar Mee found her husband in a heap on the floor. Assistance was ob- tained, and after washing his liead,on which there was a severe flesh wound, they put him to bed, and a doctor was sent for. He was in bed eight or nine days, and was attended-by Hr. Joseph until Wednesday last. He then Said he wanted to be from under the hands of the doctor, but complained of his back very much. He fell a depth of nine or ten feet through a trap door in the yard, which was only used for putting beer in. It had given way at one corner; and let him down, and then stuck in the framework. He could not recollect falling. He was insured for £250 in the Ralf way Passengers' In- surance Company. She did not know whether the com- pany disputed that her husband had died from the acci- dent, as she had not sent in a claim.—The Coroner remarked that lie was not going to let theman be buried without a post-mortem examination, if lie thought there would be a dispute.—Mr. Hatton (Messrs. Robert Davies and Co.) said lie was simply instructed to watch the case on behalf of the society, and he did not know what view they would take.—Tbe Coroner said he himself was quite satisfied with the evidence.—Mr. Rigbey, sta- tion master at the Central Station, said he did not think there would be any dispute. The company would no doubt be guided by the verdict of the jury.—Alfred Arthur Littlewood, son of the deceased, deposed that he was cleaning the cellar floor on the morning in question, when he observed the door of the cellar sinking and presently his father came through, and alighted on hia head on the floor. Deceased was confined to bed for about a week afterwards.—By Mr. Hatton : His father was ill about twelve months ago, and was attended for about a week by Dr. Joseph. On that occasion he had a fit. Witness also ststed that his father did not fall through the cellar door until a few minutes after the door itself fell.—After some deliberation the jury re- turned a verdict of "Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SIIOCKING ACCIDENT TO " TOMMY</head>
        <p>SIIOCKING ACCIDENT TO " TOMMY</p>
        <p>BD RNS.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon Torn Burns. who advertises himself as " the world's champion diver," met with a terrible accident whilst fulfilling an engagement at the Botanical Gardens, Sheffield. Burns came into notoriety a few years ago by a daring leap from London Bridge, and more recently became popular by his di.:ing feats at the Westminster Aquarium. The performance on Mon- day consisted of a dive of seventy feet into a'shallow tank, twenty feet long by nine fef t wide and eight feet deep. At five o'clock Burns came out of his tent and prepared for his sensational performance. He was hauled up towards the platform by pulleys and ropes, and had nearly reached it when his shoulders struck underneath it. The additional strain on the derrick caused the poles to snap, and Burns fell to the ground with a terrible thud, he was picked up unconscious, and removed in an ambulance to the hospital, where it was found that his right shoulder was dislocated, and he had also concussion- of the brain. He is very badly injured. The accident was witnessed by thousands of people. Burns, it is reported, is progressing satisfactorily towards recovery. When lie was admitted to the hospital his condition was believed to be critical. The shock to the system was terrible, the flesh on one of his arms wa,s badly smashed, his right shoulder was disloc- ated, he complained of pain at the chest and the 'oottom of the back; and he vomited a great quantity of blood. He was extremely weak, and fears were entertained that he 1ia..1 been injured internally to a very serious extent. On Tuesday Burns was distinctly bettor. Mrs. Burns rst stayed with her husband all through Monday night, not leaving him until she learned from the doeto showed great signs of improvement. The great loss of blood had taken much of his stretig!h away, but heh at had he less pain, and was able to talk to his wife. In the course of conversation, Burrs has stated that be could remember very little about the accident. He recollected being drawn up and hearing the creaking of the appara- tus. He called out to the men who were working the ropes to pull him further away from the platform. and , they endeavoured to do so. He then shouted "Pull quickly," thinking that he should clear the platform and however, caught the edge.akeneffert to seize hold a "astoedp before upon i he ct. Houisld shoulder, any- thing the derrick fell to tlie ground. Burns states that just before the performance he suggested that a ladder should be procured for his ascent, but .he was told that there were no ladders at hand long enough, and there was no time to fetch one from the town. He also states that he went to work with a light heart, bat he could not help remarking to a Liverpool friend of his, George Patterson, " I shall he all right when I get up, but I don't like thc pulley apparatus ; it is very shaky."</p>
      </div>
