      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. AUGUST 12, 1893.</p>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE made an important British Legisiation. announcement in the House of Commons, on Tuesday evening. It was to the effect that the Government do not intend to adjourn Parliament until the remain- ing stages of the Home Rule Bill have been disposed of, and until the voting of supply has been accomplished. After that they proposed to , hold an autumn session for the despatch of other ' business. This announcement was greeted with enthusiastic cheering by the supporters of the Government in the House of Commons, and it will, as has been aptly said, infuse fresh spirit into the Liberal party from Land's End to John o'Groats. It is a further indication that the majority do not intend to allow themselves to be thwarted by the vaunting minority opposed to them. Several leading mendeers of the Opposi- tion have declared, with an iteration which be- trayed how enamoured they were of the prospect, that this session, and indeed this Parliament, , would be devoted to Irish legislation, and Irish legislation alone. With an arrogant assumptiin that would have been scarcely justifiable had they possessed a superiority of votes, they ordained that all tho energies of the Government and all the time of the legislature . were to be taken up with the discussion of the Home Rule Bill, and as far as all other legislation was concerned there was to be absolute sterility. Sham prophets often' attempt to bring about the fulfilment of their own prophecies, and it was so in this case, but their -amiable intentions were defeated by the. vigour and resolution of the Government Hav- ing found that their cry that everything is being sacrificed to Home Rule will not hold good under the altered circumstances, they have turned their attention to Supply as affording an ample field for the devices of the obstructionist. But here again it appears as though they were to be out-manoeu- vred. Mr. GLADSToNE announces that Supply is to be dealt with before the adjournment, and this, of course, will leave the autumn session entirely open for the "other business " which was re- ferred to. The Tories and their allies may, no doubt, be relied upon to exercise all their very considerable ingenuity in concocting motions and "amendments" for the delay of business. They will, however, be faced by the rather dis- tressing fact, that the more they talk the shorter will be the period of their holidays. The Govern- ment have &amp;Tided that Supply must be finished before the adjournment, and there- fore the time that is wasted in discussing the Estimates will be deducted from the interval between the adjournment and the reassembling for the autumn. session. Should the less responsible members of the Opposition attempt to carry out to the full extent their threats of obstruction the Governuient can still fall back upon the closure. Such a course will be perfectly justifiable in the country, and it will be supported by Mr. CIIAMBERLAIN—that is to say, if Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, Who has a great deal to say about the inconsist encice of others, acts consist- ently with his expressed opinions. Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN, once denounced, when a supporter of the Government of the day, the prolonged discussion of the Estimates. At that time he wrote : "No serious purpose—no national object—is served by those discussions, which really prevent careful examination of the estimates, and criticism of the policy on which they are based. The House is wearied by the irrelevance of the speeches and the ignorance of speakers with no special acquaintance with their subject, and' the whole debate degener- ates into a physical contest of endurance between the overworked Ministers on the one side and the free lances of the Opposition on the other." Mr. CHAMBERLAIN went further than that. He sug- gested that the House should fix beforehand the number of days to be occupied by each class of the Estimates, andigthat at the end of every klitich period the class specified should be reported forth- with. Such are the recorded opinions of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, and as that gentleman places so high a valtul upon consistency he can not decently blame the Government if they, once in a way, act upon his advice. As to the measures which the Government propose to push forward in the ! autumn session, there has been no authoritative announcement. but it seems to be taken for granted that, whatever may be said about other legislation, the Parish Councils Bill is not likely to be neglected.</p>
        <p>81-METALLISM has been very much Bi- Metallism. in evidence lately,but the advocates</p>
        <p>of that monetary- system will not find any encouragement in the Message which the President of the United States has addressed to Congress. America has attempted by legislation to fix a ratipbetween gold aryl silver, with the rvsult that it hns, broilght about such an "n!arming</p>
        <p>and extraordinary business situation," that the President has been constrained to call Congrese together to repeal the law. " Whatever else the people have ," says Mr. CLEVELAND, "a right tn.. expect from Congress they may certainly deman° that legislation, condemn° d by an ordeal of three years disastrous experience, shall be removed from the statute books as soon as their representative° can legitimately deal with it." There is in the President's message another passage which will quote in full, as it deals with a phase of th currency question which does not receive .ths, attention from bi-metallists in this countrYt weo such as Mr. BALFOUR and Mr. 11.005' for instance, which it deserves. "There is," Wel the President, "one important aspect of the ject which especially should never be overleobed At times like the present, when the evils 9f 111/' sound finance threaten us, the speculator fnal anticipate a harvest gathered from the misfortune of others; the capitalist may protect himself 1)1 hoarding, or may even find profit in the fluctuatioll of va:ues ; but the wage-earner, the first to bni,nrr jured by a depreciated currency, and the lest 7. receive the benefit of its correction, in Prat, cally defenceless. He relies for work upon t"." ventures of confident and contented capital. 1111 f failing him, his condition is without alleviat</p>
        <p>for he can neither prey on the rnisforturro4 others nor hoard his labour. One of the greatesre statesmen our country has known, speaking 111° more than fifty years years ago, when a den, merit of the currency had caused commercial tress, said: The very man of all others who</p>
        <p>_ the deepest interest in a sound currency, end suffers most by mischievous legislation in</p>
        <p>• matters, is the man who earns his bread by ',- toil.' These words are as pertinent to-day as the day they were uttered, and ought to jig sively remind us that failure in the dischr</p>
        <p>our duty at this time must especially injure of our countrymen who labour and who, * of their number and condition, are entitled to most watchful care of their Government." is the language of a statesman who measures I value of a political question by its effects upon whole community, and not merely by its infior upon the fortunes of a section, even though</p>
        <p>section be, socially and commercially, a important one. If bi- metallism means a</p>
        <p>ciated currency the workingmen of this cot° must be taken into account before its a(</p>
        <p>can be secured.</p>
        <p>WE remarked a fortnight ago thfi/ Lords versus . Commons. it E was quite within the range of ....</p>
        <p>..... sibility that the House of might be in conflict with the popularly Chamber, even before the Home Rule Bill come before their lordships. The cause ef remarks was the fact that tho Peers had ' cally struck out what was known as the" merit clause" in a Bill promoted by the. County Council, althoegh that clause had endorsed by by a large majority in the Conir The justice of the principle embodied ia lause is so apparent that very few Conservai who have to appeal to the constituencies have tured to question it. What it provides for is when certain property in London has been s" enhanced in value by an expensive publie provernent which it is proposed to carry °tit' part of the enhanced value shall be - taxed. As an instance of what happens these improvements are carried out, it, is said the making of the Mack wall Tunnel raised value of neighbouring land from fifty to a - sand pounds the acre. The House of Lade dominated by ground landlords, and with ellf teristic selfishness their lordships -col even so mild an affirmation of the bett principle as that contained .in the London and the clause was accordingly expunged. 111 list of those who voted for expungement are' the "Daily News" says, some of the most WI) lar names in the country. "There that journal, "the DUKE of ARGYLE', the compromising champion of a landlord's defensible rights. There is Lord NES- who, as Lord LYMINGTON, attained the onlY tinction of his life by being the first to ratlit, the Liberal party in the House of Commons. is Lord LONDONDERRY, who, having fail° govern Ireland himself is doing his best tO- vent Lord Houourox from succeeding vih_le failed. There is Lord ASHBOURNE a type °s, least desirable form of Irish Tory, and WEMYSS, the President of the well-known for preserving its own property, while - se, liberties with other people's." A Liberal 00 Comreons, however, as might be expected, ' lutely declines to be dictated to by Peers of ;be type thus caustically described. On Thursdal tbe Bill again came before that House, and, bYtr significent majority of two hundred and Woe, one votes to eighty-eight, the ,41eted clause u re-instated. The Bill will thus be sent b3c the Lords in its original shape, and it will be, to teresting to observe how they will get put ethel 00, predicament into which they have brought selves.</p>
        <p>_ AN .UNFOIITIINATN EietatsrosusT. a'oo„s Joseph Pemberton, Warringtononet With s accident whilst journeying to Douglas on the Qii ner Victoria from Lirtrpool, en Saturday. 01° cending the -companion into the saloon, '1(1 t° }waled, and losing his balance pitched forwa'oll' the bottom, sustaining a fracture to the right, t„ ftl6„ On the arrival of the steamer to Dotigtil 1,1 sufferer was placed on a stretcher and retn°, ,o. the hospital, where his injury was attendett t' is prebable.that Mr. Pemberton will be non" the hospital for ten weeks or more. „ „ 14t' UMBRELLAS RE-COVERED.-7. and W.. DITTToN. Warrington.-Advt. 017DORN DEATH AT LATCHFORD, btef Mary Elizabeth Boardman,aged 13 years,dongfora of Charles and Elizabeth Boardman, 306, lintiteneOrt road, died at about 7-50 p.m. on Monday. it that deceased had been subject to fits for,. tn° three years, and was attended by Dr.KisbY, ""10 sume for about twelve months ago. She seeln" 00 get strtnger, but :had a fit on Walking delP ooP Monday she appeared quite well, and went 10a old in Knutsford-road. When she was returningre9. was seized with a fit, and tell down IS theui, She was carried into a house and Dr. Booiiiioo, called in, but abe was then dead. He was of that she had had a fit of apoplexy._ The bodYo,ld. afterwards removed to her home in Enutsford-r or a flee ALT. HoUSENOLD LINEN, hammed and mark* • chariv. —J. and W. DuTToN, Sankey at.—. Advt. WARRINGTON BOARD OF G tuna:lAo.. q held The fortnightly meeting of this Board wa-does' in the Board room of the Workhouse on I„Ve,„fiff: day, ween there were present 0. (chairman), R. Beaton, A. J. Jolley. W. Still gal' Davies, Hill, J. Houghton, A. Jolley, "G. Pilling harry, W, Hougkton, Turton, J. C. Sutton (C10r,.140/tolt Sutton (deputy clerk), C. H. Ward (Work.no.ol, master), and Mines (Paclgate Schools super' ,uid dent). The minutes of the House Committee 14,11Prry, contained leaves of absence to Mr.Curry,Mtbs 6, !dr: ; ‘,s and Miss Leigh were passed on 