        <p>DOG WITHOUT LICENSE AT WIDNES. t „Oa Thursday, at Widnes Petty Sessions, before Aid. F. :4-Gossage (Mayor) and Mr. J.C.Gamble. Michael Nor- L'a!id was ordered to pay 19s. 6d. for keeping a dog without a license- , ACCIDENT AT THE WIDNES ALKALI WORKS. le e,•terday morning, a man named Charles McLoughlin, iteut of a wagon and alighting on his head sustained titins injeries to his head. He lies at the Accident despital lii an unconscious condition. The WIDNES CYCLE CLUB CH HI AMPIONSP. tv_ Ve miles annual championship race took place at :'avertrce grounds on Thursday night. D. Daglish, the Present owner of the championship cup, was again an aeaslBoY winner, and retains possession of the trophy. He wins a gold medal. W. Sherratt was second and F. rzinesdrraiattth,!crei7d are awarded silver and bronze s resp NlEnsxy MISSIONS TO SEAMEN, RUNCORN. 11,," anniversary was celebrated on Sunday, when ser- Cas were preached by Mr. H. G. Jones an the Rev. , iiktl°ll Maitland Wood, vicar. In the afternoon the a Choir and children rendered the service of song entitled -I,lleacon Light." Mr. E. unison read the narrative. I se ere were large congregations, and the collections I , Wised over £5. 1 SUCCESS OF A WIDNES STUDENT. Mr- Jeff H. shores, son M H B Shores,. Liberal o. r. H. B. . tagent for Widnes Division, has been awarded by the £slolcashire County Council the chemical scholarship of 4it a Year, tenable for three years at Liverpool Univer- Colleee. Mr. Shores, who is 18 years of age, was ''.‘ileateceat St. Bedes, Appleton, and for some years Past at St. Francis Xaviers, Liverpool. WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL, WESTON POINT.. ,th.rl Monday the scholars, teachers, and friends held • the annual excursion. The steamer left Saltport in More ,ostharn ing and proceeded along the Ship Canal toi , where the trippers disembarked and a variety • th"-raes were indulged in. After thoroughly enjoying atr,enlaelves they re-embarked in the evening, and safely "ved at Saltport about nine o'clock. _ ROBBERY AT AN IRISH WAKE AT WIDNES. .11(74wrencP Brown, a youth, 17 years of age, whose% wirtri, at 38 Cromwell-st., was charged on remand, • nk stealing is. 6. from the person of another youth ivined Patrick Culvert. On Saturday the two youths I-47. at an Irish wake at the house of a man named . Abluiti, at 3 Nelson-st ,and Culvert fell asleep on a form. out five o'clock on Sunday morning the prisoner was to cut the pr osecutor's waistcoat pocket out with a • sh't ef scissors. Afterwards when apprehended denied ii„e theft, but he ultimately showed a constable where • had hidden the coin. 0 YOUTHFUL HOUSEBREAKER AT WIDNES. Thursday, at Widnes Petty Sessions, Catherine 14„42,:.2dY, Reed 13, of Caroline-street, was charged with of3r2ng and entering the house of Nehemiah Cookson, Ichfield-icad,Farnworth, and stealing a quantity of tssatlag apparel and a silver geneva watch. On Wed- e'sC,,a,_Y afternoon, Mrs. Cookson left the house at one hoa-cx and returned at five. In the meantime the 4,l;:se had been entered by the back window, and the stelen. In the evening P.C. Almond met the ner wearing the stolen hat and bodice. She made a i,'"tZernent implicating two boys who bad not been ap- hended. The prisoner was remanded until Monday. FIRE AT FARNWORTH. atlout four o'clock on Thursday afternoon, PithuaYloft above the stables of the Farnworth oe n and Size. Co.'s Works was discovered to be r,°. An alarm was at once raised, and the Widnes • "tigade were sent for, and soon arrived under the oe;:taand of Mr. Thorpe. A plentiful supply of water re- 4t hand, and after the enegertic exertions of the 'tleeTen and a number of the police the flames were sub- but not before a quantity of hay and straw was • ;I.°Yed. The roof of the building also fell in. The byri,r4rinsuorfathheee. fire is unknown. The damage is covered iaolc,"'inowen AND THE HOUSEKEEPER AT RUNCORN. 3 Tuesday at Runcorn Petty Sessions Annie Massey, • org woman, was charged with steeling a quantity sat °thing and a silver watch chain, the property of Ashley, labourer, of Prescot Terrace. It 1'‘2,2,,,• "eated that for eighteen months the prisoner had s's,„;" in Ashley as his 'housekeeper. On Friday the 28th fs;akwas the worse for drink and he told her to find a tai;" Place- Next day she went and subsequently he atoised the articles. These had been pawned with Mr. th B°o, Mr. Marsh and Mr. Craddock, who stated that 4,Prisoner had on several occasions pledged the goods, :0 redeemed them.—The woman said she had done so pr"ee_n there as not sufficient money coming in and to )21,0;ent the children from starving. Ashley knew quite Th" she was in the habit of going to the pawnbrokers._ e Pr i s2cutor denied this.—The magistrates dismissed ea?e, OnnFEttows DEMONSTRATION AT HALTON. t,a Monday morning, 47 of the members of the Loyal fent' ni Chester Lodge of the Independent order of Odd- assembled at their headquarters, Norton Arms, *On, for their bi-annual gathering. The procession, Ich -was led by the Widnes Subscription Prize Band, torched through the village, and from thence to Run- On retaining a splendid dinner was served at the asa‘3"34 Arms. The Secretary Mr. Henry Dale said he pki 4'etY pleased to say their society was in a very sPerous condition, for although they had lost three Lo7dinr,bers by death, and others had withdrawn from the ThelPi Yet they had twenty new members. (Applause.) i ast balance sheet showed the receipts n connection ,4 the sick and funeral fund to be £l9O 7s. 2P., ballisNt the expenditure WAS £57 4s. Old., leaving a • -nce of £433 3s. 2d. The receipts in the manage- tl4 dePartment amounted to £4l 178. 7id., and the £lO, rsements to £3l 6s. 611, which left a balance of terAllB. id. The customary toasts were drunk, and a ta,"lai vote of thanks accorded to the Secretary for the th;oner in which he had performed his duties during Past seven years. Dancing was subsequently indul- 'in on the " green until about nine o'clock. The HALTON WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL. annual treat of the Halton Wesleyan Sunday rwas held as usual on Bank Holiday. For the • ater part of the morning the weather was threaten- soß but the showers became, as the day wore on, like io tars 'Sits," few and far between," until, when even- the weather was perfect. The scholars as- two 'ed early in the afternoon, and after spending about th,, hours in Duke's field, kindly lent by Mr. Nixon, r.eturned to school evidently determined to discuss hr, erlous and destructive fashion the substantial vianda 441131 red for them. It was a sight to witness the attack eor„ t 4 hear the children's shout of victory at the dis- ssiAture of cake, &amp;c., was most refreshing. Having e. d about 200 of the youngsters, the older scholars th.nds then partook of tea, there being over a hun- feh'yf these, then all returned to the field, where the thitLleCarne fast and furious, culminating in a concen- -11.2.1,`", expression of astonishment and delight, when a titi•;" Of six hlontgollier balloons took pface, the last one. sized elephant, and appearing to travel as far as th;trington. When the shades of evening begaia to fall (sri scholars and friends returned te the chapel stops and 0144 the Doxology, and afterwards gave three hearty lichoZfor Mr. Nixon, Mr. Hanby, the parents and the ▪ in Want of a CHASTE and ELEGANT WEDDING Or Ts(K• -"4:2' PRESENT goto PRITCHARD'S, 51, Itigh-st,—Advt. A. ALLEGED SETTING FIRE TO A HOUSE AT RUNCORN. -Runcorn Police Court on Saturday, before Mr. es Handley, Thomas Henry Spender, 60 Greenway- *;";;,, Runcorn, was brought up, on remand, charged tbl7 having on the previous Sunday unlawfully and troa,„(ll^nsly set fire to his house, Fanny Fleetwood, his ha'"eatie servant being then therein The circumstances krve,,been reported and no evidence was now called.— for'l,`.t. Linaker, who eppeared for the prisoner, asked ao„"is discharge on the ground that the act had not been tnaliciously or with intent to defraud, and that the ▪ !t girl's was not endangered.—Mr. Handley said it re,,„lils.opinion that at the time the prisoner was not lallt.'Ts'ble for his actions through the effects of drink, telt tilat was no excuse in law. At the same time he lat he should not he taking a wrong, view if be •t„ that there had been no malicious or fraudulent in- . A u in the hope that the prisoner had now received that would be a warning to him and a turning ttlagl._7litin his career, he would set him at liberty. The Ill) a., _rate Urged the prisoner to remember his bringing expressed the hope that he would take in the 10,1iicTI Which it was intended, the prerogative of mercy, 'was 1 he (Mr. Handley) was exercising.—The prisoner then discharged. Rtr,„,„, „ ORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE—CADET SECTION. • members of the above juvenile order had their treat to Overton on Saturday. They numbered aatr• ut, 60 and were accompanied by Mr. Edward Rey eV, and Mr. Thomas Whyte treasurer, and mem- nr the Committee. The boys rambled about the lehi4tld indulged in various games. Tea was provided Tut WaS enjoyed nV the children.. After tea a !dent"- ”)Pl3? Of nuts and sweets was given to them. At a ehai'ing afterwards, Mr. Daniel Bisbrown occupied the he,pr,. He called upon Mr. Thomas Cooke to move a th'Y Vote of thanks to a number of leading gentlemen Aria tnw he n for their kindness in subscribing to t treat, ) the secretary slid treasurer for collecting the %"r!s• This was seconded bc Mr. Sam M.'Temperley, and nnanimouslv. The Chairman referred to the im- ur • .3 nee Of the work in which they were engaged, of the liii;t•"snt neeessity of teaching them young habits of thrift the lrietY. Their past history, as an Order, proved for _4llle of the juvenile order, as a rdirsery and feeder adult lodges. There were many terrible pitfalls brid, en. path and they needed advice and counsel, and tedly total ahs'inence would be a safeguard to Lot: in lifp. llfter thoroughly enjaying th, mselves :•a2 r`,:ael'e ah.alt 9 o'clock.</p>
