      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ENRY ATILLING &amp; CO, LTD</head>
        <p>ENRY ATILLING &amp; CO, LTD</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>NELSON-STREET WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN. The anniversary sermons were preached in Camden Chapel on Sunday, by the Rev. John Kirtlan, of War- rington. In the afternoon the Rev. W. Arrowsmith de- livered an address. The orchestral band assisted in the musical services. The collections were in aid of the school fund. JoSEPIC PRITcfrARD, 51. High-street, Runcorn, keeps SPECTACLES to suit all sights, from a shilling a pair.—Adrt ROBBERY OF £9 AT WIDNES. At Widnes Petty Sessions on Thursday before the Mayor (Alderman Gossage), and Mr. J. C. Gamble, Martha Watson, age 27, of no fixed abode, was charged with stealing £9 from the dwelling-house of Peter Cain, 4, Oxford-st. The woman had been lodging with the prosecutor for some time, and on Thursday the 2nd ' Inst., she suddenly departed. Next morning it was dis- covered that she had taken the cash box containing £9. She had spent the money in drinking in Liverpool where she was twice in the hands of the police. She stated she had been robbed of a portion of the money in Hun- ' ter-st., Liverpool.—The magistrates sentenced the pri- soner to a month's imprisonment. Go to PRITCHARD's, 51. High-street. RII7ICOTTI, for the best FIVE GUINEA. WATCH a workingman can have.—Advt. • BANK HOLIDAY AT RUNCORN. A general holiday was taken in Runcorn on Monday, and there was a great exodus from the town. Liverpool was the destination of the great proportion of the rail- way passengers. Some hundreds travelled to Rhyl where the Volunteers were encamped. The railway traffic was not so heavy as last year. The members and friends of the Gospel Mission, Hope Hall, had their annual trip, in the shape of a pleasant Sail on the Bridgewater Canal to Moore and a day in the country. The annual excursiom of the Mersey Mission to Seamen was also held. About 200 had a sail down the river in a specially engaged steamer. The passengers had an op- portunity of viewing the training ship Indefatigable. RUNCORN BURIAL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Cemetery on Wednesday. Mr. Imison presided and there were pre- sent Messrs. Coleburn, Littler, Dr. Gaetaldi and Williana F.Salkeld (sec.) The report stated that the interments for the four monns ending July 25th, slumbered 190; last year 149, increase 41. The fees received for the same period amounted to £172 123. 10d. ; last year, £l5l 13s. Bd. ; increase, £2O 193. 2d. The report of the Finance Committee was received and adopted.—The secretary read a letter from Mr. E. Marshall, sec. to Runcorn Improvement Commissioners requesting the use of the Cemetery field for a recreation ground for the town. As there were only four members present, it was decided to defer consideration of the matter until next meeting. If you want your WATCH REPAIRED by skilled workmen take it to PRITCHARD'S.—Advt BANK HOLIDAY AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The tradesmen of Widnes universally recognised Bank Holiday, and at most of the'works, processes were not in full operation, and the men made holiday. The morning was beautifully fine, but heavy showers fell during the afternoon, a ploaslint evening succeeded. The railway companies issued tickets for Liverpool at a cheap fare, and consequently upwards of a thousand visited that place. Blackpool and Southport were also visited by a large number. The adult members of Lacey-at., school kad their annual picnic to Alton Towers, where a plea. ant day was spent. The annual gala in connection with St. Mary's Roman Catholic Young Men's Society was held in the afternoon on St. Mary's Football ground. Varioussports were provided including greasy pole climbing, tug of war, obstacle and other races. The rain interfered with the sport but there was a large attendance. St. Mary's band played for dancing.</p>
        <p>PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test for fifty years, and qnality and first-class workmanship are still guaranteed.— Advt.</p>
        <p>UPPER MERSEY WATERMEN'S AND PORTERS' ASSOCIA- TION.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn branch of this association held a de- monstration on Bank Holiday. Unfortunately, the weather was unfavourable, and there was not so largo a muster RS had been anticipated. A procession was formed at St. John's schoolroom, the headquarters of the association, and headed by the Earl of Chester's Rifle Volunteer Band and the beautiful banner, which was unfurled twelve months ago by Mrs. Brunner. In the ranks, which numbered about 200, many small flags were carried. The procession passed through the prin- cipal thoroughfares, and on returning to the schoolroom a tea party was held. In the evening a public meeting was held under the chairmanship of Mr. Speed,of Pres- ton Brook, the president, who delivered a speech show- ing the advantages offered by the society. Mr. Ellis Gatley, the secretary, also spoke, and pointed out that althoueh the society was only formed in 1889,there were now 900 members. On many occasions the association had been of the greatest advantage to the members, and he urged those men who had not yet joined to do so at once. An entertainment of a miscellaneous character was given by a number of ladies and gentlemen. Messrs. Price and Robinson contributed songs, and Mr. George Williams gave two recitations in good style. Master Alfred and Miss Lillie Teinperley played a pianoforte and violin duet most excellently. Miss Temprrley also played the violin solo " Blue Bells of Scotland " with variations. Both the young musicians give promise of much talent. The Volunteer Band contributed two selec- tions. The pianoforte was kindly lent by Mr.Temperley, of Lowland's-road. JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High et., Runcorn.—Advt.</p>
        <p>CLAIM AGAINST MESSRS W. R. EARP AND CO., PRESTON</p>
        <p>BROOK.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn County Court on Tuesday, before his Honour Judge Ffoulkes, W. H. Bradbury, of Halton Brook, sued W. R. Earp, and Company, tanners, Preston Brook, for £3O money lent.—Mr. Tobin (instructed by Messrs, Linaker and •Linaker) was for the plaintiff and Mr. F. M. Preston (instructed by Mr. F. A. Lake) for the defendant. For the plaintiff it was stated that in 1886 the defendant was advising Messrs. Reynolds of Astmoor Tannery, with respect to the chemistry depart- ment of the tan-yard. The plaintiff was employed there. Subsequently the defendant took the Preston Brook Tannery and plaintiff having some ready money lent the defendant £3O, to hear interest at 5 per cent. Later he lent another £5O, and the plaintiff having spent a considerable amount of time in helping and instructing the defendant in certain processes at his tan-yard and particularly in the manipulation of a depilator, and the defendant as remuneration for his services gave him an 1.0. U. for a further £5O. The defendant repaid by instalments two £25 and £l4 and the plaintiff accepted a bill for £B6. The defendant also paid interest up to date. No further transactions took place. At the beginning of this year the plaintiff came across the note for £3O and immediately wrote to the defendant asking for payment. The defen- dant replied th it he did not owe the money,and that the £3O was included in the settlement of 1891. when the transactions finished.—The defence was a total denial of the claim. The defendant also denied that he had ever consulted the plaintiff as to any process in tanning and that the services he rendered were in the warehouse. In payment the note for £3O was made into £5O, but the plaintiff at the time said he could not find the original note for £3O, but he would let Mr. Earp have it, when he did so. The defendant said so far from needing advice from the plaintiff he was often consulted by tanners in their manufactories, and received £1,200 ' in fees during the period of three years.—His Honour. in giving judgment, said looking at the fact that the I plaintiff had not demanded any interest since 1891, he would come to the conclusion that the balance of evi- dence was in favour of the defendant. He accordingly gave judgment for the defendant with costs. A YOUNG THIEF.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newton, on Saturday, before Colonel .1. L. Wood, a boy named Charles Cullen, 12 years of age, was charged with stealing the sum of 10s. from the house of Septimus W. Bothwell, Legh-screet, Earlestown, on Friday. The lad, who had been living in Blue Coat-st., Warrington, went to the house of Mr. Bothwell, on the 2nd inst., and told a pitiful story to the effect that he was an orphan and was almost starving. Out of sympathy for the lad, Mr. Bothwell took him in and lodged him. On Friday evening, Bothwell placed a half sovereign on the kitchen mantle piece and went out for a little while, leaving the lad in the room, Mrs. Bothwell also being present. Shortly after- wards the lad left the house, and it being dis- covered that the money was missing, he was followed, and was found at the railway station where he had purchased a ticket for Leigh, which with the balance of the half sovereign, was found in his possession. From inquiries it transpired that the lad's parents live at Leigh and that he absconded from home five months ago. He was remanded to Newton Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>ALL HOUSMIOLD LINEN. hemmed ana marked, free of charge.—l. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-st.—Advt. BANK HOLIDAY AT WAIIII,INGTON...</p>
        <p>Several showers of rain fell on Monday (August Bank holiday), in Warrington,but this did not deter the inhabitants from seeking pastures new if we are to judge by the business done by the railway companies and the local waggonette proprietors. On the London and North-Western Railway from Saturday to Monday the traffic was exceptionally heavy. Many took advantage of the week end trips on Saturday, and the staff at the station had a heavy time of it, but everything passed off without the slightest hitch, much to the credit of Mr. Starkey, stationmaster, and other officials. A large number of excursion trains passed through Warring- ton to the north, but there was only one special train to Blackpool from Warrington. Business was also very brisk at the Central Station, and Mr. Ingbey and his staff worked hard. The receipts largely exceed those of previous years. _About 2„500 went to Mancliester and the same number to Liver- pool, 700 to Southport, and the other places of in- . tercst on this line were well patronised. There was also a number of picnics to various places of :interest in the vicinity. In the afternoon the chief '1 attraction was the Football Club Sports, and these were largely attended.</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>DIVERGENT INTERVIEWS.</p>
        <p>At 12-50 yesterday morning, Mrs. Esther Berry, widow, 32, Pinner's-brow, was awakened by hear- ing a crackling noise. She got out of bed, and on looking out of the window she saw that the bake- i house of Mr. Henry Charles Bennett, confectioner, 55, Pinner's-brow, was on fire. She hurried down stairs, and by beating at the door of Mr. Bennett's shop succeeded in arousing the inmates of the house. Mr. Bennett realised the condition of things in a moment, for his bedroom was tilled with smoke and there was a horrible stench of burning wood. He rushed onto the landing and saw huge volumes of smoke emanating from the room next to his, and which was at the top.of the stairs. In this room three of his children slept. He burst open the door, alarmed the children, and seeing there were no flames in the room,he warned them to get out of the house, and then rushed down to the bakehouse. Through a passage filled with a rolling cloud of choking smoke, he made his way until he had entered the bakehouse. A mo- ment's observation showed him that his own efforts would avail nothing here; long ton- gues of flame were darting from the woodwork about the oven, spreading along the wooden sup- ports of the wall,and devouring with a terrible crackle the support beams of the ceiling. Above the ceil- ing was a storeroom, and net to it was the room in which the yoting Bennetts slept. Bennett rushed off out of the room which was now filled with the flames; he saw people hurr3flng upstairs, and cal- culating that they would see to the safety of his family, he rushed barefooted to the fire bell in the Market Place. In the meantime a scene of in- tense excitement was kappening at the burning house ; crowds of half dressed, feat stricken people • from the neighbourhood had gathered about in the road ; the flames had gained the mastery of the bakehouse, had forced their way into the store- room and were now illuminating the whole neigh- hood Willing hands had set to work to remove as much Of the furniture from the howe as possible,and while this was going on it was discovered that Mr. Bennett's children were missing. By this time the tames had spread from the roof of the storehouse and were licking as they leaped, the window sash and cases of the chitdren's bedroom. A man named Charles Stewart rushed up into the house, along the corridor filled with blinding smoke , and up the stairway now tilled with the sparks end smoke from the storeroom, He gained the landing and found the young children at their bedroom door panic stricken, horrified and terrified, and fearing to descend the smoke darkened stair- way. At this moment the fierve heat, of the flames brought down the window sash into the Street with a crash, the flames rushed like a flood into the children's bedroo-u.and by their lurid light enabled people outside to see the scene on the landing. Stewart with commendable promptitude lifted up two of the smaller children and carried them down stairs ; deposited them safely in the street, and returned for the third child. The fire was now beginning to assume a more serious aspect, for the flames were leaping right across from the roofs 3f the bedrooms to within a few inches of the eaves of the houses across the narrow street, thus causing the inhabitants to prepare to remove their furni- ture. Then next door to the burning establishment is the pawnshop occupied by Mr. Mather, and there was an alarming danger of the conflagration reach- ing this place, in which case there would have been considerable damage done as the shop is at present heavily stocked. Eventually the fire brigade ender superintendent, Turner arrived, and by nearly three o'clock they had got the fire entire- ly under control. The roofs of two bedrooms and a storeroom were burned down, the doors of the rooms and £5O worth ot property destroyed, whilst the damage done to the building itself araounts to close upon £3OO. The house is the property of Mr. George Boardman,licensed victualler,of Orford-lane, and was insured to the extent of £2OO.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 12 1893 i SE RIOUS NFLAGRATION AT PRoPOSED LABOUR UNION FOR WAR- WARRINGTON. RINGTON. AN UNSKILLED LABOURERS CO.MI3INATION ALLEGATIONS AGAINST A public meeting was held in the Co-operative THE FIRE BRIGADE AND POLICE. Hall, Sankey-st., on Thursday night, to explain the principles ot the Tyneside and National Labour Union. There was but a small attendance. Mr. S. Wright J.P., presided, and he was supported by Messrs. Dipper (general isecretary), Mr. McKinley (St, Helens), T. Bullock, (secretary Trades and Labour Council), T. Malkin. The Chairman in opening the meeting said he felt somewhat disappointed in not seeing a better attendance to hear the principles of the Tyneside Union explained. All who had any connection with Warrington knew that the labourers were very much neglected here. The wages they earned were considerably below the average which was earned in other towns. He knew some of the labouring classes who were working for the iniserabl.. pittance of Hs. 10d. and 17s. 10d. per week. They - would all admit that that was not sufficient for any man to maintain his family. The object of the Tyneside Society was that these men should try to better there position. Hall the men in Warrington were united and organised so that one should not compete against the other they would get better terms from their employers. He thought that workmen should live in respectable houses, be able to clothe their children, and procure a lair education for them so that they should have oppor- tunities equal to those of others of. making their way into positions of trust and responsibility. He was informed that some of the worst property in Warrington was owned by members of the Town Council. It behoved the people to look to these matters when these men had to come to seek their suffrages. 'They should ask them what they intended to do and tell them that they wanted actions and not promises, as actions spoke louder than words. For some time a councillor had been advocating an advance in the Corporation labourers' wages in this town, but he had not been very successful. What did this mean ? When the officials of the corpora- tion asked for an advance, some for £lOO a year,,they lhould get it ; but labourers could not get Is. per week or Sas. a year advance. The society whose objects were to be explained that night, would take in these men as it was only intended for unskilled workmen. Mr. T. MALKIN expressed his pleasure at being at the meeting. 'They should not be afraid of ebstacles, as they would always find them in the way of every new movement. He noticed that the Hairdressers' Associa- tion recently formed was in the same position. Passing a certain street he saw a notice hung out of a doer" Hair cut for 2d. This shop open all day on Thursday." If that man wished to blackleg his own trade,. lie (the speaker) was not going to have blacklegs on his head. (Laughter.) Mr. MeKustar, of St. Helens, was the next. sneaker. He said that he had been chairman of the Miners' Asso- ciation of St.Helens,for the last 20 years. He contrasted the present ininers' strike with that which took place some years ago. The men were happier now, as they were thorouelily united. They had confidence in one another. This strike had not been brought on by the men ; the masters were really responsible for it. To show the injustice of the present system. Mr. McKinley said that while a man, Ws wife, and four children when they went into the union cost from 48s. to 50s to the ratepayers, yet, when the man was working he was expected to keep them on from 15s. to LI per week. Mr. DIPPER was now called upon to explain the ob- jects of the 'Tyneside and National Labour Society. Be said he would first explain why he was in Warrington, coming all the way from the Tyne. The reason was that in case of some dispute which might occur in their districts, no matter how well organised they were, they suffered from other counties and towns which were not organised. The union had frequently to pay almost as much money to keep men out of the town as to keep their own men when on strike. The executive council, therefore, determined to spend a portion of the money in missionary work so as to he prepared for future dis- putes. Referring to the United Alkali Company, who own most of the chemical works in Widnes and St. Helens, he said that they also owned all but one of the the chemical works on the Tyne, of which there were a dozen. This works had to compete with the company, and as they were a new company, they made the weeps as low as they possibly could. To accomplish this,triey had to go to Lancashire and the surrounding district for men. He was glad, however, to say that these men had joined the union and were even more enthusiastic than the older members, and were anxious to have their prin- ciples propagated in the places they came from. That was the cause of his canting to Warrington. He had been in St. Helens the previous night and was glad to say that there was a prospect of forming a union in that place. He might say that if a man was in a union they did not wish him to join theirs, they were satisfied if he was a trade unionist. Mr. Dipper then proceeded to give details of the working of the union, and mentioned that it could keep 750 out on strike for an indefinite period under ordinary conditions. A magic lantern entertainment was afterwards given, and the meeting closed with the usual vote of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>" BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</head>
        <p>" BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The meeting of Widnes Town Council on Tues- day was the shortest since the Corporation was formed. The business occupied not more than 50 minutes. At one time it appeared as though the Dr. (Ylieeffe incident would cause some feeling, and thus prolong the sitting, but in the end the matter was passed over calmly enough. Councillor Hodg- kinson asked a question with regard to the plumb- ers at the gasworks, in order to give Alderman Timmis an opportunity of correcting a statement made by Councillor Gough some time ago. Mr. Gough had stated that the men at the gasworks . were neither one thing nor another, and that the engineer had stated they had come to the works as mere labourers. lie therefore failed to see why they should be employed as plumbers and gasfitters. Alderman Timmis stated that the engineer denied this statement. The whole of the plumbers were properly qualified men who had served an appren- ticeship. There were labourers who had picked up pipe-fitting, but this was a common practice. Mr. Gough maintained his position, and he left it to the honour of the members to say whether the en- gineer had not made the statement attributed to him.</p>
        <p>It is an unfortunate thing that artisans should lie under the imputation of not being properly qualified for the work they have to perform and it is BO wonder that the men should complain ot the injustice. It is hardly a question of dispute between Mr. Gough and the engineer, it is a ques- tion of fact that should be easy of demonstration. After the latter's definite statement all doubts ought to be set at rest.</p>
        <p>By to-day it is expected that the manufacture of caustic soda will be totally stop?ed in Widnes, but the number of men idle is not so great as may be ex- pected. On Wednesday this number was about 400, but would have been a great deal more but for the fact that the Alkali Union are utilizing the slack time to repair their plant,and the men are kept on at this work as far as practicable. There is sufficient coal in stock to keep the bleaching powder plant at work for a month, but it is not certain that the supply of salt requisite can be kept up.</p>
        <p>The tor:heotning elect ion of Runcorn Board of Commissioners appears likely to be of more than usual interest if rumour is to be believed. It is stated that the Licensed Victuallers intend to run Mr. Thomas Jones, and Mr. Arthur Hough, and Mr. E. Imison who did good work on the Board some years ago, is als4 spoken of as a likely candidate. The retiring members are Mr. Samuel Taylor (chair- man) Mr. W. Fisher, Mr. R. Abel and Mr. E: W. Humphreys. There is a fifth place vacant through the resignation of Mr. P. Smith.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Derbyshire, Preston. son of the Rev Dr. Preston' vicar of holy Trinity Church, Run- corn, has been appointed assistant to Dr. Wrench, physician to the Duke of Devonshire, the Duke of Rutland, and other noblemen. He was selected to fill the position out of a large number of candi- dates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>TREAT TO OLD PEOPLE AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>TREAT TO OLD PEOPLE AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>When a local celebration of the marriage of the Duke and Duchess of York was resolved upon at Widnes, a hope was expressed that not only should the young people be provided with the means for a joyous few hours, but that the old people should be feasted. The former project was successfully car- ried and theto being a small surplus, the Committee decided that the latter should also be accomplished. An appeal for further subscriptions was satisfactorily responded to and the arrangements were placed in the hands of the following sub-committee :—Councillors F. Neil (chairman), Quinn. Shores, and Flodgkinson ; Messrs. D. J. Brookes' J. C. Crawford, J. W. Davies, A. Bennett, C. Flynn, B. Brown, S. Drought, and C. Emery. This Committee invited Messrs. F. Lawrinson and Super- intendent Keighley, who have had great experience in similar undertakings to assist them in the work. The clergy were also invited to assist by furnishing the names and addresses of all persons in their distrizts above the age limit fixed upon—fifty years. In order that the ut- most supervision might be maintained, and the tickets be placed in the hands of those intended to participate in the treat, thc following were appointed ticket distribu- tors: Victoria Ward, Councillor Quinn and Mr. Brookes ; Farnworth Ward, Messrs. Davies and Drought; West Rank Ward, Mr. Emery and Councillor Flodgkinson ; Waterloo Ward, Messrs. Crawford and Flynn; Simms Cross Ward, Councillor Davies and Mr. Warner ; Halton Ward, Councillor F. Neil and Mr. A. Bennett. The Sub- committee was divided into three sections. Councillors Hodgkinson and Quinn and Messrs. Lawrinsou and Flynn were deputed to arrange for the supply of pro- visions and to organise the catering; and the duty of providing tables, crockery. &amp;c., was deputed to Coun- cillors F. Neil and Davies, Superintendent Keighley and Mr. Bennett; while Councillor G. I. Neil and Messrs. Booth, Brookes, and Emery undertook to provide a tnu-ical and miscellaneous entertainment. Mr. B. S. Oppenheim, the town clerk, acted as clerk to the Com- mittee and he was ably assisted by Mr. T. Craik. That these gentlemen had performed their tasks well was seen 0% Thursday evening, when nearly 800 men and women whose ages ranged from 50 to 70 years, were comfort- ably seated in the Market Hall enjoying a capital tea of ham and beef sandwiches, tarts, and fancy breads. The tables were covered with a substitute for cloths, in the shape of white paper, supplied by Messrs.McCorquo- dale, of Newton-le-Willows. The room had been made gay with flags,and presented a light, bright, and cheer- ful appearance. Mr. D. J.Brookes had exercised his ar- tistic taste in the decoration of the temporary platform. The background was a scene of Runcorn Bridge, and flowering plants lent by Mr. H. Wade-Deacon, J.P.,were effectively arranged. The tables were presided over by the knowing ladies :—Mesdames W. P. Mitchell, Wil- liams, Palin, B. Brown; Imison, Eawrenson, Drought, Hough, Singleton, C. Flynn, flufton, A. J. Williams, Crawford, E. Smith, Grounds Christie, R. Showan, 1 Shores, Bown, Peet, Charles Whitfield. O'Donnell, Swin- dells, J. Davies, T. Roberts,Wright, Carney, A.J.Loines, Joseph Smith, Hodgkinson' Brookes, Riley, Owen, Ware- ing, Gough, G. I. Neil, A. Johnson, Patten, R. Devlin, O'Keeffe, Lowe, Wm. Wilkinson, E. Booth, Carroll,John- son, Hough, Edwardson, Hughes, Oppenheim, Potter, and Warner •, Misses Timmins,Wright, Colquitt,Shuttle- worth, and Craik. After justice had been done to the good things provided, the room was quickly cleared of the tables, and arrangedfor the entertainment. The Mayor (Alderman F. H. Gossage), who was accom- panied by the Ms voress, presided, and he Was supported by Mr. B. Wade-Deacon, Mrs. Deacon, the Misses Dea- con, Mr. and Mrs. H. S. Oppenheim, Mrs. White, Mrs. G. I. Neil, and others.</p>
        <p>_ The Mayor who wore his chain of office, was heartily greeted on rising. He hoped the old people had had as good entertainment as the young people had on the day of the marriage, when, he thought, the Widnes people displayed their loyalty in a becoming manner. There was no socialist and no democrat in that room who would wish to do away with the Royal Family as long as it conducted itself to the satisfaction of the nation as our great and good Queen had,done, and as he was quite sure the Prince and Princess of \Vales would do when they sat upon the throne. They had to congratulate themselves upon one point, and that was that an English Prince had taken to wife an English born princess. (Applause.) He pointed out that that was the first occasion on which he had been allowed to wear the Mayor's chain and badge—(applause)—and he expressed the hope that each succeeding mayor, who wore that chain, 'would be superior tA his predecessor. (Applause.) Mrs. It. White gave a pianoforte solo, Miss L. White two violin solos, Councillor G. I. Neil, a couple of humor- ous songs, Mr. A. G. Davies, three comic sketches, and Mr. J. Paton, also contributed comic songs. Professor Wyman gave an exhibition of shadowgraphy. During the interval Councillor Davies proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress for their presence that even- ing and Mr. Deacon seconded the motion, which was carried by acclamation and the singing of "For he is a jolly good fellow."—ln responding, the Mayor proposed a vote of thanks to the ladies who had presided at the tea tables and to the entertainers.—The Rev. W. Hod.-- son seconded the proposition which was heart* carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>LEAPING FROM AN EXPRESS TRAIN.</head>
        <p>LEAPING FROM AN EXPRESS TRAIN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, a lad named Hugh Joel Morris, residing at 6,Grenfell-st.,Whines,had a marvellous escape from beinekillcdOn the railway at Ditton. It appears that the lad was sent on an errand to Ditton by his mother. He obtained a ticket from the Widnes. booking office, and entered the 5.5. p.m. express from Widnes to Liver- pool, udder the impression that it was a local train,which was due at 5.23 p.m. When he found that the train did not stop at Ditten, the foolish boy opened the carriage door, and jumped nut, alighting on his head on a heap of slack. The incident was witnessed by one of she porters, who at Once ran to the spot fully expecting to find the lad dead. Fortunately, he was only stunnel. and alter a short time was able to talk. He was con- veyed home in an ambulance, and he received surgical attention. It was discovered that his head and face were bruised, and one eye almost knocked out. The latest report is to the effect that he is progressing as well as can be expected.</p>
        <p>Yesterday morning an " Examiner" reporter went to the scene of the fire and climbed into the bedrooms where he describes everything as being in a charred and ruined condition ; the burned and blackened beams of the roof lying amongst the frizzled flocks of the beds ; and articles of clothing blackened by embers, saturated by water, and trampled on till they are beyond recognition. He saw Mr. Bennett who, had sustained rather serious injuries to the hands, and in a conversation Mr. Bennett said :—I only came into the shop a little over three weeks before this fire. Strange to say I formerly occupied it for three years, and then left and went to Hall-street, and it seems an unfor- tunate stroke of business that I should come back again to have the home burned down in a fort- night." • " Were you satisfied with the work of the fire brigade." " Well perhaps they did their best, but I gave the alarm at one o'clock and I can say without a doubt that it was nearly two o'clock when they got here. There is another thing which I complain of. When I was coming back from the fire bell.l met a police- man and after giving him facts about the fire he refused to allow me to go away and detained me for half-an-hour despite the fact that I did not know bow my family and house were faring, and was anxious to get beck to render assistance.' The reporter next called upon Superintendent Turner and asked how the Fire Brigade were so late. The Superintendent said : "It is all a mis- take. We received the alarm at exactly ten minutes to two, and I can assure you that at five minutes to two we were out and approaching the fire. In less than ten minutes we were playing on the flames. The time is entered on the slate and my assistant will verify it." The superintendent's as- sistant came forward and corroborated his superior's statement,and appeared indignant at the "reflection " cast on the brigade..</p>
        <p>_ Next the reporter called upon the Chief Constable with respect to the allegation made against the policeman. The Chief Constable said :--" I have received no complaint from Mr. Bennett, and have heard of nothing of the matter you refer to. Even if what you say is true,' think it would have been done with the best intention. The constable is probably a stranger, and as the police and the bri- gade often get hoaxed by people ringing the bell, he simply did his duty by waiting till the alarm was verified. It was a natural thing for a con- stable to do under the circumstances ; and I have no doubt Mr. Bennett would feel annoyed, but authorities must work by system." Further investigations reveal the fact . that the fire broke out near the oven in the bakehouse, and was probably caused by some bits of straw, which had dropped from the storeroom becoming ignited. Much sym- pathy is felt in the neighbourhood for Mr. Bennett, whose loss in chattels and business will be consider- able.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>AMERICAN TRIVIALITIES.</head>
        <p>AMERICAN TRIVIALITIES.</p>
        <p>American society is agitated over the great question whether the Earl of Craven, on his wedding day, did or did not wear his trousers turned up. In order to relieve the public mind, a prominent newspaper sent a repre- settative to Ward McAllister, the great authority on social matters, and thiserninent person has given it as his opinion that the Earl did not intend to go through the ceremony with his tiousers turned up, but simply forgot to turn them down when he arrived at the church. Columns appear on the subject in the papers, and the persons who really saw the Earl's trousers turned up in Grace Church are perpetually being applied to by those who are anxious to have their evidence at first hand.be- ing unwilling to depend on hearsay in such a moment- ous matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LABOUR'S PUOGRESS IN AUSTRIA.</head>
        <p>LABOUR'S PUOGRESS IN AUSTRIA.</p>
        <p>The Labour Commission which has been sitting for nearly a month at the Austrian Board of Trade has taken the evidence of a number of experts. Foremost among them was Adelhaid Dworzlk, the female Socialist leader of Vienna. The Minister o Commerce was pre- sent and encouraged Fraulein Dworzak to speak freely of the position of the working women in this country. The young woman spoke for three hours and described all the horrors of the working wernan's position. She showed how the laws were not kept; how night work, which is strictly forbidden. was dmne by the wo:nen being given work to do t their homes at night after their day's work at thq factory ;how employers managed to learn of the impending visit of a Government inspec- tor ; how the women dared not complAin when the in- spector was there for fear of dismissal; how they were made to sign papers, in which they promised to attend• no'ineetings ; how girls of fourteen were made to stand ten and twelve hours near hot ovens in certain trades ; how others fainted from exhaustion while they were at work ; how at certain work their teeth fell out, and their eyes suffered—and all for wages which varied from three to six shillings a week. This is the first time that a • woman has ever been heard officially upon any subject in .11.15tria.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>SEQUEL TO THE FATAL ACCIDENT. IN</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO THE FATAL ACCIDENT. IN</p>
        <p>BEWSEY-ST REET.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Friday, Mary Hanlon, 8, Halifax-street, was charged with assaulting and beating Ellen Marratt, 17, Foundry-street,. on the 27th ult. ; and Kate Smith, Bakehouse-yard was charged with using threats to Ellen Marratt on the same date. Joseph Marratt was also charged with assaulting Hanlon. Mr. Percy Davies appeared tor complainant and Mr. Arthur Browne for defendants.—Mr. Davies said that a short time ago an inquest was held in the parade room of the police station, upon the body of a boy who had been killed by being run over. Mr. Browne at the inquest had represented Mr. roole, whose son was sup- posed to have run over the bay. Mr. Plinston was also said to have run over the boy. Mrs. Marratt had been summoned by the polite to appear at the parade room as she was prepared to say that she had seen Mr. Toole's son crying after the accident had occurred, and saying that a child had been tun over. Mrs. Marratt had no feeling either for Plinston or Toole, but it was through going to the inquest that the assault complained of took place. She lives 'in the district where Mr. Toole's ser- vants work. The inquest took place on Monday, July 25th, and on that night Mrs. Smith and some other woman in the employ of Mr. Toole, accused Mrs. Marratt of forswearing herself at the inquest. On Tues- day Mrs. Smith knocked out all the hooks upon which Mrs. Marratt's clothes line was fastened, and told her it was because she had forsworn herself, and she must hang her clothes on Mr. Plinston's line. On Wednesday, Smith brought the hooks back, and used some very abusive language. Mrs. Marraq said that the hooks were no use now, and shut the door. Mrs. Smith went to the back door with them and used some threats. Mrs. Hanlon who was near then rushed up with a jug in her hand, and pulled Mrs. Marratt out of the house, and threatened to split her skull with the jug. Mrs. Marratt's arm was made black and blue by Mrs. Hanlon, and Mr. Marratt who was in bed came down and pushed the woman away. Mrs. Marratt then went fora police- man, and was met again by Mrs. Hanlon who again threatened licr with the jug. He cMr. Davies) thought it was very unfortunate that a woman called by the police should be interfered with in this manner.—Mrs. Marratt and a large number of witnes-ea were called by Mr. Davies in corroboration.—Mr. Browne said that the threats complained of were very trivial. Mrs. Smith was not employed by Mr. Toale, but Mrs. Marratt's clothes were darkening her kitchen, and she complained to the landlord, who told her to pull out the hooks. She acc.rdingly did so Mit it afterwards turned out tnat Mrs. Marratt had received permission to put them on the wall and accordingly Mrs. Smith took them back. Mrs. Marratt told her that she would have her arrested for stealing the hooks. Mrs Hanlon also denied assaulting Mrs:lslarratt,but had summoned Mr. Marratt for assault. He was the worse for drink and called her some bad names as she passed the !muse. She went to bins to ask what he meant when he pushed her on the ground and</p>
        <p>~.)mmenced kicking her and hit her with- a scraper. His behaviour was so scandalnus that a man who was in charge of a limy get off and told Marratt that he would thrash him if he did not desist. He was then taken into the house.—A number of witnesses were called in sup- port of Mr. Browne's statement during which Mr. Browne drew a ttention to several intrjections of Alder- man Harrison who with great warmth said that he pro- tested against Mr. Browne's interference. He expected that he would not do it again. He had no right to interfere.—The magistrates dismissed the case against Kate Smith and Joseph Marratt. Hanlon was fined L 1 including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>AN INTERIZUPIED WEDDING.</head>
        <p>AN INTERIZUPIED WEDDING.</p>
        <p>A marriage ceremony was interrupted at St. Denis the other day in a singutar manner. The bride, in her white dress and wreath of orange blossoms, was escorted to the Town Hall, where the public official whom we call the Reeistrar was in attendance, with his tri- coloured scarf of office reund his waist. Presently- the bridegroom appeared with his friends, and the formali- ties proper to the occasion were begun. AL the moment when the young lady was asked whether she consented to take the y. mug man for her husband, the Registrar uttered a cry of surprise. He was casting his rye over the bride's certificate of birth, and he there discovered that, through some strange mistake that had never been rectified, she had twen registered as a boy, although her name WaS correctly given as Eliza. Webert. The dis- covery put an end tma the proceedings, much to time dis- coinliiure of all concerned. There will be some little delay before Nhillep,Webert will be able to settle in proper legal form the question as to her sex.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>CLERGY DAUGHTERS SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE CLERGY DAUGHTERS SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The three days sale of work in aid of the funds of the above deserving irts'itntion was concluded on Saturday. The total amount realized was alma 70, so that the school is still about £730 in debt.</p>
        <p>THE FOX INN, WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>One of the oldest and most interesting buildings extant in the oldest town in Lancashire, the Fox Inn, tuttermarket-street, Warrington, has, says the Liverpool " Weekly Post "within the past few days ceased to exist as an Inn, and now stands shivering on the brink of an uncertain future. Around that curious antique pile, many memories twine. The origin of the " Fox "is veiled in the dark shroud of antiquity. The date 1,110 was cut out in the window woodwork, and an inscription on the doorpost is dated 1,704,but neither of these dates can be relied upon. Its structure would indicate that it W4i youtig in the days of the later Tudors, in which days, according to Leland's " Itinerary " (Henry VIII.'s time), Warrington i was" a pavid towne of a prety bignes. The patroch chirch sat the rayle of the towne, it is a better market than Man- : chestre." Situated between " paroclechirch " the market and close to the latter, the Fox Inn was on the highway in the very thie.kest of the bustling Warrington of that day. It had never been a pot house. Its accommoda- tion must have boen considered very grand in those primitive days.. The rooms are of course low-roofed, but in size they compare favourably with the modern ideas. In common with other hostelries, the utmost en- deavour had been made to make the most of everything with nooks and corners, curious angles, and narrowest possible of passages, but once inside with a good log on the open fireplace, a cheery host, and a mug of the even then celebrated Warrington ale, tillers. M the soil, the salmon fishers from the river, the linen and sailcloth weavers could find no cosier place than the Fox par- lour in which to tell their tales, discuss their grievances, and recount the stirring events of the day. With seventy stagecoaches passing by the door, and many of the paFsengers putting us, at the Fox, there would be no latir of news. But to see the Fox in all the glory of its noonday splendour, seer it when the markets were on, which out-Manchester ed Manchester. The one began on the 18th July and the other on the 30th of November, and both lasted ten days. The shadowy relics oh those ancient fairs are still held on these dates. The women, dressed in clean mutches, and in their Sun- day best, occupied Buttermarket-et. with their kegs of butter and the barging and bargaining went on under the very windows of the For. The horse fair in Horsemarket-street near to, and the grunters on Pighill opposite, contributed a large share of the hubbub and clatter that went on. The Cloth Flail- yard was equally patronised with the cloth dealers, ' the fame of the Warrington stuff bringing buyers from afar. The holiday element was a conspicuous feature, especially when business was over. Could these walls but speak, could these yet fregh o then refters hut lay bare their secrets, curious indeed would their story be. The bustle of the fairs over and gone, the doings of the big folk at the hall, the latest culprit at the stocks, and the next wife to wear the scold's cap, were common topics of conversation within the cosy den. But it was not always peace within its walls, for these•sairte win- dows looked down on Cromwell and his Ironsides, saw too, Scotland's Bonnie Prince Charlie come ttegrief when the Liverpool Blues intercepted him by cutting down Warrington Bridge in 1745. Wars of revolution and political and social turmoil swept over the nation, but the Fox weathered them all. I Not far from its site the first paper published in Lancashire " Eyre's Warrington Advertiserr ate, was printed, and doubtless the early copies were read in the Fox parlour by the few who could to othe many who could not, amidst the unbounded delight and %vender- ment of the company.- The house is a typical one. The tiny window, about twelve inches by four wide,, which deeps round the gable corners and commands a ViTW of Sankey-st., the peculiar features-of its own. One of the numerous stories of tht place is that Cromwell from this window watched the manceuveesed his troops as far as Bewsew Hall. The present owners ot the FOxl Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., breware, have • given' up the license along with others in rettrn ,for,two newt licenses in less congested parts of the towny.thiepitice is -now" to let."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SEVERE': THUNDERSTORMS:.</head>
        <p>SEVERE': THUNDERSTORMS:.</p>
        <p>The excessive heat on Wednesday was followed dur- ing the night-at Warrington by a violent thunderstorm. It commenced about one o'clock, and for fully an hour the lightning was almost incessant, and the accompany- ing peals of thunder were of unusual intensity. The rain for a short time was MK, very heavy. No damage has been reported. At Blackburn on Thursday morning, the lightning was appallin.z.,in its vividness, and the greatest alhrin prevailed. By a final thalt which was worse than all the rest two houses• were struck, one in Holland-st,„ of which a famiiy named Hinds are tenants, and which was badly damaged. Fortunately the occupants left on Wednesday for their holidays. In the second case the house was occupied by the family of Mr. Flairsw@ll, 1 goods manager, Lancashire and Yorkshire Railway. The flash passed through a room in which Mr. and Mrs. Ilauxwell were sleeping, bringing down the chimney and ;mashing wind,4ws and doing other damage. Early on Thursday morning, a terrific thunderstorm passed over Birmingham. The lightning was of extra- ordinary brilliancy, and the flashes followed each other so quickly, that no less than 71 were counted in one minute. During the progress of the storm a talloress living at Ilandsworth died from fright. Great damage is reported in the city. Four cows and a pony were killed by lightning on Wednesday night at Ashby-de-la-Zouch. The lightning did damage to farm premises at Dimmow. Essex, and Lynn, Norfolk. At Devizes this belfry of the Mortuary Chapel in Devizes Cemetery was struck by lightning and nearly demolished. For 80 minutes thunder was almost continuous, and was accompanied by vivid light fling. &amp; terrific storm which swept over Burnley late on Thursday evening was yesterday morning found to have wrought extensive damage in all parts of the town.. In Manchester-road, where the water rolled down the thoroughfare, a yard deep, garden walls were washed away, drains were broken, and heavy portions of the roadway torn up to a depth of several feet. Several sheds were flooded, and hundreds of houses. An extraordinary fatality through Wednesday night's thunderstorm is reported from Talk, North Stafford- shire. Mrs. Kettle awoke in the night and found the bed in the next room on tire through the lightning,and her daughter, aged ten, dead and fearfully searched from head to foot. She also found that the lightning had struck the bed on which she and her husband were sleeping, luckily however, doing no further damage:than singing the p'an's hair, which presented the appearance of being covered with hoar frost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>SOLDIER'S FEET..</head>
        <p>THE SOLDIER'S FEET..</p>
        <p>There are two things which the German officer does not and cannot condone—one is non-efficiency of the soldier's rifle, the other a chafed foot. If either of these two takes place on the. march or during the manoeuvres the soldier is immediately punished with arrest and is not Allowed to °fisr any excuse. During the different nianceuvres of the German army corps that, says a writer, I have attenoled I cannot recall a handful of foot sore men in the course of a day's work, and yet at all these field operations forced marches are a feature in order to test the endurance of officers and men. The secret of this uniform excellence as regards marching powers lies in the training which the men receive, When they enter their company as recruits in October the first thing that is impressed upon their minds is the import- ance of the shoe and the musket. No pains are spared in giving the men at the start comfortable•footgear, and they are expected to look after this with as much in- terest as if it were a chronometer. In the spying follow- ing, when the snow is off the ground', marches are undertaken, and these are regulated as carefully as are the strokes and courses of the college crew under the hands of the trainer. Each day the men march half-a- mile or so further than the day before;! each day they carry on their backs an ounce or two more,each day the speed they are ahle to maintain is carefully noted—in fact the record of a company's marching. from day to day until late into the summer, when they move into the open country, is kept as minutely as if it were a single picked company training for , a march, oc competitive. ' drill. The German soldier is educated and trained for the purpose sf fighting, and to have a man fall out before he reaches the fire line is looked upon as quite as much a disaster as if he had been shot and wounded by the. enemy. The art of war, as practised in Germany, is very much the art of "getting there," and it is the general who posts himself most advantageously at the , critical moment who may be assumed to have won the I battle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SOMERSET TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>THE SOMERSET TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>Charles Squires, 28, labourer, was executed on Thurs- day morning at Snepton Mallet, Somerset. for child murder. The child's cries awoke the prisoner at mid- night, and he suffocated it by holding his hand over its mouth and nostrils, afterwards returning to bed, and in the morning he asked his wife to say she had overlaid it. Billington was the executioner. Reporters were excluded from the prison. The convict slept frorp twelve to five, and then had a light breakfast, consisting of tea and bread and butter. He walked firmly and unpinioned from the cell to the place of execution. When a few Yards from the scaffold the prisoner shook hands with all the officials and thanked them for the kindraess with which he had been treated. As the cap was being ad- lusted he ejaculated, " Lord Jesus receive my spirit." Death appeared to be instantaneous. 'nip prisoner had previously made a full confession of his crime. At the trial at Wells ASSiZRS S,quire's demeanour was very callous, but since his conviction he has been very atten- tive to the ehaplin, and he died penitent. He Wrote to his wife asking her forgiveness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>HE WAS FELLING A TREE.</head>
        <p>HE WAS FELLING A TREE.</p>
        <p>An accident ecurrtd to John Richardson, one of the gardeners of OrfordlloiH, the other day, whilst fellinq a tree in the grounds. In aiming a blow at the tree the axe lie was using slipped and came in contact with his right foct, nearly severing the large toe. Ile is progressing favourably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Holiday times do not appear to be the best times for the meeting ot Boards of Guardians and other authorities. On Wednesday it only occupied twenty- five minutes to transact the whole of tha business of the Warrington Guardians, and there were few Guardians present. At Button workhouse,also, in which the Runcorn Rural Sanitary Authority and Runcorn Board of Guardians meet;almost a similar state of affairs occurred. At the Rural Sanitary I Authority, for the greater portion of the meeting, there were only the chairman and three members present, and the Board of Guardians meeting was also very sparsely attended. It seems that at holi- day times even the crave and reverend signors wl.o. compose our local authorities do not disdain to relax their mighty " intellecks."</p>
        <p>The P.S.A. movement, which may be said to have " caught on-" in Warrington and elsewhere, has undergone a considerable amount of criticism from certain quarters and the following will show how they can be" manufactured "to deceive the public, and " A letter appeared in the " Manchester Courier" on July 4th, which was signed A Man- chester Reporter,' and purported to give an account of. a P.S.A. meeting at Oldham. According to the writer, the gallery he entered was crowded with noisy boys and girls, foul smoke and foul lan- guage were general around him, and devotion was conspicuous by its absence throughout the service. even the organist forgetting himself so far as to play a number of music-hall airs. The letter created a great sensation, following as it did upon the Bishop of Manchester's fulminations against the movement, and the charge was taken up by Mr. John W. Dent, the president of the Oldham Society, and thoroughly investigated. It transpired that the writer of the letter was not a Manchester re- porter; that he was not present at the service he pretended to describe ; and that, as he has had to admit in an apology which has been attested by a solicitor, and published in the " Courier "all his statements were absolutely false, and his own imagination was the only source from which he ob- tained material far his story. He adds that he wrote in a humorous spirit, and without intention or injuring or annoying anyone." But was the writer not too humorous when he described a meeting for men only,crowded with noisy boys and girls ?</p>
        <p>The meeting of the Tyneside and National Labour Union on Thursday night, at the Co-operative Hall was not a very great success. Trades unionism is without doubtsa very desirable thing, but it does not appear to "take on " with the unskilled work- men of the town. Quite a number of new unions hi.ve been proposed, but so far, none of them have been successful in establishing themselves in the town. Perhaps success may come in time by stick- ing at it. but from every appearance it will not for the present. Mr. S. Wright J.P., made a very good speech at the meeting in which be called attention to the increases of salaries to the corporation officials while, at the same time the labourers are kept down to their lowest level. Ile urged organi- sation of the labourer, as a means of raising the wages. The difficulty lies in getting the labourer organised. That this is not impossible was shown by Mr. Dipper the secretary of the society who told of the success of the society in Northumberland. This is encouragement for the local leaders to ke-p pegging away although the average Warrington "unskilled ',hand is so hard to were.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lahouchere in his pillory in "Truth" has recently referred to several cases tried by magis- trates in this district, and the unequal justice meted out. Two cases which were tried at the Warring- ton Borough Police Court on Wednesday, might furnish him with material for further criticism. Stephen Collins a well known chalacter to the police was charged with committing a most brutal assault on a woman. And speaking of assaults on women reminds me that such cases are increasing at an alarming rate in Warrington. The prisoner in this case threw the woman to the ground, broke her shoulder blade and kicked her several times on the ground. For this, the magistrates sent him to gaol for three months' hard labour. The next ease tried was the theft of Gra. 44. from the barracks by one of the privates named Graham who received exactly the same pnnishment—three months' hard ! Does it not seem a little inconsistent, when it is considered that at the same court a few days ago, the same penalty was inflicted on a trades union secretary, , who embezzled over £72.</p>
        <p>I am glad to learn that the new baths have been prosperous beyond the anticipations of the most sanguine members of the Corporation. I have it from a good source that the increased receipts will be more than sufficient to pay the interest on the capital outlay, in vvtich case I humbly sug- gest that our civic fathers encourage the further prosperity of the institution by providing Turkish baths They are about the only things which the baths sequins now to make them as near perfection as can be reasonably expected.</p>
        <p>The Rural Sanitary Authority will not be in the same position respecting the pollution of Sankey Brook. They will have to get the pollution stopped, and quickly too. St.Helens has already been ordered to deal with its sewage as it formerly turned it into Sankey Brook. That the Act is made to he carried out may be seen from the fact that recently no fewer than ?lose upon 40 local bodies have been prosecuted,and in many cases orders have been made for dealing with the sewage. The sooner the Act is put into force in this neighbourhood the better, for we are not without our share of filthy streams. And I hope no time will be lost in dealing with the upper parts of the Mersey.</p>
        <p>Here is something acceptable to those persons— and I presume there are a great many—who are in- terested in preserving the purity of our streams. The Local Government Board have just issued to all local authorities throughout the kingdom circu- lars calling attention to the Rivers Pollution Pre- vention Act of 1892, which has just received the Royal assent. The Act is really of one section, and has been passed to cure a defect in tke Act of 1876. and it now renders liable to proceedings any local authority which causes or allows to flow into any stream solid or liquid sewage, or if such authority allows or is aware of any person thus pilluting a streams, it is still liable to proceedings, and is thus held solely responsible for the condition of the streams in its area. As regards Warrington I leant that the Act does not apply to tidal water, until the Local Government Board gives a warning to the contrarv,This,l take it.is in consequonce of the stand which Warrington has often taken respecting the pollutionoof the Mersey. Our Corporation has offered to deal with its sewage when the sewage above the town is dealt with. Nothing fairer could be ex- pected.</p>
        <p>MERLIN.</p>
        <p>LM M. VOLUNTEER SHOOTING.—The shooting competition for the Challenge Cup of the H Comnany (Lymtn), 3rd V.8.C.11., took place on Monday at Cheadle. The prize was won by Sergeant Rogerson, with a score of 55. The cup has to be won three times to become the property of the holder. LAWN TENNIS PLATERS.—The Lytnm Lawn Tennis Club spent Monday afternoon on the club ground near to the Grammar School, and afterwards adjourned to the Conservative Club, where a canital supper was served. Dancing was afterwards indulged in, and the party broke up about one o'clock the following morning, . Several ladies and gentlemen from Warrington and dis- trict were present during the evening. BANK HOLIDAT.—Bank holiday has now become thor- oughly established in Lvmm, all the shops being closed during the day. The village was well patronised by visitors, excursion trains having been run from, as vr,ll as to. Manchester and Liverpo.d. A vast number of • conveyances also brought their quota to swell the crowd. The visitors were taken altogether, orderly, and well- behaved, and the police have received no complaints from any quarter. ACCIDENT TO A LYS= Youru.—A youth named Wm - Hinton, in the employ of Mr. John Winstanley, Dairy Farm, Lyintn, was in charge of a horse and trap, whicl. had taken aime material to Grimditch for a Lynnu builder. With a lad's curiosity he went to look down a deep well which Was being made, when from some un- explained cause his horse bolted. The youth jumped to its head but the bridle gave way, and he was thr,wrt to the ground. The wheel of the trap. which is a heavy (00, pissed over his foot, and hilt for his presonce of mind would have gone over his head. Ho was at once removed to the Warrington Infirmary..</p>
        <p>MUSIC IN TELE PARTS. The Volunteer band will to-morrow (Sunday), under the leadership of Mr. T. Armstrong, conduc- tor, render the following programme z--Choros, " Let their Celestial Conc2rt Handel ; selection, " La Fete Musical" Smith; anthem. "Turn thy face from my sins " Attwood ; selection, Tourney- merit " Round ; selection, from Messiah, Handel ; Grand March, Military, Ittlea,3;: Goa szje. thft Queen.</p>
      </div>
