      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>SEQUEL TO TI R SPORTS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Tuesday morn- ing, James Smith last from Bolton. was charged with playing at " chance" off Wilderspool-road, at four o'clock on Monday afternoon. P.S. Cheeney said he saw the prisoner on the football field playing with box and dice for money, on the occasion of the fostball sports. Prisoner who pleaded guilty was fined 20s. and costs or one month's imprisonment. The dice and dice- box were forfeited by the police. TAKING LIBERTIF.S IN ANOTHER WOMAN'S HOUSE. Elizabeth Charnock was charged at the Warrington Borough Court on Monday with being drunk and dis- orderly in Bakehouse-yard on Saturday night, and also with committing an aggravated assault upon Elizabeth Emery on the same date. From the evidence it ap- peared that prisoner went into Mrs. Emery's house on Saturday night the worse for drink. She was an entire stranger to Mrs.Emery,but she immediately sat down on the bed which was in the kitchen, and inquired tor a former tenant of the house. Mrs. Emery told her that the person to whom she referred did not live in the house.and also requested her to leave. Prisoner refused, and on a second request being made to her, she picked up a glass from the dresser and threw it at Mrs. Emery. The glass broke on Mrs. Emery's head and severely cut it. A constable came up soon afterwards and took prisoner in- to custody.—Prisoner said that she never remembered going into the house, although when she came out, she had a black eye.—The magistrates inflicted a penalty of 161 s. and costs or 14 days' hard labour. RIFLING SOLDIERS' POCKETS. John Graham, a soldier in the Liverpool regiment,was charged at the Borough Court on Wednesday, with stealing from a room at Orford Barracks a sum of money 63. 4d., the property of James Little, James Allman, Thomas Potter, and William King, at the Barracks. Prisoner pleaded not guilty. The Chief Constable stated that the complainants went to bed leaving their money in their pockets. When they got up the next morning they found it had gone, and the matter was reported to Corporal Wilcocks, and two other privates told the Corporal they had seen the prisoner during the night- time putting his hands in some of the men's pockets. The prisoner was then locked in the guard-room, but handed over very stealthily 5. to Private Bell to keep until he came out of the guard-room. Evidence was given by Little, Allman, and King as to their money being taken. George Perrin and James Bell spoke to seeing the prisoner rifle the pockets. After evidence by Corporal Wilcocks and P.C. Chrimes prisoner was sent to gaol for three months' hard labour. Masa v. BICYCLE.. • • • •</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night,- the Ist August, two youths in the employ ef Mr.flawthorne, provision dealer. Winwick-st., had determined to have a race, one of them riding on horseback and the other on a bicycle. The rider of the bicycle was Harry Lightfoot, of 28, IVinwick-street, and Walter Houghton residing at 8, Chorley-street, was on horse-back. They seemed to be quite oblivious of the fact that racing was not allowed in public streets, and were pursuing their course along Orford-lane, at eight o'clock, the horse at full gallop and the youth with the bicycle geing at full speed vhen they were seen by D. S. Monaghan and also by Mr. Taylor (P.C.M.) They com- pleted their race and decided the bicycle was the best racer. But there was stilt another little affair for them to decide and this was settled at the Borough Police Court on Saturday morning, when they were both placed in the dock charged with riding and driving furi- ously. Mr. Taylor said they would be going between 12 and 18 miles an hour when he saw them run the length of Orford-lane. D. S. Monaghan also gave evidence. Defendants who pleaded guilty were each fined 53. and - costs.</p>
        <p>SAVAGE ASSAULT ON A WOMAN. At the Borough Court on Wednesday, Patrick Collins and Stephen Collins were charged with assaulting Nancy Heath at Friars' Green, on Monday night. Mr. P. Davies appeared for the Collins's. Patrick Collins, was liberated on the complainant saying that it was Stephen Collins who assaulted her. Stephen Collins pleaded guilty. Nancy Heath said on Monday night between 7.30 and 8.30 she saw three men kicking another RUM named Hanlon on the floor, and she went to them and said" don't kill him ." Stephen Collins then got hold of her and threw her on the ground breaking her shoulder blade, and kicked her several times. Complainant ad- mitted having had two glasses of gin. Thomas Heath said he saw a man named Hanlon sitting outside on Friars' Green, when the prisoner and two others went behind Hanlon and struck him. He also corroborated Mrs. Heath's statement of the assault. Mr. Davies said the man Patrick Hanlon had been calling Collin's mother some foul names, and the prisoner struck him for it and there was afterwards a hand-to-hand fight with the prisoner and Hanlon. The woman Heath came out of the house the worse for drink and commenced using foul language which exasperated him, and he admitted assaulting her tor hell- interference. The magistrates con- sidered it a bad case and sent the prisoner to gaol for three months with hard labour. THE TRICKSTER CAUGHT. At the Warrington' Borough Court on Thursday, Richard Lowe was charged with stealing by means of a trick from William Parkinson, bay and straw dealer, Buttermarket-st., a truss of hay valued Is. Frcm the I evidence of the Cbh-f Constable it appeared that pris- oner went in the complainant's shop and asked to be supplied with a truss of hay for his father, Mr. George Lowe, who was a coal dealer living in Brick-st. Mr. Parkinson hesitated giving him the truss as he had no money, when Martin Lyons came in and said it would be all right as the man's father had plenty of money. The truss was weighed and given to the prisoner, and Mr. Parkinson sent his shop boy to follow the prisoner, when he found that the prisoner took the hay to Daley's lodging house, and asked Mrs. Daley to give him 2s. for it. Mrs. Daley told him to wait until Mr. Daley 11 came, and in the rneantisnethe police had been informed I by Mr. Parkinson. The prisoner was being brought to the police station when he slipped up James-st., but was recaptured. After evidence by Mr. Parkinson, and 1 George Lowe, father of the prisoner, who said he had I not sent for any hay,the prisoner was sent to gaol for six weeks with hard jabour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MINOIt CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOIt CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. SATURDAY.—Before Mr. John Burgess (in the chair). Alderman Chandley,and Mr. Lewis Voisey..</p>
        <p>BEGGING.—Thomas Wright, last from Liverpool, was charged with begging in Bridge-st. D.S.Monaghan said he saw the prisoner going from shop to shop. .The magistrates sent the prisoner to gaol for seven days' hard labour. DRUNK.—Kate Ann Jones,B, Ashton-t., was fined ss. and costs for being drunk in Winwick-st., on the 30th ult.—Sarah Jane Whitehouse, 7, Arnold's-yard, for a similar offence, in Haydack-st., was fined ss. and costs, or seven days. FIGHTING. Thomas Warburton, Duckley-st., and Edward Burke, 13, Pinner's-brow, were charged with fighting in Winwick-st. They were each bound over to keep the mace for six mouths, in sureties of £5, or in default 14 days' hard labour. STONE THROWING.—John Cooper, aged 10, residing at 2, Gsskell-street, was charged with throwing stones at a gas lampin Battersby-lene, on the Ist inst. He was dis- missed with a caution.</p>
        <p>GREENGROCERS IN TROUBLE.—Thomas Ellison, green- grocer, 23, Percy-St., was charged with allowing a donkey and cart to stand an unnecessary time in Market-st., on the 2nd inst. P.C. Dean said the cart stood from 9.10 a.m. to 9.45 a.m., and he had to take care of it, as nobody was left in charge. Defendant was fined the goats, es. 6d.—Wm. Rimmer, greengrocer, Bold, was charged with a simiiar offence, at the same time and place. He was fined 55., including costs. MONDAY.—Before Messrs. F. Monks (chairman), T. Bin chsll, J. W. Hayward, and S. Wright. Assauvrs.—Martin Quinn, WAS fined 10s. and costs or 14 days imprisonment tor assaulting his wife Ellen Quinn on the 16th ult. Peter Daley was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in the Market Place on Saturday night, and with assaulting P.C. Hardy at the same time and place. The prisoner was creating a disturbance in the Market Place on Saturday night, and when requested by P.C. Hardy to go home, he struck that officer twice, and he was then locked up.—Prisoner was fined ss.and costs or 7 days. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—EIIen Jannion was fined 53. and costs or seven days' imprisonment for being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-St., on Saturday night. •- . . , _</p>
        <p>TUESDAY—Before Mr. Ziba Armitage (in the chair), Dr. Starkey Smith and Mr. E. J. Gaest. A BED OF OATS.—Edward Richards and Arthur Allman, who said they came from Liverpool, were charged with sleeping in a field of oats on the farm of Mr. Norcott, and doing damage to the extent of ss. 'The prisoners were found at six o'clock on Tuesday morning in'an oat field in Minchester-road covered with six sheaves of oats. Some of the oats had got buried in the ground by the men lying on them. Mr. Norcott said he had bad his crops damaged previously by the same means. Prisoners were each fined 2s. 6d. and the costs and half the damage, or seven days' imprisonment. They went to gaol. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Geddes (in the chair),</p>
        <p>and Councillor J. H. Fleming. ANOTHER BATCH OF SLEEPERS OUT.—Henry Quinn, John O'Miller, William Horns, John Russell, and Joseph Jackson, all strangers to the town, were charged with sleeping in an oat field of Mr. Norcott's, Finger Post Farm, Manchester-road, on Tuesday night and doing damage to the extant of 15s. The prisoners had untied a large quantity of the sheaves of oats and scattered it on the ground to make a bed. They were fined 2s. 6d. each and costs or seven days. TRAVELLING WITHOUT TICKET.—James Chisnall was charged with travelling on the C.L.C. railway, from Manchester to Warrington without paying the fare. Mr. P. Davies appeared to prosecute on behalf of the railway company.He said that on the arrival at Warring- ton of the 6 30 p.m. train from Manchester the prisoner was seen by one of the ticket collectors to leave the train. He was asked for lais ticket but he had none,and no money to pay the fare. The prisoner said he had worked at Mr. Barlow's warehouse in Manches'xr but had been discharged for dishonesty. The magistrates fined him 10s. and costs or 14 days. CHALICE OF ASSAULT.—Fraok Hares and Mary Hares were charged with assaulting Ann Hares, mother of the male prisoner, on Saturday night. The complainant said both prisoners came into her house drunk and kicked and thumped her on Saturday night. There was no one else in the house at the time.—P.C. Wheatley said he saw the complainant on the night in question. She was lying outside her house, and it was with diffi- culty he could get her in the house. She had had some drink. She complained of being abused. He saw the two prisonerl in the house, but they denied striking her. The magistrates were of the opinion that there was a doubt in the case and dismissed it. AN ABSENTEE MILITIAMAN.—John Dean was charged with absenting from the annual training of the 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Militia on the 24th July. He was fined 40s. and costs or seven days.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY .—Before Alderman Chandley (in the chair), and Mr. L.Voisey ANOTHER DESERTER. —Thomas Browne was charged with being a deserter from the Royal Artillery. P.C. Berry said at 10.25 on Wednesday morning he met the prisoner in Orford-lane, going towards the Barracks. He was wearing military boots and shirt at the time. Pris- oner's answers not being satisfactory ho told him to accompany him to the police station, when the prisoner made an attempt to run away. The magistrates remanded him to Strangewaysto await a military escort. The officer applied for and received the usual reward. DRUNK.—William Wilkinson was dismissed on 'a charge of being drunk at the police station the previous after- noon, it being his first offence. HANDED OVER TO THE WIDNES POLICE.—John Smith, was charged with being in possession of five planks of timber, and a fire shovel. P.C. Evans said he went to the defendant's house in Dolman's-lane, and found him drunk in bed. He brought him to the police station and told him he was charged on suspicion with having taken some planks and a fire shovel. In reply he said " I have taken the planks from the chemical works in Mersey-st., and it was my intention to take them back, but I got drunk." He was unable to account how he came in possession of the fire shovel. It was found that the planks were taken two months since from Messrs. Roberts, Dale, and Co., and left in Mr. Jackson's yard in Golborne-st., until Wednesday, when the defendant sold them to Mr. Beswick for firewood for :v. 6d. A police officer from Widnes stated that it had beeu found that the fire shovel was stolen from the Market Hotel, Widnes. The magistrates therefore handed prisoner over to the Widnes police. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASES.—Mr. G. Riley, School At- tendance Officer prosecuted in the following cases of parents neglecting to sent their children regularly to school :—William Ward, filecutter. Rodney-streer, 53. and child ordered to attend Heath Side School; Frederick Roberts, clockmaker, Keen-street, ss. ; Elizabeth Light- foot, Hardy-street, ss. ; William Leyland, Bowcock-st., ss. ; Joseph Ellis, 53., and boy ordered to attend St. Barnabas School ;Peter Clemo, Guardian-street, 2s. 6d. ; John Barton, labourer, Old Gardens, 2s. 6d. ; James Bentley, forgeman, Winwick-street, 2s. 6d.; John Bur- gess, forgeman, Bewsey-road, 2.5. 6d. ; Hannah Mapp, widow, Stanley-street, boy John sent to the ship " Clio. ' Mr. Wright, attendance officer for the Warringtvn Union, prosecuted in the following case, John Bibby, Woolston, ss. ASSAULTING HIS WlFE.—Peter Caldwell was charged with assaulting his wife Elizabeth on the 9th inst. Mrs. Caldwell said her husband went to bed drunk, and when he awoke he got hold of her by the hair of her head and gave her two black eyes. He also threatened to murder her for screaming out. D.S. Monaghan said the prisoner had worked at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles for over 40 years and bore a good character. His wife was said to be a drunken wernan. Prisoner was bound over to keep the peace for six months. DRUNK AND RISORDERLY.—SamueI Terry was charged with this offence in Winwick-road the previous after- noon and also refusing to quit the licensed premises of Mr. Peers Brimelow, called the " Horse and Jockey." He was fined 103. and costs or 14 days' hard labour. SELLING MILITARY SHIRTS.—Walter WartalrtOß was charged with receiving two military shirts and dispos- ing of same on the Bth August. D.S. !donee:an said at 7.30 on Tuesday evening he saw the prisoner in com- pany with two soldiers. From a conversation he heard he made inquiries and found that the prisoner had offered two military shirts for sale, at Mr. Leslie's, Mer- sey-st., and that the prisoner had afterwards sold them for Is. each to John Phcenix, Winwick-road. Privates A. J. Kelly and Modgin, deposed to meeting prisoner in Walker's Vaults, Scotland-road. and they asked him to sell the shirts for them. The prisoner asked them whether they were regimental shirts, and they said "No." Mr. Leslie said the prisoner brought the shirts to him and he refused to have them, as they were regimental shirts,and were marked so. Provo-Sergeant Stringfellow said from information received from D.S. Monaghan, he asked the two privates for their shirts, and they ad- mitted that they had 'given them to a man to sell. The two privates were now in his custody. The magistrates fined Warburton 10s. and costs or 14 days. FalDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Wright, Councillor H. Roberts, and Dr.</p>
        <p>Gornall. DESERTED.—WiIIiam Fitzgibbon was remanded to Strangeways Gaol for deserting the First Battalion of the Royal Irish Regiment,stationed in Kildare on August 7th. THREATS.—Catherine Bate, 75. Stamford-st., was dis- missed on chaige of using threats to Hannah Kelly, 16, Jones Buildings, on the Bth inst. Alderman Harrison was very severe upon the complainant for bringing up the paltry charges and listening to every bit of gossip that was set about.—Sarah Gleave, 3, Back Stamford-street was bound over for six months for using threats to Sarah Rowson, 4, Back Stamford-street, on August Bth. A FUSTIAN CIITTEII AND ms EMPLOYEES.—Edmund Mills, fustian cutter, Orford-st., summoned Elizabeth Rowlands, for non-payment of 53.,ordered by the magis- trates. The woman was ordered to pay the amount within two months,or seven days' imprisonment. Mr. Mills also claimed from Edward Unsworth 103. as dam- ages for breach of contract on the 22nd July. The magistrates ordered him to pay 53. damages, and the MAUS at 2s, ed. per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>IMPURE BLOOD.</head>
        <p>IMPURE BLOOD.</p>
        <p>Impure blood is a fruitful source of many complaints aiid illnesses, and is generally caused y some derangement ot one or more of the vital organs of the body—for instance, by defective diges ion, and a torpid or sluggish liver. Among other symptoms of the derangement of these organs, and consequently of impure blood, are—nervousness. weakness, low spirits, melancholy, uneasy sleep. friglatful dreams, loss of appetite, skin eruptions. Ac. from which so many suffer and which produce ro many discomforts in our homes, aud sometimes lead to consequences still more painful and appaling. For each ^nd all of these ailments and others caused by INDIGESTION or DE- RANGED LIVER, It is demonstrated more and more clearly from day to day that GWILYNI EVANS' BITTERS is the " Best Remedy of the Age," for it is com- posed of NATURE'S REMEDIES only, and contains no mineral or other injurious sub- stance.</p>
        <p>It is effective when taken by the strong man and will not Injure the weakest and most delicate woman or child ; it is suit- title to all ages, at all seasons of the year ; It directly aims to remove the cause and root of the evil ; is easily obtained, and can always be at hand and Is a. remedy that has proved efficacious in numerous in- stances when all other remedies had failed.</p>
        <p>Send for Pamphlet of Testimonials. FRAUDULENT COUNTERFEITS.</p>
        <p>We are particularly anxious to caution the public against the attempts of some Members of the trade to pass substitutes or even counterfeits of our preparation. Ask plainly for GWILYM EVANS' QUIN- INE BITTERS, and see that the name GWILYM EVANS is on label, stamp, and bottle. Every bottle sent out of the lab- oratory is prepared ac-ording to his recipe, and under his direct management.</p>
        <p>- Sod by all Chemists in is. lid, 2s. 9d., and 4s. 9d. bottles, or direct from the Proprietors, carriage free by Parcels Post.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>INCIDENT IN AN ASSIZE COURT.</head>
        <p>INCIDENT IN AN ASSIZE COURT.</p>
        <p>ASTONISHING A SPECTATOR.</p>
        <p>An action for trespass was being tried in the Civil Court at the Leeds Assizes on Wednesday al ternoo n,when 1 a funny remark made by a witness led to a kud out- burst of laughter and applause. Lord Chief Justice Cole- ridge Instantly stopped the case, and seeing a man clap- ping his hands in the gallery ordered a policeman to bring him down. The man gave the name of Tom White, and said he was a lithographer, living at 30, Back Bar- rack-st., Leeds. His Lordship said that he would not have such unseemly conduct in his court,and as a lesson to others he would send him to gaol for forty-eight hours. White : When.-. The Judge : Now.—VVliite,who seemed considerably surprised, was then removed. At tha con- clusion of the case he was brought up and,after a severe admonition from the judge, liberated.</p>
        <p>WONDERFUL U A Sixpenny Bolt le of Mason'a Extract of ll«rb3 with the addition of Sugar and Water will make on eight gallons of Sparkling Deer. Don't accept Imitations Sample post free for nine stamps. Newball and Mason, Not- tingham Agents wanted.-4</p>
        <p>„ WARRINGTON CORPORATION OFFICIALS AT DINNER.</p>
        <p>Saturday evening was in many way a an enjoy.. able time for the 200 servants of the Wairingtom Corporation who met together at dinner in the Drill Hall. It was the first such gathering they have bad, but owing to the successful manner in which it passed off there is every reason to believe it will be' come an annual event. When the employes turned into the Hall before six o'ckck they could see that pains , had been expended by the Committee not only in or- ganizing the dinner but in making the surroundings pleasant to the eye, for there had been suspended from the support rods of the roof, clusters of pennants and banners, and in appropriate spots about the room were displayed plants and grasses, whilst near the entrance door was a refreshment bar. The dinner was a sub. stantial one, and was served up promptly and in good style by the caterer Mrs. Booth, assisted by Mr. and Mrs. Powiss, Town Hall keepers. At the head of the centre table sat Councillor Heaton, Councillor Dr. Cannell, and Messrs. Stanley Wilson, (deputy town clerk), J. Parker (head gardener), John Todd (foreman fitters Gas Works), J. Whitfield (foreman scavengers), and J. P. Leigh (cemetery). The committee was made up of two men from each department, viz :—Park and Cemetery: Messrs. William Ashton (secretary), and Isaac Parker; Water: Messrs,. William M, nks, and Samuel Turner ; Gas : Messrs. A. Robinson and Lees ; Paving and Sewerage: Messrs. William Woods (treasurer) and Samuel Ellison ; Sanitary Works : Messrs. H. Harding and A. tioult : Scavenging : Messrs. J. Whit- field and J. Molyneux. After dinner a short list of toasts was honoured. That of "The Queen "was proposed by Councillor R. Heaton who found opportunity to allude to the dinner, and to express the hope that it would become an annual one because of the pleasant and congenial chances it afforded of allowing the men to cement their sympathies and to patronize in the manner which men who worked together should. Mt.Ashton,of the Park department,submitted the health of the " Mayor and Corporation of Warrington" on behalf of the Committee he sincerely thanked Council- lor Heaton and Councillor Dr. Cannon for honouring the gathering with their presence that evening. Councillor Dr. Cannell responded on behalf of the Mayor and Cor- poration. After expressing the pleasure he found in attending that dinner be said he sincerely hoped it would be made an annual affair and offered his assistance in augmenting the funds at any future dinner. It was surprising, he said, when looking at the 200 men who were present to notice how many faces were familiar to him and other members of the Corporation, faces of men whom they met dailv,and vet were not aware that they were Corporation officials. He knew the Corporation would be pleased to hear of their officials meeting in that way, and on his own and on their behalf he wished all future gatherings should be as successful as that one. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Stanley Wilson next read the following letters from the Mayor (Alderman E. Greenall), and Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (Town Clerk) :—Town Hall, Warrington, July 17th, 1893. To Mr. Ashton. Sir,—T regret being un- able to accept the kind invitation on behalf of the Cor- poration einploye4, to preside at their dinner on August sth, but expect to be from home on that date.—Yours truly, E. Greenall, Mayor.—Town Clerk's Office, War- rington, sth August. Dear Ashton,—l regret to find that I shall not be able to join you at the Drill Hall to- night. I have caught cold in one of my eyes, and it is very much inflamed. I hope you will have a successful gathering and all will be pleased with the dinner. I think you ought to make it an annual affair. If you should run short of funds I shall be pleased to help you further.—Yours faithfully, J. Lyon Whittle. , After this little speaking, the diners settled themselves down to enjoy a "smoke," at which an entertainment was given by the Warrington White Star Minstsels. Those " White darkies " who took pirt in the perform- ance were :—Messrs. G. Coggins (manager), T. Hassal, J. Brown, J. Emson, G. Newton, J. Wright, W. Dockery A. Bates, W. Morgan, Rowley, P. Gray, T. Wooler, and T. Yeardley. Mr. Geo. Riley acted as accompanist.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. AUGUST 12, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>COAL TRADE CRISIS.</head>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>A Manchester correspondent telegraphs :—There is no probability that for the next couple of weeks any move will be made on either side to bring about a resumption of work at the pits. The collieries are still able to supply the requirements of regular customers, and therefore the coalowners will not stir, and as the men have not yet begun to draw on the funds of the district organisa- tion, the Miners' Federation will not depart from its pre- sent position. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Glover, J.P., miners' agent, has decided to throw in his lot with the miners in so far that he will sot receive one penny in salary while the men are out of work. Time is beginning to hang very heavily oe the hands of the men, and many families are feeling the pinch of approaching poverty. At the same time, they are firm in their determination to resist the reduction. Throughout the district the same spirit prevails. At the Frodsham Pit of the Liverpool and St. Helens Brick Company, Ravenhead, the men arc not asked to submit to a reduction, but they voluntarily tendered their notices, which expired on Wednesday. and 100 top and bottom hands ceased work. A colliery proprietor des- cribes the position of the coal trade during the past six mouths as a complete collapse, and argues that labour cannot control the supply and demand. The effect of the present strike will be to drive trade out of the country. MR. S. WOODS, M.P., ON THE STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Addressing a mass meeting of miners at Pendlebury, near Manchester, on Wednesday, Mr. S. Woods, M.P., said the stacks of coal which owners piled up were so many rods in pickle for the miners. He hoped in the future that if any employer in the area of the Federa- tion attempted to make a stack the miners would leave the pit. If they prevented stacking there would be very few struggles in the future. It was a reflection on the honour of the Northumberland, Durham, Scotch,and South Wales miners that they were not with the present strikers. From all he saw,the masters were not united, and there might be hopes of a settlement shortly. THE HAULIERS 4ND THE COLLIERS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of 10,000 colliers and hauliers from the eastern and western villages of Monmouthshire was held at Crumlin, on Wednesday, and it was nnanimously de- cided that the colliers would stand by the hauliers in their demand for 20 per cent.advance, and consequently the strike will be continued. The hauliers subequentiv held a private meeting to discuss the situation, but from this meeting Press representatives were excluded. The strike of hauliers at the Celynen and Prince of Wales Collieries, Abercarn, has thrown idle 3,000 col- liers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>TINPLATE INDUSTRY.,</head>
        <p>THE TINPLATE INDUSTRY.,</p>
        <p>Notices affecting 5,000 operatives were on Wednesday posted at the tinplate works in the Llanelly and Swansea districts to terminate contracts,owing to the uncertainty of coal supplies, and the general depression in trade. The hauliers in the Maesteg district resumed work on Wednesday, the coalowners having withdrawn the sum- monses.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY PANIC AMONG FLINTSHIRE COLLIERS.</p>
        <p>The Bettisfield Colliery, the largest in Flintshire has been stopped since Tuesday owing to the scare among the colliers that the Wrexham man, who are out on strike, are coming over to destroy the works while they were below engaged in the pit. The Denbighshirt col- liers did not arrive either on Wednesday or Thursday, as was expected, and the Bettisfield men will return to work later on. TFIE NORTHUMBERLAND MINERS.</p>
        <p>A Newcastle telegram says the Northumberland min- ers' officials have issued a second manifesto correcting the statements that ara circulated to induce them to join the strikes. It is pointed out that wages are 10 per cent, higher now that even the scale of 1883 wculd have been, and that a strike would be abortive. As to the demand of 161 per cent advance, it would probably end in their having to submit Co a seri- ous reduction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BRITISH SILLS V. HOME RULE. MR. LABOUCHERE ON THE LEGISLATIVE DEADLOCK.</head>
        <p>BRITISH SILLS V. HOME RULE. MR. LABOUCHERE ON THE LEGISLATIVE DEADLOCK.</p>
        <p>Returning again this week in " ;Nth," to a considera- tion of the political situation, Mr. Labouebere, M.P., writes Everything depends upon whether the next session is to be a British session, or whether we are to pass the time sending up Home Rule again to the Lords. We British have given up one session to Home Rule, and so far as regards the House of Commons it will have passed. We have a right, therefore, to claim that opr bill is sent up to the Lords once, before the Home Rule Bill is sent up twice ; all the more as sending it up a second time to the Lords will not advance Home Rule by one single day. The Irish ought to be—and I presume are—grateful to us for putting their bill in the forefront. Our bills are as important to us as Home Rule is to them. If the Irish are not prepared to do for us next session what we have done for them this session, the alliance which is based upon " give and take "must come to an end. No one is a m• re ardent Home Ruler than I am. But lam not one of those who live alone for Home Rule. / live for the trial of democracy in all parts of the United Kingdom, and lam not prepared to subordinate, in matters of procedure, everything to the arbitrary will of the Irish members. This would indeed be a strange mode of giving effect to an alliance between the British Radicals and the Irish Nationalists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>PRESTON DRESSMAKER ROBBED OF</head>
        <p>A PRESTON DRESSMAKER ROBBED OF</p>
        <p>00.</p>
        <p>Just after four o'clock, on Wednesday, Mrs. Carter, dressmaker. of Preston, hao £9O worth of Bank of Eng- land notes stolen from her hand in Upper Kent-street. Mts. Carter had been to Preston Savings Bank and with- drawn the money, being paid in fifty and two twenties. In Upper Kent-street a young fellow slipped up behind her aNd snatched her satchel and made good his escape, and has not been captured.</p>
