      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>COTTON SHIPMENTS TO MANCHESTER DIRECT. Mr. Marshall Stevens, manager, has issued a circular, in which he says that the Manchester Ship Canal, which is now rapidly approaching completion, will be ready very early in 1894, and in time to receive vessels convey- ing a portion of this season's cotton crop. The saving to merchants sending their cotton in vessels bound direct to Manchester will, he states, be considerable, amount- ing to at least six shillings par ton. The object of the circular is to recommend shippers, merchants, and spin- ners not to complete any further arrangements for the shipment of cotton without providing for the option of ordering the vessel to Manchester to discharge if the navigation is open.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>HEREFORD ELECTION.</head>
        <p>HEREFORD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>NOM IN ATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chave, deputy-Mayor, sat at the Guildhall from ten to twelve o'clock on Thursday, for the purpose of receiving nominations for tha Parliamentary vacancy. Mr. Radcliffe Cooke (Conservative) and Sir Joseph Pulley (Liberal) were duly nominated, six separate papers being handed in on behalf of each candidate. Mr. M'Cullock the" Independent" has withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>LOYAL ORDER OF ANCIENT MUT, HE RDS.</head>
        <p>LOYAL ORDER OF ANCIENT MUT- , HE RDS.</p>
        <p>The Pride of Latchford Lodge of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds, held a social gathering on Saturday at their lodge room, Cheshire Cheese Inn, Latchford, when about 59 members and visiting brethren sat down to a substantial dinner provided by the host, Bro. Thomas Ireland. Amongst those present were Councillor Dr. Cannel!, and Messrs. Paul Peters, James Gresty, James Lowe, Wm. Gleave, James Parr, and Peter Edgar (district officers),Peter Kirkham (sec.), Thomas Ireland (treadurerl, Chorley, Howard, Williams, Burgess, Cliff, Jeffries, and others. After dinner a short adjournment was made to the gardens of the host,which were much admired. A smoking concert was held under the presidency of Bro. James Gresty. The usual loyal toasts were given. Bro. George Howard proposed" Success to the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds." District Secretary P. Edgar,in responding, thanked the members for their kind invitation, and also for the hearty and en- thusiatic manner in which they had received the toast. He said that the order was in a highly satisfactory con- dition. The present capital was over £400,000, and its membership nearly 100,000. " (Applause.) Last year, out of an income of about £146,000, there was distri- buted to sick members, &amp;c, the sum of about £lOO,OOO. The order added to its capital after paying all expenses over £26,000. (Applause.). This, he thought, would speak with much more force than any words that he might give utterance to in regard to the work and ob- jects of the order. The money 80 distributed had, he felt, brought relief to manl homes whose members bad been laid aside by sickness, and all this had been done without the least tinge of charity. He urged, in conclu- sion, upon the members to be more ih earnest, and try by every means in their power to increase the member- ship of the lodge, and the Warrington district. (Ap- plause.) The toast of the s' honorary members" was, in a neat speech, proposed by Bro. Burgess, and the same was ably responded to by Councillor Dr, Cannel' and Mr.P. Peters. Bro. Thomas Chorlev proposed "Suc- cess to the Pride of Latchford Lodge," which was res- ponded to by the Lodge Secretary (Bro. Kirkham), who gave statistics to connection with the lodge which were highly satisfactory. During the evening songs, recita- tions, and selections of instrumental music were well rendered by Messrs. A. Grounds, A. Potter, H.Williarns, Joseph Baker, Wm. Massey, and J. Burgess. Mr. A. Grounds was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PLAGUE OF WASPS.</head>
        <p>THE PLAGUE OF WASPS.</p>
        <p>In Buckinghamshire the wasp plague has been offici:. ally recognised. A part of the main road between Aylesbury and Winslow was infested with the nests of these troublesome insects, making it dangerous for travellers to pass that way. The Bucks County Council by means of tar destroyed no less than sixty nests along two miles of the roadside and the Winslow villagers are once more able to journey to Aylesbury market in peace. It is reported that a plague of wasps, similar to that in Kent. prevails in Essex and Suffolk. At Mark's Tey, near Colchester, two children have been rendered blind and delirious for several hours in consequence of being stung by the pests. The people of Eye ,are being`tor- ) !muted by these objectionable insects.</p>
