      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>HATCHING EXTRAORDINARY.</head>
        <p>HATCHING EXTRAORDINARY.</p>
        <p>- d 4rnong the latest acquisitions of the ornithological ~,ePartment in the -Museum of Natural Bistory of . reulh Kensington is a very handsome emu in per- ,ee.t pluulaße, presented by the Rev. George Keight- of Stambridge, in Essex, to whom the I,,e4lnlseuni was already indebted for another fine ape- Ntlerl,of these strange Australian birds. Both had been ilF° e'r upwards of 25 years, says Miss Gordon Cum- . / b,!lg in the columns of a contemporary, having been ~ h:uught from the Antipodes, and their successive deaths Zwe been somewhat of the nature of family bereave- . The first died Irons natural causes, but the last i-45 dile to an unfortunate accident, some bullocks hay- into the emu's paddock and chased him,even ,owir,.. ~.. dieri "' "tin into his shed. The poor bird probably tielidPnstlY from terror. The emu is naturally very (its} +sad this one was almost blind, as one eye had been qh`r°Yed et a clod of earth thrown at him by a carter hc ° declared that the emu's approach frightened his ie,,c4s. rhe other eve suffered in sympathy. A curious ent in the life of this lonely bird is worth record- -1 Tile death of his companion left him very desolate, dtf4 after a while be seemed to sicken. His compassion- ,. d tnistress brouelit dainties to tempt his appetite, also ac,asitillYjar of water. Again and again she remonstrated 1 thy, st some tiresome or cruel unknown person who vrateTri.lcazied off the jar, leaving the poor bird without ;IP his ;IllallY Mr. Emu retired into his shed, folded elng lees and sat down utterly refusing ithlsh`t'e out till his master, convinced that the bright lore, Ils's would do him good, lifted him out by main talii,,' There, beneath the bird, were all the missing sligh'icc.t' l The conscientious emu, convinced ,that he see h,e`yi°, be fulfilling his destiny by hatching some eggs, th(, is-ng no wife to lay any for him, had substituted IsDhlyeeci smooth, round jars which he had abstracted 'ark ~„ aY l Of course,his wife s eggs would have been Se h''''een, but, as the French philosopher said. ' When, 80 h aye not what we like,we must like what we have ! etiat: re ade the most of what he could get. An amusing eggBvl4l.s t9ld concerning a setting of six or seves emu's Ise siyid In Berkshire. The father having at diligently chick- weeks, unfortunately died just a week before the tal,gra were hatched. Arrangements were made by Skthaph for tne transfer of the eggs to the care of th,te er emu in the Zoological Gardens in London, but leNi was i grave anxiety lest they should cool in the n- ki ti, ere tho foster father was ready to receive them. liuße' patent incubator was available suitable for such Slithue.ggs—they are about the size of an ostrich's—an the,e St'astic lady took them into her nice warm bed,and ' er etita;hed, mothering them till the last moment hat we 1,-I- 'rale started which conveyed them to the , i re they were , duly hatched a few days later. °Y sn'a,,ls devotion reminds me of what was told me Cr lady' who, when recently travelling in the %apt s,'-!! etance halted at a farm where the careful pea- Was sdlle thus Utilised her fat, bedridden husband, who 4141,,a,Shivieldv as to be unable to turn in bed. By ,dUty it `e scolding and coaxing she pursuaded him to do L'sr tNsLan Incubator, and to lie very still, hatching eggs ' lag t'et by the natural warmth of his body ! So there 441;4 ,w, it half a dozen eggs under each arm, afraid to LticeeeCi'eeds ti .lightest movement, and thus he had actually ';,! whi' 'lm ,_le hatching several coveys—an achievement wises"e was half proud and half shy! Of course, in se the eggs were those of common domestic fowls.</p>
      </div>
