      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>tettero to the Odor.</head>
        <p>tettero to the Odor.</p>
        <p>THE NEW BATHS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—What is the matter with the water supplied to the new baths ? It is being regarded with a considerable amount of disfavour, not to say suspicion, by some bathers. The water is considered to be somewhat too inferior to be altogether healthful, and I know of more than one case lately of young men who have been taken suddenly ill, either while in the water, or very soon afterwards. It may be, of course, that the persons I have alluded to have been " out of condition :" but, to say the least, the similar- ity of the symptoms displayed is rather remarkable. At any rate, as one who hears a great deal of nommen- on the subject, I would suggest that the Baths Committ tee go to the trouble of investigating the quality of the water supplied. If it is a fact that the water is not suit- able for the purpose, the sooner it is reme lied the better for the comfort of the bathing public and the prosperity of the new baths which are certainly eiccellent in every other respect.—Yours, &amp;c., AN OLD SWIMMER.</p>
        <p>A VINDICATION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have to-day returned from a most enjoyable excursion to Norway, and shall be glad if you will allow ma very brief space to make one special remark concern- ing it. It has been my privilege to be a passenger on board " The City of Richmond "steamer, and as statements to the detriment of that vessel have been circulated in many places, and in Warrington itself, I wish to say that there is no truth in them whatever. No vessel could well offer greater advantages in every way for such a tour as that I have just enjoyed among the Norwegian Fjords. Her size makes her unusually steady in cross- ing the North sea,and as my own experience has proved, is no hindrance whatever to her passing through the best of the narrowest Fjords. I was told in Warrington after my passage had been secured that I was going to sea in an unseaworthy vessel ; and moreover that if I escaped drowning, I would certainly suffer from starva- tion, as the food wa4 both inadequate and unappetising. These are simply mischievous canards, at any rate as respects the excursion just concluded. The ship gave every sign of being entirely qualified to face wind or wave ; and as for the commissariat and the cuisine,they were simply beyond all praise. The " menu " for break- fast, lunch, and dinner, was such as would satisfy the most ticklish appetite, and save on one or two occasions of alight " mal-de-mer " the passengers did full justice ' to the viands. The civility of captain, officers, and stewards was also beyond all praise, and I write in the bone that these lines may decide any who are hesitating whether to visit Norway in the "City of Richmond " or nut. Let them decide to go next year, for the trip com- mencing as these lines appear, is the last to Norway this season. There, is, however, an excursion advertised on this vessel for August 28th, from London returning to the Clyde on September 8, and including Brighton, Cowes, Cork, Dublin, Llandudno, Isle-of-Man and the West Coast of Scotland which looks very attractive. I need hardly add that I have no interest whatever in writing this letter, except as a matter of common justice. When either a man or a number of people called " a company "have their character or their busi- ness aspersed, it is only right for such as know the false- ness of the aspersions to give them contradiction.— Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>_ . . JOHN YONGE. 19, Wilson Patten-street, August 11, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>FUNERAL OF THE LATE MR. CHARLES</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF THE LATE MR. CHARLES</p>
        <p>ASIIIIRSI'.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mr. Charles Ashurst, ex- stationmaster at Bank Quay, Warrington, were in- terred at Rainford,on Sunday,amidst many tokens of respect. The coffin was carried to Bank Quay Station, and the procession started from the residence of the deceased, 11, Thynne-street, shortly before one o'clock, and a spe-Aal train was in waiting to Cr..mvey the mourners to Rainford. The chief mourners were Mrs. and the Misses Ashurat, Mr. Glover, Mr, W, Chairry, Mr. F. W. Chantry. Among "thoge present were Dr. Starkey-Smith, Rev. H. V. Pigot, vicar of St. Paul's; Mr. D. Starkey, stationmaster, Bank Quay ; Mr. Harter, night sta- tionmaster ; Mr. Peters, trafflo inspector; Mr.Brook- shaw, assistant district inspector ; Mr. Hodgson, district signal inspector ; Mr. Marshall, locomotive foreman ; Messrs. Turton an,l Tanner, platform in- spectors. Preceding the coffin were a number of the members of the Gilbert Greenall Lodge of Free- masons.of which the deceased was last year Worship- ful Master. Among these were Bros.C.Holiand,W.M.,; D. W. Finney, P.M., P.P.G.D.C. ; S. Wallhead, P.M., P.P.G.J.D. ; W.Taylor, Prov. A.G.P. : W.Richardson, P.M. : R. Heaton, P.M, ; T. Hutchinson, P.M. ; J. Smethurst, S.W. ; A.F.G. Potter, P.M.; J. Shillcock, P. 1.; W. Bellian, J.D. ; R. T. Smith, J.W.; E. Rose, P.M.,secretary ; J. Dobson, S.D. ; R.Shackleton, J.H. Scarisbrick, LG.; John Hampson, W.Whittaker, W. Chantry. S. J. Beaman. P. Edgar, R. W. Collin, A. Jolley, J. C. Spinks, J. W. G. Craddock, W. Coppack, Lythgoe, Clay, Pearson, Joseph Taylor Clare, H. Greenhough, D. Sumner, John Hesketh, The body was borne by Messrs. Bolton, Hitchmough, Limb, Morris, Ratcliffe,Newtou, and Taylor,sorne of the oldest guards on the line, and there was also a large number of railway officials of every grade present. The following gentlemen were present from St. Paul's Church,of which the deceased gentle- man was a sidesman, and also had been a teacher in the Sunday school, and walked to the station : Councillor Smethurst, warden; Messrs.Thos. Morris, F.G.S., John Hayes, Wm. Lawless, and Jos. Lawless, sidesmen ; Messrs. Wm. Richardson, and Hough, lay representatives. The funeral rites were performed by the Rev. Mr. Bridger, vicar of Rainforrl, and the Rev. H. V. Pigot. Wreaths and crosses were sent by the following :—Gilbert Green- al Lodge of Freemasons (1250) ; Ernploy6s London and North-Western Railway, Bank Quay Station; Mr. and Mrs. Chantry and family: employes of Mr. Chantry; Mr. and Mrs. W Lavvless, sen. ; the Misses Brown,the Misses B. and M. Lythgoe; Mr.and Mrs. Bate; Mr. Jones and.family, and others.</p>
      </div>
