      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>AHILETICS.</head>
        <p>AHILETICS.</p>
        <p>hoThS ON GAMES A. 2,1)</p>
        <p>CILICICET.</p>
        <p>Fortune is fickle ; at any rate, the fortune of the Warrington first eleven is fickle. They have had two more defeats this week. Of course no team can always be victorious, and if the players do their best and fail I to win you cannot blame them ; you may only say that their best does not seem to be very good. At he same time if the players are not exactly to blame, there is perhaps some fault to be found with the selecting of the teams. The team on Saturday was by no means as good as might have been chosen from the list of playing members. True, some of the best players were "away," but, then. what I contend is this, that by going away and allowing their club to suffer they are to some extent displaying a lack of true intereat in their home team.</p>
        <p>The scores were: Warrington 38, Wigan 77. Smith was captaining the home team, and when he won the toss he repeated G. Clarke's trick of last week and sur- prised the visitors (and spectators) by inviting them to take the first wicket. This they did, with a rush, but subsequent consideration apparently showed them that they had taken "the bait." Anyhow, their taking it did not allow Smith to say what Clarke had said" We've pulled it off." The wicket was very wet, and it would ce,tainly have gone hard with the visitors if it had not been for Macaulay and Kenwick ; the former scored 40 and the latter 20. This made up the score with the , exception of 14. The Warrington score getting was a pretty equally divided task for the highest score was eye. The bowling by Warrington was certainly very fair. Smith took four wickets for 17 runs, Podmore two wickets for 35 runs, and Ashton three wickets for 21 runs. The latter is an " innovation" into the team but he showed such surprising form, in bowling and bat- ting,that both the players and the spectators were taken by storm ; the excitement of the latter became so uncon- trollable that it was finally carried out en cheers. (No! I don't care for laudanum). Ashton certainly shows great promise. He is a workingman.; bowls with the left hand, and in every way promised to be a good can- didate for the vacancy created by the late Mr. T. Jolley.</p>
        <p>The other match which the first eleven lost will make yon laugh. In jest last week I suggested when the eleven wanted a licking it should procure it from a home junior team. It was simply a jest ; and in the wildest fight of speculation in which my mind indulged ; in the highest flight to the improbable, or the ludicorious on which my imagination journeyed, the conceptions pro- duccd were as starved clams compared with the collosal absurdity of the truth. (This is not a cribbed perora- tion). The real truth is simply this : The first eleven were beaten, on Wednesday, by the Bank-street Mission Club ! Joylul isn't it; creditable you milht say ! The scores were Warrington 83, the Mission, 85 ; of the War- rington score Podmore made 37 ; ana took eight wickets. What did the remainder do? Well, when they were fielding some one mentioned that they should use dolly tubs to catch the ball with.</p>
        <p>A benefit match for Sugg and Clarke (professionals) has been arranged. Warrington will meet Mefton, and the game will be played to the playing of music by the bands cf the 3rd and 4th Lancashire. F. Sugg of the Lancashire county is down to come; but in case he plays against the Australians on the date of the match he has promised to procure the assistance of a reserved man from the Australian ranks. The following are the teams :—C. L. Jones, F. J. Jones, G. C. Rees, T. A. Stubos, T. W. Stubbs, Harold Ratcliffe, F. Edwards, H. Brotherton, C. Overton, J. Latham, flandford (Pro.) Oakley (Lan. Cty.), R. Smith (Lan. Cty.), S. M. Cros- field (Lan. Cty.). F. Sugg (Lan. Cty.), G. Clark, W. Podmore, L. A. VoiseY, E. M. Griffin, W. S. Tyler, J. Bibby, E. Parry, Sugg (Derby Cty.), Clarke.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wesleyans after their easy victory of the previous week were badly beaten on Saturday by Widnes White Rose. The play of the White Rose in every department was excellent, their left hand batter H. Wall hittine out all over the field, and had scored 23 before he was stumped smartly by Clare. Sadler was in his best form with the ball, capturing five wickets for six runs. On the side of the Wesleyans, Simpson was the best man in both batting ad bowling as he was top scorer on his side for eight, and also captured five wickets for 13 runs. Jefferson captured four wickets for 13 runs. The Wesleyans have a stiff task before them to-day when they meet the Penketh Friends. The last match with this club was a good one, the Friends winning by 71 to 60.</p>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>A number of the members of the Warrington Cycling Club were to have gone on a tour through the Midlands during this week. They have not gone. Only Messrs. E. Pickavance (the genial secretary), Mounfield (cap- tain), Garnett and Short turned up on Saturday, arid they decided to alter the route to a trip in North Wales. So they set out and reached Llangollan that evening, and stayed there until Monday morning, when they set out for Bartnouth ; but Fate did not allow them to achieve this purpose. No; Dame Fate coquetted with that mischievous Old Man Pluvius, and old Sha made the latter smile with tropical brightness until the unsuspecting ones were five miles from any dwell- ing, and then she went over to Pluvius and induced him to pour down a special brew—probably telling him it was to help the crops.</p>
        <p>Anyway when it was stopping raining the wheelmen found themselves to be a bedraggled quartett, with a more pressing necessity to know how to swim than to know how to ride. They were rescued from a watery grave at Bala, where some good Samaritans took them in, and fed them and lent them some more clothes and boots while their own dried. Evidently they didn't have enough water for they went fooling about Bala lake, pretending to fish, and one of them in a letter says they succeeded in catching 13 perch weighing 11lbs ! They returned the borrowed clothes, strange ti say, on Tuesday. and cleared off to Barmouth. The latest I heard of them there was that they were "enjoying the minstrels."</p>
        <p>What did you think of A. T. Daintith's performance at the football sports on Monday ? I thought it was good to see the way he carried off the W.C.C.Champion- ship against such competitors as Fair and Buxton. I was not aware we had such a smart young rider amongst us until then, but although it was practically his first appearance before a Warrington audience, it was evi- dent from the hearty reception he got that his popularity had preceded him—and unlike some riders, popularity did not precede his ability, it stopped solid with him. The great feature of his riding is the amount of staying power he seems to possess, and the astounding strength he can throw into his final spurt.</p>
        <p>There is great promise for this rider, who is compara- tively Young,as I pointed out last week, I think he can pretty well clainvto be the quickest local short distance rider, now, anyway. He practically only started riding this season, and he carried off the 2nd prize at Garston Cricket Sports in the one mile, and the 3rd prize in the three mile ; he took seconds in the two miles handicaps at both Eyerton Football and Tranmere Football Sports, and a second at the Liverpool League of the Cross SpQrts last week. I hone he will keep this successful career up, and do credit to the Warrington cycling world, as 14e promises to do.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT HOMING</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Cheltenham Race: 106 miles, took place on Mon- day, August 7th, 10 members sending 200 birds, which were liberated in a strong north-west wind at 9.40 a.m. Mr. henry Clare, of Flag-lane, was the first to time in, being quickly followed by Messrs. Parkinson, Greenough, Burrows, and Buckley. CHELTENHAM.</p>
        <p>Velocity Result. Name. yds. per minute. I—H. Clare 945 2—C. D. Parkinson 3-11. F. Greenough 927 4—E. Burrows 5 6—John Buckley 7—H. Clare B—J. Buckley 9 10 11-11. F. Greenough 892 12—E. Burrows 13—H. F. Greenough B7l 14—John Houghton 15—J. Naylor 16 17—C. D. Parkinson 18-11. J. Longton 19—C. D. Parkinson 20—John Naylor 789 21—P. Leicester _ . 22—H. J. Longton 776 _</p>
        <p>Swindon Race: 131 miles, took place on W ednes- day, 10 members sending 190 birds which were liberated in a south-east wind, by the Manchester Flying Club convoyer, at 830 a.m. The first two birds were timed in by Mr. Burrows of Legh-st., at 11-21 am. effecting the grand velocity of 1,360 yards per minute. '</p>
        <p>SWINDON.</p>
        <p>Velocity Rost'lt. Name. yds. per minute. 1 1 2—E. Burrows 1 3 4—P. Leicester 5 6—E. Burrows 1306 7—H. P. Greenough B—H. Clare 9 10 11-0. D. Parkinson 1233 12-11. J. Longton . 1224- 13—H. Clare 14 15 16—H. F. Greenough 17-5. llouOiton 18—J. Hou2llton 19—U. J. Longton Owing to an unavoidable error the returns of the Cheltenham race published last week were given. erroneously. The above are the corrected returns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, AUGUST 12, 11893.</p>
        <p>a conversation in the middle of a sentence where she had stopped to die a bit—at least a nurse said she re-opened it there.</p>
        <p>From 'here I went through the old male inmates sitting room. it is a large spacious, clean compart- ment and is used by the old men over sixty who are excused from working. There were about 12 of them and they were sitting and chatting quite con- tentedly. Further on there was a similar room for all women over sixty. From here we emerged into a large court yard—or something that looked like one. Here the capable inmates were at work; some chopping chips, some mending boots, some assisting the tailor, some painting. As with ti.e vagrants, so with many of these inmates so far as work goes; they cannot understand why they should work for their keep when the ratepayers pay for it, and many of them firmly believe that they are working for the interests of the officials. Everything was decorous and clean, and there were few young men observable, and what there were consisted of a few imbeciles and two or three men, who, as one put it with unintentional truthfulness, "are incapable of working." The routine life of the house is very quiet. At six o'clock in the morning all are sup- posed to rise and prayers are read by the Master. I learn that most of the inmates seem to have got beyond being interested in prayer and very few of them turn in. At seven, breakfast is held—most all of the inmates turn in to this. After breakfast the able bodied people go to their work, the women ' being engaged in domestic duties chiefly. Dinner is served at 12; more work until six; tea then, and two hours " mosying" around and bed at eight. Breakfast consists of bread and butter and tea or coffee; dinner of meat and potatoes; and tea of bread and butter and tea. Each) member is pro- vided with a double outfit, and this dissipates the idea held by some people that they only get one outfit and lie in bad one day a week while it gets washed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>j PEEPING AT PATTERLAND.</head>
        <p>j PEEPING AT PATTERLAND.</p>
        <p>AT THE WARRINGION WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>BY LUKE BLUNT.</p>
        <p>I refused to speak to the tramp at the door. It was not because I have not a heart with some kindness in it, or because I was too proud to be seen speaking to a tramp. I simply didn't do it be- cause he claimed to know me—and there were listeners hard by. He admitted that it was hard on me to run unexpectedly against" an old pal " to use his vulgar term—but he would have to get use to remember when I was hard up, too. But I did not wait for him ts do this ; I did not care to. When he thrust out his hand for me Co shake, I put a penny in it, and murmured audibly " Poor beg- gar—and his head's affam ed too." bla3 be it was mean to do that—but when a man is respectable he's got to keep so, and not get talking and chatt- ing with people who are poor and low. Before they would pass me through the door into the yard of the Workhouse, my name was taken, my occupation, where I slept last night, where I was going to go in the morning, could I chop chips (to which I said yes), any money about me (which of course I hadn't), and had I got an order of admittance. I said " did they take me for a tramp" and they said they did—at first—but they regretted the mistake, now I told them I wasn't.</p>
        <p>First of all I visited the vagrant ward. All tramps are kept here; they are only allowed to stay one night as vagrants. If they stay longer they become inmates and have to remain in the House for a certain length of time before they can leave. There are two things which these vagrants do not like in connection with a night's sojourn in the Union. First of all, when they arrive they have to undergo a bath—its very hard for then!, but some get used to it by tramping about from one Union to the other, and the remainder have sometimes heard of people recovering from a bath, so they collect their courage together and ,get through the ablu- tions like so many timid cats. Then they have some cold water and bread—or " skilley "—and go to bed, after making a meal of it; not the bed but the ekilley. Occasionally there will happen among these itinerant beings a man of more comprehen- sive mind—and tastes— and lie will object to the ".skilley," and want a bit of chicken or venison, or even beef, end some beer, and he says he knows enough about the poor law to tell him that he is entiticd to it, no matter what the master thinks. This man soon learns that the union isnot a Social- istic Utopia, built according to his views. Then he gets the grip, so to speak, and makes music by waltzing about the establishment with a jack knife in one hand and a stone in the other, vowing to "do for 80,11( body." Sometimes such men have to be placed under arrest. As a rule, however, these tramps are quiet enough, when the bath is over,. until the next morning, when they have to set to , and break some stones—half a ton I think. For some reason they object to this—they will agree to express their gratitude verbally, but for some un- accountable reason they will not do it by breaking a few stones. They would do anything rather than that—they would rather break the first command- ment, or even the whole ten and some more, and think it easier. They are strange people. I went amongst some ten or twelve of them while they were engaged in their morning avocation. Now, it is not often that a man is compelled to do his task if he turns obstinate. He is generally kept in until afternoon and then released, whereas he might have completed his work by nine or I ten o'clock, and been away on his journey. Some of them are aware of this and act accordingly. One of the group that I saw had seated himself near the heap of stones and was amusing himself by shying his hammer at any prominent stone, just for fun, , but allowing all broken stones to form a balance against his obligations. Most of the others worked as though there was a wager to be earned—for the one who broke the fewest stones in the longest time. I s.oke to the man who was playing" Aunt Salley" with the stones,and tried to raist him from the slough of laziness into which he had fallen. I spoke quietly to him, but earnestly. He said" I'm not lazy—l'm refusing to work simply on principle. You know that the fatepayers pay for the mainten- ance of paupers—l done it when I had a house— and what I says is this : How can they pay for my maintenance if I got to break these belly stones for a single night's doss. I'd be maintaining myself, when my keeps already paid for, and that ain't square by no means." Poor, fallen, soulless (and voteless) mendicant ! When such ptinciples become common here as they did in Italy and Spain, we'll be a nation of beggars with no " glorious empire " to brag about.</p>
        <p>From here I went through the whole of the inmates' establishpients. What a medley of human beings were here ? There were decrepit old men, feeble old women; middle-aged men, fallen through unfortunate habits, women of similar ages degener- ated into the same fashion ; there were male and female imbeciles of all ages ; there were blind old people lying abed, divested of physical strength by age, and waiting day by day with quiet, pathetic patience, for Time to drop them from the pauper bed to the grave. There were homeless young people, stricken with the wasting decay of consumption; young people for whom life bad sunshine no more, in whom hope had been chased away by the know- ledge :of a certain and imminent death, and who waited with uncomplaining resignation until the disease should finish its cruel task that they might find respite in the deathly stillness of a pauper's tomb. And how quickly all this flies to the visitor's mind and heart, how keenly he appreciates the cruel fate of being driven in sickness and want, to the house of the pauper; to be without the sym- pathies of friends and relatives, to be without the careful watching and nursing of parents ! Yet amongst all this it is difficult to find one person totally unreconciled to his surroundings, for use, and the kindness of the Matron and nurses, have worked out the inevitable result ot stilling to some extent the sense of loneliness. I could not help hut- perceive bow vital to the interests of these inmates is the disposition ot Matron or Master and nurses of the house—kindness of nature does not come in the contract when these responsible officials are en- gaged, but it is like a Godsend to the inmates and others, and it is a virtue which none can deny those in charge at the Union at present. This I am sure of from personal observation. Yet it does not do to suppose that all the inmates suffer when they come into the House; there are those who take to the life immediately, and there are some charac- ters whose vagaries help to throw a considerable amount of sunlight into the daily routine life. For I instance I saw a little scene enacted in one of the 1 convalescent wards, the chief character in which was a religious imbecile. She was a strong looking woman of about 40, and to her own belief had been dying every day for the past two years. There were four other women in the room with her, and when she found it" coming on," she shouted out, for these room mates to get the service agoing. The day I was in I was not aware of this, and conse- quently surprised when I saw the woman extend her arms, allow a fixed petrified glare to enter her eyes, and then say: "Oh ! He's coming again. . . . See. . lie is all in white. His raiment is of white robe. His hat is of a golden crown. . . . Oh, save, oh, save me, everyone." The other four simply took her and led her to the bed, and knelt down by her side, and didn't show any more concern in doing it than one would in removing a fly from one's nose. Then she said : "Will you sing—it calms my soul ; and Ile will like it, especially • Abide with us.'" "Yes, we'll sing; he calm . . . and don't dirty the quilt with your boots." Then those four women got their hymn books, and commenced to sing "Abide with us," and didn't see the weakness of the offer, though it was made in good part. In a while the dying woman said feebly :</p>
        <p>" It's nearly over, and they are all ready to take me up in a chariot. Sisters do me a last little favollr--"</p>
        <p>They didn't need to hear the request; they had been there before. They formed in a procession (that, WRB what she wanted), and rr arched round and round the bed,looking like a funeral procession without the heurse,and singing like, like—well,like anything; I won't make a comparison. This held out fOr about five minutes, during which the dy- ing (!) woman moaned and sobbed, and cried, and sang snatches of the hymn. and when one of the other four substituted the himm with a "passage their poor sister fell into a kind of a stupor. In a while she opened her eyes quite calmly. The fit had pas.:ed over. She sat up. and pointing to a table, ' said:</p>
        <p>'Gimme that crust, will yo?' They gave it her, and she got up, and no one seemed a tift surprised or glad or anything that she had not died ; they simply went back to What they had been doing, whilst the dying woman ,re-opened</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>MARRIAGE AT RUNCORN. •</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE AT RUNCORN. •</p>
        <p>A marriage in which much more than the ordin- ary interest was mahifested took place on Wednes- day afternoon, at Runcorn Parish Chur h. The contracting parties were Miss Mary p lughton, daughter of eaptain William Houghton, if Bootle, and Mr. Albert Abel, son of Mr. Richard Abel, of Tower House, Oreenway-road, Runcorn, who is a wellknown boat builder and trader, and a memb• r of the Board of Improvement Commissioners. Long before the bridal party arrived, the sacred edifice was crowded whilst hundreds thronged the entrance to the church yard, and it was with some little difficulty the wedding guests obtained entrance. The ceremony took place at two o'clock, the Rev. J. Holden being the officiating clergyman. The bride was attired in a pearl grey cashmere dress, trimmed with white silk and white bugles, and wore a white hat trimmed with white ribbm and feathers. She also carried a bouquet of choice flowers. She was attended by her sister, Miss Sarah Houghton, and Miss Fanny Ann Abel, sister of the bride- groom, and they were both attired in white and cream dresses with hats to match. Mr. Fred Abel was the best man. The bride was given away by her father. The guests present were Mr. and Mrs. Abel, Mrs. Dykes, Mr. and Mrs. Hawkins, Mr. and Mrs. Barnes, Mr. Blundell, Miss Hettie Waterworth, Miss Carrie Abel, Mr. Harry and Mr. Willie Burton (Warrington), Miss Maggie Lowe, Mr. Thomas Clark and Miss Elizabeth Houghton. After the ceremony and the usual formularies in the vestry, the happy couple were the receipients of hearty good wishes from many friends and and at the porch were greeted with showers of rice. Luncheon was provided at the house of the bride's sister in York-street, and Mr. and Mrs. Abel departed by the 5.10 train for Winder- mere, where the honeymoon will be spent. Messrs. F. and %V. Yarwood supplied the coaches. The following is a list of the presents Mr and Mrs Abel, household furniture Mr and Mrs Houghton, sets of carvers and family Bible and glass dishes Mr Fred Abel, 1 pair of water colour paintings Fannie and Carrie Abel, handwainted plaque, with easel and stool</p>
        <p>Mr Abel's workmen, marble clock Mrs Barnes, pair of glass salts with spoons set in silver Mrs Hawkins, doz. china cups and saucers Mrs Coward, 1 pair fish carvers Mrs Rushton 1 pair china vases Mrs Dykes, 1 set of toilet trinkets Mr and MN T Clarke, 1 set of toilet trinkets Miss Waterworth, coal boa Mrs S Hough, 1 brass stool May Hough, moustache cup and saucer Mrs J Jones, paper rack Mrs J Horsefield, cheese stand Mrs H Rathbone, breakfast cruet Mrs Eden, photo stool and vase Mr Hampson, kettle, lading can, cullender, and shovel</p>
        <p>Miss Brocklehurst, a view in plush frame Mr and Mrs II Barnes, china tree pot stand Miss Lowe, pair of china ornaments Mrs Atkin set of jugs Mr and Mrs 11 Davies, sugar basin, cream jug, and flower stand</p>
        <p>Mr and Mrs F Gibson, flower stand • J, Lottie Gibson, butter cooler Mrs J Handley, set of jugs. A, Friend, a pair of china ornaments ..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>TEE ACCOUNTANT AND THE BRICKLAYER.</head>
        <p>TEE ACCOUNTANT AND THE BRICKLAYER.</p>
        <p>A RUNCORN DEBT COLLECTING CASE. At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Tuesday before Dr. Steele and Mr. P. Speakman, a bricklayer, named William Howard, of Wellington-street, was sum- moned for assaulting George Torrie, an ex-county couit bailiff. The complainant who said he now followed the occupation of an accountant, stated that on Saturday afternoon, the first July, he was standing opposite .Miss Mack's shop in High-street, in company with Mr. Arkwright, the bailiff, when the defendant came up and threatened to take his life. He could see that the defendant was a little in drink. He asked him " Do I owe you anything?" and the defendant replied "No, but I owe you £5." "Then," said the complain- ant," go home and get it paid." After again threaten- ing the complainant, the defendant went in the direction of home. A quarter of an hour later he (complainant) was passing down the "Theatre Wynt," when the defendant came behind him and struck him a blow on the ear, stunning him and causing him to drop his stitk. As he was stooping to pick up his stick, the defendant struck him again and he escaped by getting into the yard of the Derby Arms Elotel,and a powerful woman came and took the defendant away.—Robert Arkwright deposed to the threats.—The defendant said complainant had bought a debt owing by him and had recovered an order against him in the County Court. Because the money was not paid just in time he had asked his wife for the money, and had written letters threatening to" distress "him. —The complainant: That's a lie.—Dr. Steele: Silence, you must not use those words in Court.—The defendant denied that he had struck the complainant ; but he had told him next time he wanted money, to get it off him because it was a " building debt" and not a household debt. —The complainant denied,that he ever went, to the house.—The Bench dismissed the case.—Complainant (in astonishment) : Dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>LILY OF THE VALLEY LODGE NO. 515</head>
        <p>LILY OF THE VALLEY LODGE NO. 515</p>
        <p>RUNCORN DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>On Saturday night after,the ordinary business oi the above lodge, the officers and members took ad- vantage of their removal to the Primitive Methodist new school, Greenway-road, to present Bro. Thos. Nicholls, trustee, with a life-sizJ portrait of himself, for his valuable services to the lodge for upwards of 20 rears. The portrait WAS neatly executed by ii6ssra. A. and G. Taylor, Church-st., Liverpool. Bro Samuel Boyers, N.G., occupied the chair, a large nualber of members and past and present officers being present. Bro. Walter Worthington, a co-trustee, was called upon to make the presentation, and in a neat speech he des- cribed the valuable serviaes Bro. Nicholls had rendered to the lodge at a sacrifice to himself, and he hoped that Mr. Nicholls would continue to do so with that persever• ing energy which was characteristic of him as one of the pioneers of reform in connection with frieudlv societies, and his own lodge in particular. The members wished that he would receive the present as a token of the res- pect and esteem in which he was held by the members of the lodge.—Bro. Nicholls, in reply, remarked that it was.guite A surprise to him when it was mentioned that they wished him to sit for his portrait; however he thanked the officers and members, one and all. He des- criherl the position of the lodge when he first took office , some 20 years ago and its present condition which, he said, was second to none in the order. He hoped they would not think this was through his energy alone, ; they had all worked for the same goal, especially their secretary, Bro. John Holt, Bro. Worthington, and a few others. He hoped and trusted the lodge would still go on prospering.—After a few remarks by Bro. l'ohn Bolt, the secretary, sod other members, the meting was brought to a conclusion by singing the Oddfellows' clos- ing song.</p>
        <p>LONGEVITY IN THE FEN DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>A man named Horsley, living at Soharn, in Cam- bridgeshire, haejust entered'up3u his 103rd year, and he is not only side to take kNercise, but can read without glasses. ti Tham is in the Fen District of England,which was formerly regarded as the most unhealthy part of the country; hut of late years cases of longevity has been prolific there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday. The Mayor (Alderman F. H. Gossage), presided, and there were present Aldermen J. W. Wareing, H. Timmis, J. Williamson, Councillors G.I. Neil, H. B. Shores, J. Shuttleworth, C. E. Barlow, S. Quinn, T. Carney, W. Christie, J. Gough, H. O'Don- nell, I'. Longton, S. Owens, G. Davies, H. .S. Oppen- helm (clerk), T. Craik (deputy clerk), I. Carr (en- gineer), and W. Lowe (nuisance inspector.) APPOINTMENT OF COMMITTEEB. The Town Clerk reported that there were vacancies on the Finance, Technical Instruction and Library Commit- tees, caused through the disqualification of Councillor Fitzpatrick, and on the motion of Alderman Wareing seconded by Councillor Quinn, Councillor O'Donnell was elected a member of those Committees. Councillor O'Donnell inquired if it were open far the members of one Committee to attend the meetings of another Com- mittee. The Mayor replied that it was not usual and the Town Clerk said it rested with each Committee.</p>
        <p>_ lIGHWAY MATTE us. The Highway Committee had resolved that Mr. Chas. Davies be paid £3l 10s., for the 136 square yards of land taken into the roadway in Brown-at. The Surveyor re- ported that Deacon-road and Appleton village sewers were completed. Lowerhouse-lane would be completed in a fortnight's time.—Councillor Christie moved the adop- tion of the minutes.—Councillor Gough said he had be- fore drawn attention to the danger of the railway cross- ing in West Bank-st., but nothing had been done. Gates ought to be erected. No later than Saturday two chil- dren had to be pulled from beneath a train there.—Coun- cillor Christie replied that the Surveyor was away on his holidays, the subject would be laid before him on his return.</p>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE. The minutes of this committee included the following —Medical Officer—The committee have heard statements hy the chairman and other members of the committee with regard to the conduct of Dr. O'Keeffe, the medical officer of health,on the occasion of the annual inspection on the 25th ult., and having also heard Dr. O'Keeffe in reply, adjGurned the further consideration of the same to a special meeting of the committee to be held on Wed- nesday the 2nd prox., at 1-30 p.m.—Hospital reports— The following reports submitted :—Accident Hospital— Member of patients at last meeting, 3 ; admitted since, 5 ; discharged, 2 ; died, 0; patients still in, 6.—lnfec- tious Diseases Hospital—Number of patients at last meeting,6 ; admitted since, 18 ; discharged since. 3 ; died since, 3 ; patients still in, 18.—Resolved : That having, regard to the number of patients now in the hos- pital,an additional trained nurse be engaged temporarily. The report of the Medical Officer of Health submitted showed that 78 births and 18 deaths, being at the res- pective rates of 31'2 and 324 per 1,000. The deaths in- cluding children under 1 year, 34 ; children between 1 and 5 years, 10 ; Zymotic diseases, 30. Notifications of Infectious Diseases as follows were received :—English Cholera, 6 ; Enteric Fever, 27 ; Erysipelas, 3 ; Mem- braneous Croup, 1. Total 37. The report continues: The death rate continues to be unusually higk. This is chiefly accounted for by the unfavourable weather which has prevailed for some time. As usual the deaths of children under one year account for nearly half the total and they are due to A great extent to one cause— diarrhcea. The cases of enteric fever notified are much more numerous than usually. I think that chloride of lime might with advantage be sprinkled in back pas- S3geS in various parts of the town periodically, say once a week during the prevalence of the hot weather. Water closets wraild too be of inestimable advantage." It was resolved that the Nuisance Inspector be. instructed to cause the back passages and aohnits in the various parts of the town to be sprinkled with chloride of lime as sug- gested by the Medical Officer of Health. At a special meeting or the Committee held on the 2nd inst. The Committee further considered the matters referred to in the minutes of the proceedings of the last meeting of the Committee with respect to the occurrences at the annual inspection, and a letter from Dr. O'Keeffe tender- ing an ample apology, read. It was resolved that the apology tendered by Dr. O'Keeffe be accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>AN EXTRAORDWARY</head>
        <p>AN EXTRAORDWARY</p>
        <p>Councillor Neil, in moving the adoption of tho minutes, said there was an epidemic of an extraordinary kind in Widnes and many deaths had occurred not only at the Accident Hospital but in private houses. He had been informed oy several medical men and by the matron at the hospital that it was the worst type of typhoid fever they had ever treated. He had called at the hospital every day during the epidemic and he had always found the matron and her staff doing their best to cope with the disease.—Councillor Shores suggested that the Com- mittee should hold an inquiry as to the origin of the typhoid fever in the town. It might be possible to tabu- late the streets in which the disease occurred, and note points of similarity and the circumstances surrounding the various patients.—Councillor Neil said he had no ob- jection to hold an inquiry; the Committee would only be too pleased to try and find whence the disease arose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>DR. O'KEEFFE EPISODE.</head>
        <p>THE DR. O'KEEFFE EPISODE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Neil referring to the minutes with regard I to Dr. O'Keeffe said it was within the knowledge of all the members that a certain official did use language and I conducted himself in a manner which was not in keep- , ing with his position. The committee had given the matter their most anxious consideration, alld they had decided to recommend the council to allow things to remain as they were.—Councillor Carney seconded the adoption of the minutes.—Councillor Shuttleworth ' inquired If the chairman of the committee had any objection to the apology being read. He thought the Council were most grossly insulted.—Councillor O'Don- nell inquired whether the chairman would also st.te I whether Dr. O'Keeffe was on duty, and where the occur- rence took place.—Councillor Neil said he had not the slightest objection to the letter being read, but he sup- posed there would have to be a resolution of the Council to that effect. He did not think the medical officer was on duty, he was an invited guest.—Councillor Shuttle- worth moved that the letter he read.—Councillor Christie seconded.—Councillor Quinn moved an amendment that the matter be allowed to drop. He thought they had heard quite enough of it. There was no one.felt it more than he did and those with whom he had the honour to belong ; still at the same time they were going too far, more especially with the Press, because he was given to understand by one of the members of the Press that nothing would be said:of the incident,yet in one of the weekly papers this stared them in the face in opening it. —Alderman Williamson seconded the amendment. He thought it wise and proper that the council should leave the matter as it stood on the minutes.—ln favour of the letter being read there voted Alderman Timmis, Coun- cillors Christie, Neil, Shuttleworth, and Davies-5 ; against, the rest of the council. The Mayor therefore declared the motion to read the letter, lost.—The minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GAS AND WATSR CnISMITTHE.</head>
        <p>GAS AND WATSR CnISMITTHE.</p>
        <p>The Engineer reported to this committee that the new engine "Benjamin Brown," at Netherley, has continued to run in a satisfactory manner during the whole of the past month. The average speed has been 14 strokes per minute. Notwithstanding the continuous pumping at the rate of ai million tons per diem. the level of the water in the boreholes remains practically unaltered.— Coal Strike—Stocks of Fuel—There is about a week's supply of fuel in stock from to-day at'both pumping sta- tions. An extra 100 gallons has been bought for Nether- ley, which will last a fortnight longer, and if any diffi- culty should arise at the expiration of that period in ob- taining a further supply, gas coke can be sent over from the Gas Works. The quantity of coal in stock at the Gas Works is 1.100 tnns.or thirty days ordinary supply, but if the works in the town should close' this quantity will last out for a much longer period. The power of the gas during the month had averaged 22.28 candles.—Alderman Tinimis moved the adoption of the minutes, and said he wishe4 to express the appreciation of the members of the committee,of the highly eulogistic manner in which the members of the Council had been pleased te refer to the condition and efficiency of the un- dertakings, which reflected a great deal of credit upon the engineer. As to the coal strike, the committee had purchased a further 100 tons for Netherley, and there was now a month's supply there.—Mr. Davies seconded the motion.</p>
        <p>TEE PLITmEERS AT TRE GAS vroaxs. Mr. Hodgkin so n said at a recent meeting, a member of the Council had stated that the reason the men at the Gas Works had not asked for an advance was that they were neither one thing nor another,and that the engineer had acknowledged himself that the men were merely labourers, and that being the case he failed to see why they shottld be employed as plumbers and gas fitters. In the interest of the men it ought to be made known Whether this was so or not.—AldermanTimmis said the matter had been before the Committee and the Engineer had presented a report, in which he said Mr. Gough had wrongly quoted him. He had never admitted that men were employed as plumbers, who came to the works as labourers. None but properly qualified men, who had served an apprenticeship, were permitted to do work for the Corporation. What the Engineer had said was that they had labourers whohad taken up pipe fitting, which was practiced,not only in Widnes but throughout the country. The plumbers themselves had complained that the statement was prejudical to their interests, as it might he used against them in seeking employment ekewhere.—Councillor Gough said he should not ask Mr. Carr to substantiate anything he had said; he had had enough of that experience already ; but he would leave it to the honour of the gentlemen who were present at the meeting to testify to what Mr. Carr said on that day. If they did not do so, then things must remain as they were.—The Mayor: I don't see that there is any necessity to answer this question.—The minutes were adopted. IMPROVED RAILWAt TRAIN SERVItE.--ITIE MAYOR'S GIFT. The General Purposes Committee contained the fol- lowing :—The Chairman (Alderman Wareing) reported that be and the Town Clerk bad again waited upon Mr. Shaw, distiiet superintendent, London and North- Western Railway Co.. and urged the necessity of an im- provement in the train service especially on the Widnes and St. Helens line, and the provision of an additional waiting room at Appleton, and that Mr. Shaw had pro- mised to lay their representations before his directors. The Committee had. also requested Mr. William Pol. litt, general manager, M.S. and L. Railway, to receive a deputation on the subject. —The Committee had I also resolved that the resolution thanking the Mayor</p>
        <p>----- Mr. Ruskin' countless admirers will be reassured to learn on authority that he has uow almost completely recovered his health, and is able to take two walks each day, and that he spends his evenings entertaining his friends and visitors at Brentwood, and in playing games of chess. He has, however, absolutely abandoned all literary work and is taking a complete rest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>4 A JUDGE'S SURPRISE.</head>
        <p>4 A JUDGE'S SURPRISE.</p>
        <p>During the hearing of a libel action brought by the Trocadero Club against the Liverpool " Porcupine," at Assizes, yesterday, a witness said the local magistrates recently extracted from license holders a promise not to supply drink to women of ill-fame. —Justice Kennedy (with surprise) : Dave they ? Counsel : Yes.—Justice Kennedy : Under what statute ? Counsel : Under none; under a self-made law.—The matter then dropped, and the trial proceeded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>BELFAST RIOTS OF 1886.</head>
        <p>THE BELFAST RIOTS OF 1886.</p>
        <p>Joseph Walker, who was sentenced to 20 year's penal servitude for the manslaughter of Private Hughes, West Surrey Regiment, during the 1886 riots in Belfast, and on whose behalf a memorial was recently presented to the Lord-Lieutenant, arrived in Belfast yesterday morn- ing having been released from Mountjoy Prison, Dublin, on Thursday night after serving seven years of his sen- tence. Walker shot a soldier and a police head-constable, who had refused to allow him to go inn certain direction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>SERIOUS RIOTS IN BOMBAY.</head>
        <p>SERIOUS RIOTS IN BOMBAY.</p>
        <p>TROOPS CALLED OUT,</p>
        <p>AN ALARMING SITUATION.</p>
        <p>liomnav, FRIDAY.—In consequence of the ill-feeling engendered by the recent fatal riots between Hineloos and Mahomedans in the Junaghar districts, serious con- flicts occurred to-day between hostile religious parties in several parts of this city, and particularly near the Jumma-Musjeed, the principal mosque. Many were injured on both sides, and had to be taken to the hos- pital. The riots hay° assumed serious proportions, and are spreading in various directions over the city. Traffic Is stopped, and troops—European and native—have been called out to restore order. The police worn out and undermanned', are incapable of dealing with the mass of rioters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>HALIFAX ELECTION PE TITIOW.</head>
        <p>HALIFAX ELECTION PE TITIOW.</p>
        <p>The Halifax election petition came on for trial yester- day, in the Queen's Bench, before Justices Hawkins and Cave. Mr. Arnold, the unsuccessful candidate, being the petitioner, and Mr. Rawson Shaw, sitting member, the dofe,ndant. Mr. Arnold stated in evidence that he attended the counting of votes, and there appeared to be great confusion. Several persons not sworn to secrecy assisted in the counting, and his agent com- plained to the Mayor about it, but nothing came of the objection.—Mr. Shaw said he watched the counting and saw no irregularity.—Mr. Justice Hawkins said there was not a particle of evidence to show the election was void, and he had no alternative but to dismiss the peti- tion. Justice Cave concurred. The court also con- demned the petitioner in costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SEQUEL TO THE ESSEX MURDER TRIAL.</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO THE ESSEX MURDER TRIAL.</p>
        <p>The police yesterday arrested the man Japes Ramsey, on a charge Of stealing the corn which was being car- ried away from Hazeleigh Hall at the time when Ser- geant Eves was murdered, an for whose murder the brothers' Richard and John Davies, now lie under sen- tence of death. Ramsey, it will be remembered, was tried on the capital charge together with the two con- demned men, at Chelmsford, but was acquitted. Ramsey was brought before the magistrates later in the day at Moat Hall, Maldon, and remanded fora week. He stated that he was innocent, but that he heard Richard Davies say to John at the assizes "Hold tight, Charley has got off, and he will take care of the old people," meaning the condemned men's parents.. Since their conviction the brothers Davies have made confessions, in which they declare Ramsey was the man who actually cut the policeman's throat, and John added that he held the officer down while Ramsey accomplished this. Great excitement ha.. been caused locally by the new turn events have taken. It is conjectured that Richard Davies will be reprieved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>CHOLERA,</head>
        <p>THE CHOLERA,</p>
        <p>Sr. PETERSZURG, FRIDAY.—TiIe cholera has now spread over 16 anvernmPnts. An alarminif increase of the epidemic is reported from the governments of Orel and Kherson. According to the official returns just published here, the number of cases in the capital during the last year was 4,269, of which 1,363 termin- ated fatally.</p>
        <p>A correspondent who has by residence in the East experienced cholera is of opinion that if the plague is to visit us this year it will not be long before it makes its unwelcome appearance on our shores. All the condi- tions favourable to the advent of the disease have pre- vailed in the close, enervating atmosphere which has pervaded the greater portion of the, country during the past week. Bright sunshine with a dry air, however hot it may be, can be regarded without the slightest misgiving ; but when added to a moist air there is intense heat, such as that which was felt yesterday and the day before, it is time to take precautions in the matter of diet and clothing. :Nothing, my correspond- ent ades, will render one more-susceptible to choleric attack than neglect in one attire, and what is known in the East ass ctrelera belt—viz., a simple piece of flannel —will do more to ward off an attack than all the nostrums of those empirics who are already advertising their wares. Strong black coffee, taken in conjunction with a cigarette or pipe, in the early morning, is regard- ed by medical men in India as tho safest preventive against, the scourge, and as they invariably take their own physic in this respect at least one is inclined to think that so simple and inexpensive a prophylactic must have considerable virtue in it.</p>
        <p>AT OUR LOCAL CHURCHES,</p>
        <p>WA R RINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE. The P.S.A. meeting in Wycliffe Church on Sundar afternoon was largely attended. Mr. T. Malkin occuP" the chair and delivered a brief address. Mr. Janie° Evans of Leicester-street was the speaker and he de- livered an excellent address, basing his remarks on the words "Thy kingdom come." An organ solo was we? rendered by the organist Mr. W. U. Payton. Messrs. J• Ball, A. Birch and R. Atkinson also took part in the meeting.</p>
        <p>FIRST TIME IN WARRINGTON. The Rev. James Hannah, who is an old Warrington Grammar School boy, and a son of the late Mr. Hattn&amp;n'' Preached for tlie first time in Warfington at Holy Triniq Church on Sunday evening. Mr. Hannah, who has &amp; good voice, preached extempore, and the discourse 9/4 not only an interesting one, but was deliverA vitt much tiarnestness, and was appreciated by the lark, Congregation.</p>
        <p>PADGATS CHAPEL ANNIVERSARY. The pulpit at the Padgitie. Wesleyan Chapel on day, the occasion of the chapel anniversary, wss 1 cupied at both services by the Rev. G. E. Cutting, 11°, • j of Manchester, but formerly of the Warrington (-limn. In the evening the rev, gentleman said that since ha ministry amongst them six years ago, lie had het&amp; much of their success at Padgate. It could not he of the people of Padgate " Ye live in seemly houses, ti 4 allow my house to lie in waste." fie could not fancy chapel previous to the extension. The architectural an: symmetrical features were now complete, and it was; pleasure to preach in the chapel.—Miss Bennett ( i•Sped hurst) was at the organ. The choir had been ainiqm'nt•ts for the occasion, and in the evening an old favour' anthem at Padgate, entitled " God be with you till meet again," was rendered. The collections aounteo to £l4. m vrt</p>
        <p>JUBILEE CELEBRATION AT RIX lON 14'ESLFY0/</p>
        <p>CHAPEL. The Rixton Wesleyans celebrated the jubilee of [y, chapel on Sunday and Monday, the chapel being built q*: 1843. The building had been beautified and repaireW for the occasion. On Sunday the preacher in the after', noon and evening was the Rev. Buckley Yatesjormerlls minister in the circuit. The evening service was sc. attended notwithstanding the rain. During the servicAP the choir gave an anthem "1 know that ray liediwnier:, liveth," the duet being taken by Miss F. Clarke and 1 Thos. Thomason. The collections for the day a monte". to RS. On Monday the members of the conr,reg At ion 3°l " children marched in procession round Rixton and t A neighbourhood. 'A large party came from Ward ilgtol7 also from Ham and Cadishead. After the proce,,i.n" 1 party was held in the chapel, after which a well A: teo,l ~„z meeting was held, Mr. James Summersgill of Warrinetn taking the chair. Addresses were delivered by Rev..P." Yates, Messrs. A. florrecks, Elijah Ford, and (Were' of Warrington, Mr. J. Torige of lti stem, and Ir. Blythe and S. Wright two old Sunday school r•hol: During the afturnoon the children indulged in g4ie, a held kindly lent by Mr. Mason. •The total prott'Os ° the jubilee amounted to £l7.</p>
        <p>FLOWER SERVICE. In connection with the Padgate Parish Church Su; day School, the annual flower service WAS held in Cho Church, on Sunday afternoon, and was very well tended. The scholars walked in procession lip the c.b; aisle of the church, and occupied seats set. apArt '°„A them. During the singing cf a hymn the children in single file past tpe choir stalls up to the rail where the flowers were taken by the Rev. Langliam (vicar), and Mr. Thomas Antrobus, and Pl3e t on and around the altar. The vicar delivered instructive and interesting address. Tiiere was collection of fruit and flowers, which were on Id'u'dtre, presented to the Warrington Infirmary for the use in t , sick wards.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL SERMONS. The annual Sunday school sermons at Orford Choi:, were preached on Sunday, the pulpit being occupied " the Rev. S. 11. Haslam, M.A., headmaster of Clithe. Royal Grammar School. The anthem at the morn, service was " Come unto me, all ye that labour (Cale 3 Simper), Mr. William Richardson rendering the dillic`?4, tenor solo with great credit. Li the afternoon a eV° flower service was held, conducted by the Rev. Phillips, (chaplain). and the Rev. S. 11. Haslarnt 1014, This is the first service of the kind held at Orford, al, every effort was made to maite it a success. The dren formed in procession, carrying the flowers clergy in the communion. Mr. John Cann was at the, organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-12_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>HOUSE OF COMMONS..-FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>THE Loss OF TEE VICTORIA.—Sir U. KAY•SMUTTLE• wownr, replying to Mr. Banbury, said it was not thought necessary to take any special steps for the re- placonent of the Victoria, otherwise than by pressing onward the battleships already being built. The Med- iterranean and other squadrons were at their full complement, and the British navy was not at such a narrow margin as to render special provision for re- placement.necessary. TAB NAVY ESTIMATES.—Sir U: KAY-SHUTTLEWORTH answering Lord George flatnilton,said the consideration of the navy estimates would be resumed as soon as the Home Bill was disposed of.— Answering Mr. TOMLINSON. the hon. baronet said the money granted for the years' naval expenditure £5,240,000 would be sufficient to carry the Government beyond the tirne,when they hoped to proceed with the Navy Estimates. THE ARMENIAN PRISONERS.—Mr. S. SMITH asked the Under Secretary for Foreign Affairs if he was aware that the remaining 58 Armenian prisoners at Angora were accused in the same way as Professors Thoumaian and Kaya,ian by means of forged documents and false „witnesses, and that their fate will be terrible unless in- Bence is brought to bear on the Porte, and what steps htir Majesty's Government are taking to prevent the frightful abuse of justice.—Sir E. GREY said the Turk- ish Government had promised to supply the process verbal to her Majesty's Embassy, and until that was received he could express no opinion in the matter. Tux Bolen RULE DEBATE,—The discussion on the re- port stage of the Home Rule Bill was resumed by Mr. COURTNEY, who continued his argument in favour of the new clause proposed by him providing that any mem- ber of the Executive Committee might attend and speak at any sitting of either House of the Irish Legislature but without power to vote unless dely elected.—Mr. GLADSTONE said this was a matter that was well within the province of the Irish Legislature, to which it ought to be kept.—After further discussion the clause was negatived.—The next clause on the paper relating to Boundary Commissioners stood in the name of Lord Walmer.—Mr. GLADSTONE rose at the invitation of the Speaker, and with the indulgence of the House, to state the views and intention of the GSvernment. Ile said the Government could not accept the clause enacting the appointment or Boundary Commissioners. When he expressed his contingent willingness to entertain the question of single members' constituencies 11, referred to a method of proceeding by Order or Council, subjese to the power of Parliatneutary intervention.</p>
