        <p>51r. Cooper responded suitably, and gave the health of ;he home team, which was accepted with musical hon- airs.—During the evening the following songs Sze., were Oven: song, " The old musketeer," Mr. P. Hastie ; song, " When I was single," Mr. royner ; song, " Take ,ne home Kathleen," Mr. Sherlock ; song, " The soldier's ,srave," Mr. Stanton; song. "I'm another," Mr. J. Huddleston ; recitation, "The dying soldier," Mr. W. 'Sutton ; song, "The warrior bold," Mr. J. Barlow, song, "The Stowaway," Mr. Sherlock; recitation, "The dream of Eugene Aram," Mr. Sutton. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT BOWLING LEAGUE. CEMETERY V. BOLLS HEAD—Played on Saturday. When the aggregate came to be totalled up it was found that the Bull's Head had recorded another brilliant victory by nine points, and although the majority was hardly so large as on the first meeting-25 points—the winners are to be complimented en accomplishing a feat which had hitherto been beyond the range of any team in the leagde. The green was in capital condition, and he arrangements were of such a nature as to reflect credit on the establishment. Mr. W. M'lntyre, vice-president, officiated as referee. Score : BULL'S READ. CEMETERY. It Anderton D Wilcox B Hall l4 J Ashton l5 j Shaw l5 R T.. lor 9 W Bolton l3 T Bolton 5 T Locker l3 R Sewell D Daniels R Shaw T Critchley T Hackett Fairbrother F Berry 162 153 A CLERGYMAN'S SUICIDE. __— PATHETIC CASE. ' The Liverpool Coroner held an inquest on Thursday on the body of Jefferson Lowndes, aged 35. described as a private secretary, who lived with his mother at 152, Holland-road, Kensington' London. The deceased was discovered lying dead in his bedroom at noon, having apparently shot himself with a pistol which was found in his hand.—Mr. Elizabeth Dorothy Lowndes stated that she was the widow of a newspaper editor. The de- ceased was her son. He was blind in one eye and the other was veay much affected, and an oculist had told him that when he was fortp years of age he would be stone blind. During the past few weeks he had been greatly depressed. He was an M.A. of Oxford, and was a fully ordained clergyman, although he had not offi- ciated for some years. His wife died in the West Indies twelve months after marriage, and this had also greatly affected him. Witness last saw him alive on the 4th inst., when be left London, and on the Bth she received a letter from him enclosing his will and a few bills which he asked her to pay. He said in the letter that he was going to Hustralia,ond that she would hear from him on landing. That same evening she received a telegram from the police in Liverpool stating that her son had committed suicide. Two letters found on the dressing table in the deceased's bedroom were read. The first was addressed to the manager of the hotel, and was as follows :—" August 8, 1893.—Dear sir,— On retiring to bed to-night at about twelve I found I was blind. I tried by means of matches, but could not see the light of them, though they burnt my fingers. I had intended to emigrate, sailing with a friend of mine this week ; but what use is a blind man in the colonies? If my sight is not all right in the morning I shall shoot myself. Communicate with my friends, 152, Holland-road, London, W. My name is J. Lowndes. I have enough to pay my bill. If there is any balance over send it to Mrs. Lowndes, 152, Holland-toad, London." The other letter was a follows :—" My dearest mother,—l have nearly gone blind. I cannot be a burden to you and family. I had intended to emigrate, but a blind man can do no good in the:colonies. 'rhere is nothing to be done ; but God bless you and forgive me.—Your loving son, J.L." The writing in both letters are very irregular, thus bearing out the Statement of the deceased as to hit blindness.— The jury returned a verdict of suicide whilst labouring under temporary insanity. THE SAILORS' UNION. ACTION FOR LIBEL. The trial of the action for libel brought by Messrs. Allan Brothers, of Liverpool and Glasgow, against the Sailors and Firemen's Union, the General Secretary of Union, Mr. J. H.Wilson, M.P.,and the general treasurer, Mr. John Smith, was concluded at Liverpool, on Wed- nesday. In consequence of a strike in the early part of this year, the plaintiffs asked the mes whom they em- ployed on their Atlantic boats to sign an agreement binding themselves for 12 months. Men who broke their , agreement had to forfeit £5. The Glasgow officials of the Sailors' Union tried to induce Messrs. Allan's mans- ger to withdraw the proposal, but- he declined to to so. Placards and pamphlets were then issued by the branch, in which Messrs. Allan were charged with cruel and tyrannous conduct towards their men, and an allegation was also made that the lives of the paseseng,ers by the Allan line steamers were endangered owing to the en- gagement by the firm of " cornermen, boot closers," Sze., to take the places of the qualified seamen who had re- fused to sign the agreements. The actions against the Union and the general treasurer were dismissed, hut a verdict of £2OO and costs was given against Mr. Wilson, M.P. Stay of execution was granted, with a view to an appeal.,</p>
